const ORIG_QUESTIONS = [{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which type of acute kidney injury is caused by decreased blood flow to the kidneys?","opts":["Prerenal AKI","Intrarenal AKI","Postrenal AKI","Chronic AKI"],"ans":0,"expl":"Prerenal AKI results from hypoperfusion of the kidneys due to conditions like hypotension, heart failure, or volume depletion.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the hallmark finding in acute tubular necrosis (ATN)?","opts":["Marked proteinuria in the urine","Muddy brown casts in urine","Gross hematuria in the urine","Glucosuria on urinalysis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Muddy brown granular casts are characteristic of ATN, indicating tubular epithelial cell damage and sloughing.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which stage of CKD corresponds to a GFR of 15-29 mL/min/1.73m²?","opts":["Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4","Stage 5"],"ans":2,"expl":"CKD Stage 4 is defined by GFR 15-29 mL/min/1.73m², indicating severe kidney damage and preparation for renal replacement therapy.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What electrolyte abnormality is most commonly associated with advanced CKD?","opts":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypophosphatemia"],"ans":0,"expl":"Hyperkalemia occurs in advanced CKD due to decreased potassium excretion by damaged nephrons.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which condition is characterized by heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia, and edema?","opts":["Nephritic syndrome","Nephrotic syndrome","Acute glomerulonephritis","Pyelonephritis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Nephrotic syndrome presents with massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia due to glomerular permeability changes.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the primary cause of postrenal AKI?","opts":["Glomerulonephritis","Urinary tract obstruction","Acute tubular necrosis","Renal artery stenosis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Postrenal AKI results from obstruction of urine flow, such as from kidney stones, tumors, or prostate enlargement.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which medication class should be used cautiously in patients with CKD due to nephrotoxicity risk?","opts":["ACE inhibitors","NSAIDs","Beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers"],"ans":1,"expl":"NSAIDs can cause vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles and worsen kidney function, especially in CKD patients.","obj":5},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the most common cause of glomerulonephritis worldwide?","opts":["Diabetic nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Lupus nephritis","Post-streptococcal GN"],"ans":1,"expl":"IgA nephropathy (Berger disease) is the most common primary glomerulonephritis globally.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which finding distinguishes nephritic from nephrotic syndrome?","opts":["Marked proteinuria only","Hematuria with RBC casts","Generalized edema of the legs","Severe hypoalbuminemia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Nephritic syndrome is characterized by hematuria with RBC casts, hypertension, and mild-to-moderate proteinuria.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What systolic blood pressure target does KDIGO recommend for most patients with CKD and high blood pressure?","opts":["<150 mmHg","<140 mmHg","<130 mmHg","<120 mmHg"],"ans":3,"expl":"KDIGO 2021 (Blood Pressure in CKD) recommends treating CKD patients with high blood pressure to a target systolic BP <120 mmHg using standardized office BP measurement, a change from the earlier <130/80 target; KDIGO 2024 retains this for non-dialysis CKD. Individualize for frailty, orthostatic hypotension, and limited life expectancy.","obj":2},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which hormone deficiency contributes to anemia in CKD?","opts":["Insulin","Erythropoietin","Aldosterone","Parathyroid hormone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Damaged kidneys produce less erythropoietin, leading to decreased red blood cell production and anemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the primary treatment for hyperkalemia in a patient with peaked T waves on ECG?","opts":["Oral potassium binders","IV calcium gluconate","Dietary restriction","Loop diuretics"],"ans":1,"expl":"IV calcium gluconate stabilizes cardiac membranes immediately in severe hyperkalemia with ECG changes.","obj":2},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which type of dialysis uses the peritoneal membrane as a filter?","opts":["Hemodialysis","Peritoneal dialysis","Continuous venovenous hemofiltration","Plasmapheresis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Peritoneal dialysis uses the peritoneal membrane for solute and fluid exchange via instilled dialysate.","obj":3},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the most accurate indicator of kidney function?","opts":["Serum creatinine level alone","Blood urea nitrogen level alone","Glomerular filtration rate (GFR)","Daily urine output volume"],"ans":2,"expl":"GFR is the best overall measure of kidney function, estimating filtration capacity per minute.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which condition is a common cause of prerenal AKI in hospitalized patients?","opts":["Sepsis","Glomerulonephritis","Kidney stones","Polycystic kidney disease"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sepsis causes vasodilation and hypotension, leading to renal hypoperfusion and prerenal AKI.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What dietary modification is typically recommended for CKD patients with hyperphosphatemia?","opts":["High protein diet","Phosphate restriction","High potassium diet","Low calcium intake"],"ans":1,"expl":"Phosphate restriction and phosphate binders help control hyperphosphatemia in CKD.","obj":2},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which finding suggests acute glomerulonephritis?","opts":["Muddy brown granular casts","RBC casts and dysmorphic RBCs","White blood cell casts","Hyaline and granular casts"],"ans":1,"expl":"RBC casts and dysmorphic red blood cells indicate glomerular bleeding, characteristic of glomerulonephritis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the definition of oliguria in adults?","opts":["<500 mL/day","<400 mL/day","<300 mL/day","<100 mL/day"],"ans":1,"expl":"Oliguria is defined as urine output less than 400 mL per day in adults.","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which complication is associated with rapid correction of hyponatremia in CKD patients?","opts":["Acute cerebral edema and seizures","Osmotic demyelination syndrome","Sudden cardiac arrest and arrhythmias","Recurrent seizures and coma"],"ans":1,"expl":"Rapid sodium correction can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis).","obj":1},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the primary mechanism of metabolic acidosis in CKD?","opts":["Increased acid production","Decreased acid excretion","Bicarbonate overproduction","Respiratory compensation"],"ans":1,"expl":"CKD impairs the kidney's ability to excrete hydrogen ions and regenerate bicarbonate, causing metabolic acidosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which solutes move across a dialyzer membrane during hemodialysis?","opts":["Ultrafiltration","Diffusion","Convection","Osmosis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Diffusion is the primary mechanism of solute clearance in hemodialysis, where solutes move from an area of higher concentration (blood) to lower concentration (dialysate) across a semipermeable membrane.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which vascular access type has the lowest risk of infection and longest patency for chronic hemodialysis?","opts":["Tunneled dialysis catheter","Arteriovenous graft","Arteriovenous fistula","Peripheral IV line"],"ans":2,"expl":"Arteriovenous fistulas (AVF) have the lowest infection rate, best long-term patency, and are considered the gold standard for chronic hemodialysis access.","obj":6},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What does Kt/V measure in hemodialysis?","opts":["Blood flow rate","Dialysis adequacy","Membrane permeability","Ultrafiltration volume"],"ans":1,"expl":"Kt/V is a dimensionless number used to quantify hemodialysis adequacy, where K is dialyzer clearance, t is treatment time, and V is volume of distribution of urea.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"During hemodialysis, ultrafiltration primarily removes which of the following?","opts":["Large proteins","Excess fluid","Red blood cells","Glucose"],"ans":1,"expl":"Ultrafiltration is the process of removing excess fluid from the blood by applying hydrostatic pressure across the dialyzer membrane, forcing water and small solutes through.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which anticoagulant is most commonly used during routine hemodialysis treatments?","opts":["Warfarin","Heparin","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"ans":1,"expl":"Unfractionated heparin is the most commonly used anticoagulant during hemodialysis to prevent clotting in the extracorporeal circuit.","obj":5},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the most common intradialytic complication during hemodialysis?","opts":["Air embolism","Hypotension","Seizures","Hemolysis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Intradialytic hypotension is the most frequent complication, occurring in 20-30% of treatments, often due to rapid fluid removal or autonomic dysfunction.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which dialysate component is typically prescribed at about 137-140 mEq/L to remain isonatremic?","opts":["Potassium","Calcium","Sodium","Bicarbonate"],"ans":2,"expl":"Dialysate sodium is usually prescribed near the patient's serum sodium, about 137-140 mEq/L, to avoid osmotic shifts. Values at the 144-145 mEq/L end of the usable range are at the extreme and are avoided in contemporary practice.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is convection in the context of hemodialysis?","opts":["Solute movement due to concentration gradient","Solute movement with water flow across membrane","Movement of water only","Movement of proteins"],"ans":1,"expl":"Convection is the movement of solutes across the membrane along with water flow (solvent drag), particularly important for middle molecule clearance.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which vascular access requires the longest maturation time before use?","opts":["Tunneled catheter","Arteriovenous graft","Arteriovenous fistula","Non-tunneled catheter"],"ans":2,"expl":"Arteriovenous fistulas typically require 3-6 months to mature before they can be safely used for dialysis, longer than grafts or catheters.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the recommended minimum Kt/V value for adequate hemodialysis?","opts":["0.8","1.2","2.0","3.5"],"ans":1,"expl":"KDOQI guidelines recommend a minimum single-pool Kt/V of 1.2 per dialysis session to ensure adequate dialysis delivery.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which symptom is most characteristic of dialysis disequilibrium syndrome?","opts":["Severe hypertension and tachycardia during the treatment","Neurological symptoms including headache and confusion","Severe muscle cramps in the legs and abdomen","High fever and chills during the dialysis session"],"ans":1,"expl":"Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome presents with neurological symptoms (headache, nausea, confusion, seizures) due to rapid solute removal causing cerebral edema.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the primary cause of muscle cramps during hemodialysis?","opts":["Severe hyperkalemia during the treatment","Rapid ultrafiltration and volume depletion","Abnormally high dialysate calcium concentration","Excessive anticoagulation during the treatment"],"ans":1,"expl":"Muscle cramps during dialysis are commonly caused by rapid fluid removal leading to volume depletion and changes in muscle perfusion.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which air embolism symptom would appear FIRST if air enters the venous line?","opts":["Chest pain and dyspnea","Sudden bradycardia","Severe hypertension","Sudden high fever"],"ans":0,"expl":"Air embolism typically presents first with sudden chest pain, dyspnea, and cough as air reaches the pulmonary circulation.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the function of the dialyzer membrane's pore size?","opts":["Determines the blood flow rate of the pump","Controls which molecules can pass through","Sets the dialysate temperature of the bath","Regulates the anticoagulation dose given"],"ans":1,"expl":"Membrane pore size determines the molecular weight cutoff, controlling which solutes can be cleared while retaining larger proteins like albumin.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which dialysate temperature is typically used to reduce intradialytic hypotension?","opts":["Warmer dialysate, about 39°C","35-36°C (cool dialysate)","Hotter dialysate, about 42°C","Room temperature, about 22°C"],"ans":1,"expl":"Cool dialysate (35-36°C) promotes peripheral vasoconstriction and improves hemodynamic stability, reducing hypotension episodes.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the primary advantage of high-flux dialyzers?","opts":["Lower cost","Better clearance of middle molecules","Reduced anticoagulation needs","Shorter treatment time"],"ans":1,"expl":"High-flux dialyzers have larger pores that allow better clearance of middle molecular weight toxins like beta-2 microglobulin.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which access complication is characterized by a thrill that is weak or absent?","opts":["Infection","Thrombosis","Aneurysm formation","Steal syndrome"],"ans":1,"expl":"A weak or absent thrill suggests thrombosis or stenosis of the vascular access, requiring immediate evaluation.","obj":1},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the purpose of bicarbonate in the dialysate?","opts":["To lower blood pressure","To correct metabolic acidosis","To prevent clotting","To reduce potassium"],"ans":1,"expl":"Bicarbonate in dialysate helps correct the metabolic acidosis common in ESRD patients by diffusing into the blood and buffering acids.","obj":3},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which patient position is recommended if an air embolism is suspected?","opts":["Supine, lying flat on the back","Left lateral decubitus with head down","Sitting fully upright in the chair","Prone, lying flat face down"],"ans":1,"expl":"Left lateral decubitus (Durant's maneuver) with head down traps air in the right ventricle, preventing it from entering pulmonary circulation.","obj":2},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the typical blood flow rate range for maintenance hemodialysis?","opts":["50-100 mL/min","200-500 mL/min","600-800 mL/min","1000-1200 mL/min"],"ans":1,"expl":"Standard blood flow rates for hemodialysis range from 200-500 mL/min, with 300-400 mL/min being most common for adequate clearance.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which peritoneal dialysis catheter type is placed using a laparoscopic approach and has a pre-sternal exit site?","opts":["Tenckhoff catheter","Presternal catheter","Moncrief-Popovich catheter","Fluoroscopically placed catheter"],"ans":1,"expl":"Presternal catheters are surgically placed with the exit site on the chest wall, beneficial for patients with abdominal ostomies or obesity.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the recommended frequency for exit-site care in stable peritoneal dialysis patients?","opts":["Daily cleaning and dressing of the exit site","At least twice weekly, and after showering or vigorous exercise","After every single exchange, without exception","Only when drainage is noted at the exit site"],"ans":1,"expl":"ISPD 2023 recommends cleansing a healed PD exit site at least twice weekly and after every shower or vigorous exercise, using plain liquid soap or an antimicrobial cleanser, then drying. Frequency increases with drainage, crusting, or infection. Avoid hydrogen peroxide and povidone-iodine on a healed site because they impair healing.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which organism is the most common cause of peritoneal dialysis-associated peritonitis?","opts":["Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Candida albicans"],"ans":1,"expl":"Coagulase-negative staphylococci, particularly S. epidermidis, are the most frequent pathogens in PD peritonitis due to skin flora contamination.","obj":6},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What does a high transporter status on the Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET) indicate?","opts":["Slow solute clearance that requires much longer dwell times","Rapid solute equilibration with poor ultrafiltration on long dwells","Normal membrane function with no clinical significance","Membrane failure that requires immediate transplant referral"],"ans":1,"expl":"High transporters rapidly equilibrate solutes but lose osmotic gradient quickly, leading to poor UF on long dwells; they benefit from shorter cycles.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which dialysate solution is preferred for patients with hyperkalemia?","opts":["Standard lactate-buffered solution","Potassium-free solution","Bicarbonate-buffered solution","Icodextrin solution"],"ans":1,"expl":"Potassium-free dialysate helps correct hyperkalemia by promoting potassium removal across the peritoneal membrane.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the primary mechanism of ultrafiltration in peritoneal dialysis?","opts":["Diffusion","Osmosis","Convection","Active transport"],"ans":1,"expl":"Ultrafiltration occurs primarily through osmosis, driven by the osmotic gradient created by glucose or icodextrin in the dialysate.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"CAPD stands for:","opts":["Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis","Cyclic Automated Peritoneal Dialysis","Chronic Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis","Continuous Automated Peritoneal Dialysis"],"ans":0,"expl":"CAPD is Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis, performed manually throughout the day without a machine.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which symptom is most indicative of peritonitis in a PD patient?","opts":["Mild abdominal discomfort","Cloudy effluent with abdominal pain","Increased ultrafiltration volume","Decreased appetite alone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Cloudy effluent combined with abdominal pain is the hallmark presentation of peritonitis and requires immediate evaluation.","obj":6},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the first-line empiric antibiotic coverage for PD peritonitis?","opts":["Gram-positive coverage only at first","Gram-negative coverage only at first","Both Gram-positive and Gram-negative","Antifungal coverage only at first"],"ans":2,"expl":"Empiric therapy must cover both Gram-positive (e.g., vancomycin or cephalosporin) and Gram-negative organisms (e.g., ceftazidime or aminoglycoside).","obj":5},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Ultrafiltration failure is defined as:","opts":["Inability to achieve target solute clearance","Persistent inability to remove adequate fluid volume","Complete loss of peritoneal membrane function","Catheter mechanical failure"],"ans":1,"expl":"UF failure is the inability to remove sufficient fluid despite appropriate osmotic agents, often due to membrane changes or high transporter status.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which nutrient requires careful monitoring in PD patients due to glucose absorption from dialysate?","opts":["Total protein intake","Serum potassium level","Calories/carbohydrates","Serum vitamin D level"],"ans":2,"expl":"Significant glucose absorption from dialysate can contribute 100-300+ calories daily, affecting glycemic control and weight.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the recommended protein intake for stable PD patients?","opts":["0.6-0.8 g/kg/day","1.0-1.2 g/kg/day","1.5-2.0 g/kg/day","No restriction needed"],"ans":1,"expl":"PD patients need 1.0-1.2 g/kg/day due to protein losses in dialysate (5-15g/day) and catabolic state of uremia.","obj":2},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which PD modality uses a cycler machine primarily during nighttime sleep?","opts":["CAPD","CCPD","Tidal PD","Intermittent PD"],"ans":1,"expl":"CCPD (Continuous Cyclic Peritoneal Dialysis) uses a cycler at night with a daytime dwell, offering more freedom during waking hours.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the purpose of the Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET)?","opts":["To measure the patient's residual kidney function","To classify peritoneal membrane transport characteristics","To diagnose and manage acute peritoneal infection","To assess the patency of the peritoneal catheter"],"ans":1,"expl":"PET classifies patients as high, high-average, low-average, or low transporters to guide prescription optimization.","obj":6},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which catheter complication presents as leakage of dialysate around the exit site?","opts":["Catheter migration","Exit-site infection","Pericatheter leak","Omental wrapping"],"ans":2,"expl":"Pericatheter leaks occur early post-placement or from increased intra-abdominal pressure, causing fluid to track around the catheter.","obj":6},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Icodextrin solution is particularly useful for:","opts":["Patients with peritonitis","Long daytime dwells in high transporters","Emergency hyperkalemia treatment","Patients with fungal infections"],"ans":1,"expl":"Icodextrin provides sustained osmotic gradient for long dwells, ideal for high transporters who lose glucose-based UF quickly.","obj":6},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the minimum dwell time typically recommended for adequate exchange in CAPD?","opts":["15 minutes","30 minutes","2-4 hours","8 hours"],"ans":2,"expl":"Standard CAPD exchanges use 2-4 hour dwells to balance solute clearance and ultrafiltration efficiency.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which finding suggests catheter tip migration on imaging?","opts":["Catheter tip resting normally in the pelvis","Catheter tip in the upper abdomen or subphrenic space","The catheter cuff clearly visible at the exit site","A completely normal catheter course on imaging"],"ans":1,"expl":"Migration to the upper abdomen can cause inflow pain and drainage problems; the tip should rest in the pelvis.","obj":3},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Encapsulating Peritoneal Sclerosis (EPS) is characterized by:","opts":["A reversible inflammation of the membrane","Fibrotic encapsulation of bowel with obstruction","Temporary ultrafiltration failure that resolves","A mild infection at the catheter exit site"],"ans":1,"expl":"EPS is a rare but serious complication with thick fibrous membrane formation around intestines, causing obstruction and requiring surgical intervention.","obj":6},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the role of heparin in PD dialysate?","opts":["Anticoagulation for systemic effect","Prevention of fibrin clot formation in catheter","Treatment of peritonitis","Enhancement of ultrafiltration"],"ans":1,"expl":"Heparin (500-1000 units/L) prevents fibrin formation in the catheter or effluent without causing systemic anticoagulation.","obj":6},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Compared with deceased-donor kidneys, living-donor kidneys generally provide which advantage?","opts":["Better long-term graft survival, largely from shorter cold ischemia time","No difference at all in long-term graft or patient outcomes","Higher rates of delayed graft function and rejection episodes","Shorter overall graft survival than deceased-donor kidneys"],"ans":0,"expl":"Living-donor kidneys generally provide better long-term graft survival than deceased-donor kidneys, driven mainly by shorter cold ischemia time and the ability to transplant electively before dialysis is needed. Living RELATED donors are not inherently superior to living UNRELATED donors: outcomes are similar, and modern matching and immunosuppression have narrowed any historical difference.","obj":3},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the primary mechanism of hyperacute rejection?","opts":["T-cell mediated cytotoxicity","Pre-formed antibodies against donor HLA antigens","Chronic antibody-mediated injury","Drug toxicity from calcineurin inhibitors"],"ans":1,"expl":"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours due to pre-existing antibodies in the recipient that react against donor antigens, causing immediate thrombosis and graft necrosis. This is why crossmatch testing is critical before transplantation.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which immunosuppressive medication is most commonly associated with nephrotoxicity?","opts":["Mycophenolate mofetil","Prednisone","Tacrolimus","Sirolimus"],"ans":2,"expl":"Tacrolimus and other calcineurin inhibitors cause dose-dependent nephrotoxicity through vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles. Therapeutic drug monitoring is essential to balance rejection prevention with kidney protection.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the typical time frame for acute cellular rejection to occur post-transplant?","opts":["Within the first 24 hours of transplant","Days to weeks, most common in first 3-6 months","Only after 5 years have passed post-transplant","Only during pregnancy or after childbirth"],"ans":1,"expl":"Acute cellular rejection most commonly occurs within the first 3-6 months post-transplant when immunosuppression levels may be subtherapeutic or during medication adjustments. It can occur at any time with inadequate immunosuppression.","obj":1},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which virus is most concerning for post-transplant infection in seronegative recipients receiving organs from seropositive donors?","opts":["Hepatitis A virus","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)","Rhinovirus","Parvovirus B19"],"ans":1,"expl":"CMV D+/R- (donor positive, recipient negative) represents the highest risk scenario. CMV can cause direct tissue invasion and indirect effects including increased rejection risk and opportunistic infections. Prophylaxis is standard for high-risk patients.","obj":6},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the primary purpose of induction therapy in kidney transplantation?","opts":["To treat a rejection episode that is already well established in the graft","To provide intense immunosuppression during the highest risk period immediately post-transplant","To replace the patient's maintenance immunosuppression for the long term","To prevent common viral infections during the first year after transplant"],"ans":1,"expl":"Induction therapy uses potent immunosuppressive agents (such as basiliximab or antithymocyte globulin) during the immediate post-transplant period when rejection risk is highest, allowing delayed introduction or lower doses of nephrotoxic maintenance agents.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"BK virus nephropathy primarily affects which part of the transplanted kidney?","opts":["Glomeruli only","Renal tubular epithelial cells","Blood vessels only","Collecting system only"],"ans":1,"expl":"BK virus infects and replicates in renal tubular epithelial cells, causing interstitial nephritis and potential graft loss. Screening with urine decoy cells or plasma BK viral load is standard in the first year post-transplant.","obj":2},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which prophylactic medication is typically given to prevent Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP) in transplant recipients?","opts":["Oral acyclovir taken daily","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Oral fluconazole taken daily","Oral isoniazid taken daily"],"ans":1,"expl":"TMP-SMX is first-line PJP prophylaxis, recommended for a minimum of 6-12 months post-transplant. Prophylaxis is often extended (lifelong in some programs) and re-started after rejection treatment or intensified immunosuppression; it also covers Toxoplasma.","obj":6},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the hallmark histological finding of chronic antibody-mediated rejection?","opts":["Acute tubular necrosis of the graft","Transplant glomerulopathy with double contours","Severe acute tubular injury of the graft","Glomerular hyperfiltration in the graft"],"ans":1,"expl":"Chronic antibody-mediated rejection is characterized by transplant glomerulopathy (duplication of glomerular basement membranes), interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and often C4d deposition in peritubular capillaries.","obj":1},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which factor is an absolute contraindication to kidney transplantation?","opts":["Age over 70 years","Active malignancy","Controlled hypertension","Type 2 diabetes"],"ans":1,"expl":"Active malignancy is generally an absolute contraindication due to the risk of cancer progression with immunosuppression. Most centers require a cancer-free period (typically 2-5 years depending on cancer type) before listing.","obj":3},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the standard preservation solution used for deceased donor kidneys during cold storage?","opts":["Chilled normal saline with added dextrose","University of Wisconsin (UW) solution","Lactated Ringer's solution at 4°C","5% dextrose in water, chilled to 4°C"],"ans":1,"expl":"University of Wisconsin solution is the most commonly used preservation fluid, containing impermeants and substrates that minimize cellular swelling and injury during cold ischemia. It allows safe preservation for 24-48 hours.","obj":3},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which maintenance immunosuppression regimen is most commonly used in kidney transplantation?","opts":["Monotherapy using prednisone alone for maintenance immunosuppression","Triple therapy: calcineurin inhibitor + antimetabolite + corticosteroid","Dual therapy combining two different calcineurin inhibitors","A steroid-only maintenance regimen for all kidney transplant patients"],"ans":1,"expl":"Triple therapy combining a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus), antimetabolite (mycophenolate), and corticosteroid provides synergistic immunosuppression with different mechanisms, allowing lower doses of each agent and reduced toxicity.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What has become the leading cause of late allograft loss (beyond the first year)?","opts":["Hyperacute rejection","Death with a functioning graft","Acute rejection","Surgical complications"],"ans":1,"expl":"Beyond the first year, death with a functioning graft, most often from cardiovascular disease and infection, has become the leading cause of allograft loss, reflecting improved rejection prevention alongside the ongoing burden of immunosuppression. In the FIRST year the leading causes are rejection, vascular thrombosis, and surgical complications.","obj":1},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which HLA locus has traditionally been considered most important for kidney transplant matching?","opts":["HLA-DP","HLA-DR","HLA-A","HLA-C"],"ans":1,"expl":"HLA-DR has traditionally been considered the most important locus for kidney transplant matching, followed by HLA-B and HLA-A. Contemporary practice increasingly recognizes the importance of HLA-DQ and of avoiding donor-specific antibodies against any locus, including DQ and DP. Zero-antigen-mismatch deceased-donor kidneys still receive priority allocation.","obj":2},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is delayed graft function (DGF) defined as?","opts":["Need for dialysis within the first week post-transplant","Elevated creatinine noted after the first six months","Immediate and abundant urine output right after transplant","Acute cellular rejection confirmed by biopsy in the first week"],"ans":0,"expl":"DGF is typically defined as the need for dialysis within the first week after transplantation. It is more common with deceased donor kidneys, prolonged cold ischemia time, and donor hemodynamic instability.","obj":3},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which medication requires therapeutic drug monitoring due to narrow therapeutic index and variable metabolism?","opts":["Prednisone","Mycophenolate mofetil","Tacrolimus","Dapsone"],"ans":2,"expl":"Tacrolimus has significant interpatient variability in metabolism via CYP3A4/5, requiring regular trough level monitoring. Target levels vary by time post-transplant and rejection risk.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the primary mechanism of action of mycophenolate mofetil?","opts":["Inhibition of calcineurin and T-cell signaling pathways","Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase","Inhibition of the mTOR pathway and cell cycle progression","Blockade of the IL-2 receptor on activated T-lymphocytes"],"ans":1,"expl":"Mycophenolate inhibits IMPDH, blocking de novo purine synthesis. Lymphocytes depend on this pathway (unlike other cells that can use salvage pathways), providing selective immunosuppression.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which condition is a common metabolic complication of long-term corticosteroid use?","opts":["Hypoglycemia and low fasting glucose levels","Hyperglycemia and new-onset diabetes","Hypotension and chronic low blood pressure","Weight loss and progressive muscle wasting"],"ans":1,"expl":"Corticosteroids cause insulin resistance, increased hepatic gluconeogenesis, and can lead to post-transplant diabetes mellitus (PTDM). They also contribute to weight gain, dyslipidemia, and bone disease.","obj":1},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the recommended screening interval for BK virus in the first year post-transplant?","opts":["Weekly plasma viral load testing for the first six months after transplant","Monthly for first 3-6 months, then every 3 months (continuing 1-2 years)","Annually, with a single plasma BK viral load checked at each yearly visit","Only when symptoms such as fever or rising creatinine develop in the patient"],"ans":1,"expl":"Standard BK virus screening is monthly plasma BK viral load (or urine decoy cells) for the first 3-6 months, then every 3 months, continuing to at least 1-2 years post-transplant, because BK viremia typically appears after the first month and peaks within the first year. Early detection allows immunosuppression reduction before BK nephropathy develops; screening should also be prompted by a rising creatinine.","obj":2},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which type of rejection is primarily mediated by donor-specific antibodies?","opts":["Acute cellular rejection","Antibody-mediated rejection","Interstitial fibrosis","Drug toxicity"],"ans":1,"expl":"Antibody-mediated rejection (AMR) is caused by donor-specific antibodies binding to graft endothelium, activating complement and causing injury. It can be acute or chronic and is diagnosed by histology, C4d staining, and DSA detection.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which solute removal mechanism in CRRT relies primarily on hydrostatic pressure to move fluid and dissolved solutes across a semipermeable membrane?","opts":["Diffusion","Convection","Adsorption","Ultrafiltration alone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Convection uses hydrostatic pressure to create solvent drag, pulling solutes along with fluid across the membrane. This is the primary mechanism in hemofiltration-based modalities like CVVH.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In CVVHD (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodialysis), what is the primary mechanism of solute clearance?","opts":["Convection with replacement fluid","Diffusion with dialysate flow","Adsorption to membrane","Plasma separation"],"ans":1,"expl":"CVVHD uses dialysate flowing counter-current to blood, creating concentration gradients that drive diffusive solute clearance, similar to conventional hemodialysis but at lower flow rates.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What distinguishes CVVHDF from CVVH and CVVHD?","opts":["It uses convection alone, without any diffusion","It uses diffusion alone without replacement fluid","It combines both convection and diffusion","It runs with no dialysate or replacement fluid"],"ans":2,"expl":"CVVHDF (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodiafiltration) combines both convective clearance (via replacement fluid) and diffusive clearance (via dialysate), maximizing solute removal across molecular weight ranges.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"SLED (Sustained Low-Efficiency Dialysis) is best described as:","opts":["A continuous therapy run for a full 24 hours without interruption","An extended intermittent therapy typically run 6-12 hours","A high-flow, rapid dialysis session lasting under two hours","A variant of peritoneal dialysis using the abdominal cavity"],"ans":1,"expl":"SLED is an extended intermittent renal replacement therapy, typically performed for 6-12 hours with lower blood and dialysate flows than conventional HD, offering hemodynamic stability between continuous and intermittent approaches.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"According to KDIGO guidelines, what is the recommended delivered CRRT dose for critically ill patients with AKI?","opts":["15-20 mL/kg/hr","20-25 mL/kg/hr","25-30 mL/kg/hr","35-40 mL/kg/hr"],"ans":1,"expl":"KDIGO recommends a delivered dose of 20-25 mL/kg/hr for CRRT in critically ill patients. Higher doses have not shown improved outcomes in major trials.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which vascular access site is preferred for CRRT catheter placement when feasible?","opts":["Femoral vein","Internal jugular vein","Subclavian vein","External jugular vein"],"ans":1,"expl":"The right internal jugular vein is preferred as it provides straight access to the SVC/RA junction, has lower infection risk than femoral, and avoids subclavian stenosis risk that could compromise future AV fistula creation.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is a key advantage of regional citrate anticoagulation over systemic heparin in CRRT?","opts":["Lower cost","Reduced bleeding risk","Simpler monitoring","Faster circuit clotting"],"ans":1,"expl":"Citrate anticoagulation works regionally within the circuit by chelating calcium, minimizing systemic anticoagulation effects and reducing bleeding risk, particularly beneficial in patients with coagulopathy or active bleeding.","obj":5},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"When using citrate anticoagulation in CRRT, which electrolyte requires close monitoring for potential accumulation?","opts":["Potassium","Sodium","Calcium","Phosphate"],"ans":2,"expl":"Citrate chelates ionized calcium in the circuit, so ionized calcium (both circuit and systemic) requires close monitoring, along with the total-to-ionized calcium ratio. Normally the citrate-calcium complex is metabolized by the liver and kidney, regenerating bicarbonate (which can cause a metabolic ALKALOSIS). When citrate is not metabolized, as in liver failure or severe shock, citrate ACCUMULATES, producing a high anion gap metabolic ACIDOSIS with systemic hypocalcemia, a rising total-to-ionized calcium ratio, and rising sodium from the citrate load.","obj":5},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In sepsis-induced AKI requiring CRRT, what is the primary indication for initiating renal replacement therapy?","opts":["An elevated serum creatinine alone","Life-threatening complications of AKI","Oliguria lasting for about 6 hours","A BUN level that rises above 50 mg/dL"],"ans":1,"expl":"CRRT initiation in sepsis-induced AKI is based on life-threatening complications: severe acidosis, hyperkalemia, volume overload refractory to diuretics, or uremic complications—not solely on BUN/creatinine values.","obj":6},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which buffer solution is most commonly used in modern CRRT and provides better hemodynamic stability than lactate?","opts":["Acetate","Bicarbonate","Lactate","Citrate alone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Bicarbonate-buffered solutions are now standard in CRRT, providing more physiologic pH correction and better hemodynamic stability compared to lactate, which requires hepatic metabolism and can cause vasodilation.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the primary cause of circuit clotting in CRRT?","opts":["Low blood flow rate","Inadequate anticoagulation","High ultrafiltration rate","All of the above"],"ans":3,"expl":"Circuit clotting can result from multiple factors: low blood flow causing stasis, inadequate anticoagulation allowing thrombus formation, and high ultrafiltration rates increasing hemoconcentration within the filter.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"During CRRT, transmembrane pressure (TMP) elevation most commonly indicates:","opts":["Filter clotting","Access recirculation","Hypotension","Electrolyte imbalance"],"ans":0,"expl":"Rising TMP suggests increased resistance to ultrafiltration, typically from filter membrane fouling or clotting. Progressive TMP elevation often precedes complete circuit thrombosis and signals need for circuit change.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the typical blood flow rate range for CRRT?","opts":["50-100 mL/min","100-200 mL/min","200-300 mL/min","300-400 mL/min"],"ans":1,"expl":"CRRT typically uses blood flow rates of 100-200 mL/min, lower than conventional hemodialysis. This allows for gentler, continuous solute and fluid removal with better hemodynamic tolerance.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which complication is specifically associated with rapid correction of chronic hypernatremia during CRRT?","opts":["Cardiac arrhythmias","Cerebral edema","Hemolysis","Hypotension"],"ans":1,"expl":"Rapid correction of chronic hypernatremia can cause cerebral edema: brain cells that accumulated idiogenic osmoles swell as extracellular tonicity falls too quickly. Sodium correction should not exceed 10 mEq/L per 24 hours (about 0.5 mEq/L/hr).","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In CRRT, what does the term 'effluent' refer to?","opts":["The blood returning to patient","The dialysate inflow","The waste fluid removed from circuit","The replacement fluid"],"ans":2,"expl":"Effluent is the total waste fluid removed from the CRRT circuit, including ultrafiltrate, spent dialysate, and any net fluid removal. Effluent flow rate determines the prescribed CRRT dose.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which electrolyte abnormality is most commonly encountered during initiation of CRRT in severe AKI?","opts":["Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypophosphatemia"],"ans":2,"expl":"Hyperkalemia is a common indication for urgent RRT initiation in AKI. CRRT effectively removes potassium, but initial rates should be monitored to avoid overly rapid shifts that could precipitate arrhythmias.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the primary mechanism of phosphate removal in CRRT?","opts":["Diffusion only","Convection only","Both diffusion and convection","Adsorption to membrane"],"ans":2,"expl":"Phosphate is removed by both diffusion and convection in CRRT. However, phosphate clearance is often inadequate, leading to hypophosphatemia during prolonged CRRT, requiring supplementation.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which factor most significantly reduces delivered CRRT dose compared to prescribed dose?","opts":["Blood flow rate","Replacement fluid rate","Treatment downtime","Dialysate temperature"],"ans":2,"expl":"Treatment downtime from circuit clotting, patient transport, procedures, or machine alarms significantly reduces delivered dose. Studies show delivered dose is often 15-30% lower than prescribed due to interruptions.","obj":5},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"For a patient with severe metabolic acidosis (pH 7.15) and AKI, which CRRT prescription adjustment would best address the acidosis?","opts":["Decrease the effluent rate to reduce bicarbonate losses","Increase bicarbonate concentration in dialysate/replacement fluid","Reduce the blood flow rate to limit further acid production","Switch to heparin anticoagulation to improve acid clearance"],"ans":1,"expl":"Increasing bicarbonate concentration in the dialysate or replacement fluid enhances bicarbonate delivery, helping correct metabolic acidosis. Standard solutions contain 25-32 mEq/L bicarbonate, which can be adjusted based on acid-base status.","obj":3},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the primary advantage of continuous renal replacement therapies over intermittent hemodialysis in hemodynamically unstable patients?","opts":["Lower cost","Faster solute clearance","Better hemodynamic tolerance","Shorter treatment time"],"ans":2,"expl":"CRRT provides slow, continuous fluid and solute removal over 24 hours, avoiding the rapid osmotic and volume shifts of intermittent HD that can precipitate hypotension in unstable patients.","obj":3},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with stage 4 CKD has a hemoglobin level of 9.2 g/dL. What is the primary mechanism causing anemia in this patient?","opts":["Decreased erythropoietin production by the kidneys","Increased red blood cell destruction","Acute blood loss from the GI tract","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"ans":0,"expl":"In CKD, damaged kidneys produce less erythropoietin (EPO), the hormone that stimulates red blood cell production in bone marrow. This is the primary cause of anemia in CKD patients.","obj":2},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which laboratory finding is most characteristic of secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD?","opts":["Elevated PTH with low or normal calcium","Low PTH with high calcium","Normal PTH with low phosphate","Elevated PTH with high calcium"],"ans":0,"expl":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD presents with elevated PTH as the parathyroid glands overwork to compensate for low calcium (caused by low vitamin D activation) and high phosphate levels.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD patient presents with metabolic acidosis. Which mechanism best explains this complication?","opts":["Decreased ability to excrete hydrogen ions and regenerate bicarbonate","Increased production of lactic acid caused by tissue hypoperfusion","Excessive loss of bicarbonate through chronic diarrhea and vomiting","Failure of respiratory compensation to correct the acid load"],"ans":0,"expl":"Damaged kidneys lose the ability to excrete acid (hydrogen ions) and regenerate bicarbonate, leading to metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the primary nursing concern when a CKD patient has a serum potassium level of 6.2 mEq/L?","opts":["Risk of life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias","Increased risk of bleeding","Development of metabolic alkalosis","Progression of bone disease"],"ans":0,"expl":"Hyperkalemia above 6.0 mEq/L can cause dangerous cardiac arrhythmias including ventricular fibrillation. This is a medical emergency requiring immediate intervention.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which intervention is most appropriate for managing hyperphosphatemia in a CKD patient?","opts":["Administering phosphate binders with meals","Increasing dietary phosphate intake","Giving vitamin D supplements alone","Restricting fluid intake"],"ans":0,"expl":"Phosphate binders should be taken with meals to bind dietary phosphate in the gut, preventing its absorption. This is a cornerstone of CKD-MBD management.","obj":2},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with diabetic nephropathy is at highest risk for which cardiovascular complication?","opts":["Accelerated atherosclerosis and coronary artery disease","Pulmonary hypertension from chronic volume overload","Aortic stenosis from accelerated valve calcification","Peripheral varicose veins from chronic venous stasis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Diabetic nephropathy significantly increases cardiovascular risk due to accelerated atherosclerosis, endothelial dysfunction, and chronic inflammation associated with both diabetes and CKD.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the target hemoglobin range for most CKD patients being treated with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs)?","opts":["10-11 g/dL","12-14 g/dL","8-9 g/dL","14-16 g/dL"],"ans":0,"expl":"Current guidelines recommend targeting hemoglobin of 10-11 g/dL in CKD patients on ESAs. Higher targets increase cardiovascular risk without improving quality of life.","obj":2},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which type of bone disease is characterized by low bone turnover and suppressed PTH in CKD patients?","opts":["Adynamic bone disease","Osteitis fibrosa cystica","Osteoporosis","Paget's disease"],"ans":0,"expl":"Adynamic bone disease features low bone turnover with suppressed PTH, often from over-suppression with vitamin D analogs or calcium-based binders. It increases fracture and calcification risk.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the primary reason CKD patients develop dyslipidemia?","opts":["Decreased clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins","Increased dietary fat absorption in the intestine","Enhanced HDL production by the liver and intestine","Reduced cholesterol synthesis within the liver"],"ans":0,"expl":"CKD causes dyslipidemia primarily through decreased clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins (VLDL, chylomicrons) due to reduced lipoprotein lipase activity.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD patient complains of persistent nausea, loss of appetite, and muscle wasting. Which complication should the nurse suspect?","opts":["Protein-energy wasting (malnutrition)","Chronic hypercalcemia and bone pain","Metabolic alkalosis and low potassium","Iron overload with hemochromatosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"These symptoms indicate protein-energy wasting, a common complication in advanced CKD caused by uremic toxins, inflammation, metabolic acidosis, and inadequate nutrient intake.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which form of vitamin D requires activation by the kidneys and is typically deficient in CKD patients?","opts":["Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D)","Cholecalciferol (vitamin D3)","Ergocalciferol (vitamin D2)","25-hydroxyvitamin D"],"ans":0,"expl":"Calcitriol is the active form of vitamin D produced by kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase. In CKD, this enzyme activity decreases, leading to calcitriol deficiency and impaired calcium absorption.","obj":5},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the most common cause of death in patients with chronic kidney disease?","opts":["Cardiovascular disease","Infection","Progression to ESRD","Malignancy"],"ans":0,"expl":"Cardiovascular disease accounts for approximately 50% of deaths in CKD patients, with risk increasing as kidney function declines. This includes coronary artery disease, heart failure, and sudden cardiac death.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with CKD has elevated intact PTH, elevated phosphate, and low calcium. What stage of CKD-MBD does this represent?","opts":["Secondary hyperparathyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism"],"ans":0,"expl":"This pattern indicates secondary hyperparathyroidism: the parathyroid glands increase PTH production in response to low calcium and high phosphate from impaired kidney function.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which dietary modification is most important for a CKD patient with hyperkalemia?","opts":["Limiting high-potassium foods like bananas, oranges, and potatoes","Increasing protein intake to rebuild muscle mass and strength","Restricting all dairy products to reduce dietary calcium and phosphorus","Eliminating all carbohydrates to reduce the glycemic and potassium load"],"ans":0,"expl":"Patients with hyperkalemia should limit high-potassium foods. Common sources include bananas, oranges, tomatoes, potatoes, spinach, and salt substitutes containing potassium chloride.","obj":2},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the role of sevelamer in CKD management?","opts":["Non-calcium phosphate binder that reduces phosphate absorption","A potassium-lowering medication that removes excess potassium","An erythropoietin stimulator that raises red blood cell counts","A vitamin D analog that suppresses parathyroid hormone release"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sevelamer is a non-calcium, non-aluminum phosphate binder that binds dietary phosphate in the intestine. It's preferred when calcium levels are elevated or vascular calcification is present.","obj":5},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which finding suggests uremic pruritus in a dialysis patient?","opts":["Generalized itching without visible rash, worse at night","Localized rash with vesicles","Hives appearing after medication administration","Dry, scaly patches only on extremities"],"ans":0,"expl":"Uremic pruritus presents as generalized itching without primary skin lesions, often worse at night. It's related to uremic toxins, dry skin, and immune dysregulation in CKD.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD patient is prescribed sodium bicarbonate. What complication is being treated?","opts":["Metabolic acidosis","Hyperkalemia","Hyperphosphatemia","Anemia"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sodium bicarbonate treats metabolic acidosis by providing bicarbonate to buffer excess acid. Target serum bicarbonate is typically 22-26 mEq/L in CKD patients.","obj":5},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which CKD increases fracture risk?","opts":["CKD-mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MBD) affecting bone quality","Increased physical activity and weight-bearing exercise over time","Excessive vitamin D intake causing abnormal bone turnover","Chronically low phosphate levels that weaken bone structure"],"ans":0,"expl":"CKD-MBD causes abnormal bone turnover, mineralization, and strength through disturbances in calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D metabolism, significantly increasing fracture risk.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which iron study pattern is typical in CKD-related anemia?","opts":["Normal or high ferritin with low transferrin saturation","Low ferritin with high transferrin saturation","Low ferritin with low transferrin saturation","High ferritin with high transferrin saturation"],"ans":0,"expl":"CKD anemia often shows functional iron deficiency: normal/high ferritin (acute phase reactant) but low transferrin saturation due to inflammation blocking iron release from stores.","obj":1},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the significance of elevated FGF23 in early CKD?","opts":["Early marker of phosphate retention and predictor of cardiovascular events","An indicator of improved kidney function and enhanced phosphate excretion","A sign of iron deficiency and impaired red blood cell production in CKD","A marker of resolved metabolic acidosis and improved acid-base balance"],"ans":0,"expl":"FGF23 rises early in CKD to promote phosphate excretion. However, elevated FGF23 is independently associated with left ventricular hypertrophy and increased cardiovascular mortality.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"A 4-year-old presents with periorbital edema and 4+ proteinuria on dipstick. Which finding confirms nephrotic syndrome?","opts":["Serum albumin of 4.0 g/dL","Serum albumin less than 3.0 g/dL with edema","Blood pressure above the 95th percentile","Serum creatinine of 1.5 mg/dL"],"ans":1,"expl":"In children, nephrotic syndrome requires nephrotic-range proteinuria (UPCR at least 2.0 mg/mg), hypoalbuminemia (serum albumin below 3.0 g/dL per KDIGO 2025), and edema. The correct choice captures the low-albumin plus edema criteria; a normal albumin or isolated hypertension does not confirm nephrotic syndrome.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which organism is most commonly associated with typical hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) in children?","opts":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Escherichia coli O157:H7","Staphylococcus aureus","Salmonella typhi"],"ans":1,"expl":"Typical HUS is most commonly caused by Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, particularly serotype O157:H7, often acquired from undercooked ground beef or contaminated produce.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the preferred initial dialysis modality for infants and young children with end-stage kidney disease?","opts":["Hemodialysis via central venous catheter","Peritoneal dialysis","Continuous venovenous hemofiltration","Kidney transplantation immediately"],"ans":1,"expl":"Peritoneal dialysis is preferred in infants and young children because it provides gentle continuous ultrafiltration, preserves residual kidney function, and allows better growth and nutrition compared to hemodialysis.","obj":3},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"A newborn male has bilateral hydronephrosis on prenatal ultrasound. Which condition should be highest on the differential?","opts":["Vesicoureteral reflux","Posterior urethral valves","Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Multicystic dysplastic kidney"],"ans":1,"expl":"Posterior urethral valves (PUV) occur only in males and cause bilateral hydronephrosis due to bladder outlet obstruction. It is the most common cause of lower urinary tract obstruction in male infants.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which medication is first-line treatment for minimal change disease in children?","opts":["Cyclosporine","Mycophenolate mofetil","Prednisone","Rituximab"],"ans":2,"expl":"Corticosteroids (prednisone) are first-line therapy for minimal change disease, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. Most children achieve remission within 4-8 weeks of steroid therapy.","obj":5},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the classic triad of hemolytic uremic syndrome?","opts":["Fever, rash, and migratory joint pain with conjunctivitis and swelling","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury","Hematuria, proteinuria, and hypertension with peripheral edema","Vomiting, diarrhea, and dehydration with severe abdominal cramping"],"ans":1,"expl":"HUS is characterized by the triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes on smear), thrombocytopenia (low platelets), and acute kidney injury. This results from endothelial damage caused by Shiga toxin.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which nutritional intervention is most important for children with chronic kidney disease?","opts":["High-protein diet to prevent malnutrition","Adequate caloric intake to support growth","Unrestricted potassium intake","High-phosphorus diet for bone health"],"ans":1,"expl":"Children with CKD require adequate caloric intake (often 100-140% of recommended daily allowance) to support growth. Protein should be adequate but not excessive, and potassium/phosphorus may need restriction depending on kidney function.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the most common congenital anomaly of the kidney and urinary tract (CAKUT)?","opts":["Renal agenesis","Horseshoe kidney","Hydronephrosis","Posterior urethral valves"],"ans":2,"expl":"Hydronephrosis is the most common CAKUT, detected in approximately 1-5% of prenatal ultrasounds. It can result from various causes including UPJ obstruction, VUR, or PUV.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"A child with posterior urethral valves is at highest risk for which long-term complication?","opts":["Recurrent urinary tract infections only","Chronic kidney disease and bladder dysfunction","Nephrotic syndrome","Kidney stones"],"ans":1,"expl":"Children with PUV are at significant risk for chronic kidney disease due to obstructive uropathy and associated bladder dysfunction (valve bladder). Long-term monitoring of kidney function and bladder health is essential.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which laboratory finding is characteristic of renal tubular acidosis in children?","opts":["Metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Renal tubular acidosis causes a normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis due to impaired acid excretion or bicarbonate reabsorption. Potassium levels vary by RTA type.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the most appropriate initial imaging study for a child with suspected vesicoureteral reflux?","opts":["Renal ultrasound of both kidneys","Voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG)","CT urogram with contrast dye","MRI of the abdomen and pelvis"],"ans":1,"expl":"VCUG is the gold standard for diagnosing vesicoureteral reflux as it visualizes urine flow during voiding and can grade the severity of reflux. Ultrasound may suggest VUR but cannot confirm it.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which finding on urinalysis suggests glomerular disease in a child?","opts":["Isolated leukocyte esterase positivity","Dysmorphic red blood cells and red cell casts","Crystalluria with normal pH","Specific gravity of 1.005"],"ans":1,"expl":"Dysmorphic RBCs and red cell casts indicate glomerular bleeding and are hallmarks of glomerulonephritis. These findings suggest the kidney itself is the source of hematuria.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the primary goal of growth hormone therapy in children with CKD?","opts":["To improve kidney function and slow CKD progression","To achieve normal height velocity and final adult height","To reduce proteinuria and limit glomerular damage","To prevent bone fractures and strengthen bone density"],"ans":1,"expl":"Growth hormone therapy in children with CKD aims to overcome growth failure and achieve normal height velocity and final adult height. It does not improve kidney function but addresses CKD-related growth impairment.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which electrolyte abnormality is most concerning in a child with acute kidney injury and oliguria?","opts":["Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hyperphosphatemia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Hyperkalemia is the most immediately life-threatening electrolyte abnormality in AKI with oliguria, as it can cause fatal arrhythmias. It requires urgent treatment with calcium, insulin/glucose, and possibly dialysis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the most common cause of acute kidney injury in hospitalized children?","opts":["Glomerulonephritis","Ischemic or nephrotoxic injury","Congenital anomalies","Urinary tract obstruction"],"ans":1,"expl":"In hospitalized children, AKI is most commonly caused by ischemic injury (sepsis, hypotension, dehydration) or nephrotoxic medications (aminoglycosides, vancomycin, contrast). This is called intrinsic or acute tubular necrosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which statement about steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome in children is correct?","opts":["It rarely progresses to kidney failure","Kidney biopsy is typically recommended","Steroids should be continued indefinitely","It is always caused by minimal change disease"],"ans":1,"expl":"Steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome (no remission after 8 weeks of steroids) typically requires kidney biopsy to determine underlying pathology, as focal segmental glomerulosclerosis and other lesions are common causes.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the recommended daily fluid intake calculation for a healthy child weighing 25 kg?","opts":["1000 mL plus 20 mL per kg over 20 kg","1500 mL plus 20 mL per kg over 20 kg","100 mL per kg for all weight","50 mL per kg for all weight"],"ans":1,"expl":"The Holliday-Segar method calculates maintenance fluids as: 100 mL/kg for first 10 kg, 50 mL/kg for next 10 kg, and 20 mL/kg for each kg over 20 kg. For 25 kg: 1000 + 500 + 100 = 1600 mL/day.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which condition is associated with an increased risk of Wilms tumor in children?","opts":["Nephrotic syndrome","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Posterior urethral valves","Vesicoureteral reflux"],"ans":1,"expl":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (characterized by macrosomia, macroglossia, and abdominal wall defects) is associated with increased risk of Wilms tumor. These children require regular renal ultrasound screening.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"What is the primary mechanism of anemia in children with chronic kidney disease?","opts":["Iron deficiency from dietary restrictions","Decreased erythropoietin production","Hemolysis from uremic toxins","Bone marrow suppression from medications"],"ans":1,"expl":"Anemia in CKD is primarily due to decreased erythropoietin production by damaged kidneys. Treatment typically involves erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron supplementation.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"Which intervention is most effective in preventing progression of chronic kidney disease in children?","opts":["A high-protein diet started early in childhood","Blood pressure control with ACE inhibitors or ARBs","Aggressive diuretic therapy given daily to all children","Calcium supplementation given alone, without other therapy"],"ans":1,"expl":"Blood pressure control, particularly with ACE inhibitors or ARBs, is the most effective intervention to slow CKD progression in children. These medications also reduce proteinuria, which is independently nephrotoxic.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Which physiologic change occurs in the kidneys during normal pregnancy?","opts":["GFR decreases by 20-30% due to increased plasma volume","GFR increases by 40-50% starting in the first trimester","Serum creatinine rises to 1.2 mg/dL as a normal adaptation","Renal plasma flow decreases significantly after week 20"],"ans":1,"expl":"During normal pregnancy, GFR increases by 40-50% beginning in the first trimester due to increased renal plasma flow and vasodilation. Serum creatinine normally decreases to 0.4-0.8 mg/dL, not increases.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"A pregnant patient at 28 weeks has BP 150/95 mmHg and proteinuria. She had normal BP before pregnancy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","opts":["Chronic hypertension present before the pregnancy","Gestational hypertension progressing to preeclampsia","White coat hypertension from clinic-related anxiety","Renal artery stenosis causing secondary hypertension"],"ans":1,"expl":"New-onset hypertension after 20 weeks gestation with proteinuria indicates preeclampsia. Chronic hypertension would have been present before pregnancy or before 20 weeks gestation.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Which laboratory finding helps differentiate chronic hypertension from preeclampsia in a pregnant patient?","opts":["Elevated uric acid levels","Hypertension present before 20 weeks gestation","Proteinuria greater than 300 mg/24 hours","Platelet count below 100,000/mcL"],"ans":1,"expl":"Chronic hypertension is defined as hypertension present before pregnancy or diagnosed before 20 weeks gestation. Preeclampsia develops after 20 weeks. Both can have proteinuria, but timing distinguishes them.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the hallmark triad of HELLP syndrome?","opts":["Hypertension, edema, liver failure","Hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets","Hyperuricemia, edema, lethargy, proteinuria, seizures","Hypotension, elevated lactate, low potassium"],"ans":1,"expl":"HELLP syndrome stands for Hemolysis (microangiopathic), Elevated Liver enzymes, and Low Platelets. It is a severe variant of preeclampsia requiring immediate delivery.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Which medication is considered first-line for blood pressure control in pregnant patients with chronic kidney disease?","opts":["Lisinopril","Losartan","Labetalol","Atenolol"],"ans":2,"expl":"Labetalol is first-line for BP control in pregnancy. ACE inhibitors (lisinopril) and ARBs (losartan) are contraindicated due to fetal renal toxicity. Atenolol is associated with fetal growth restriction.","obj":5},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"A patient with stage 3 CKD (baseline creatinine 1.8 mg/dL) becomes pregnant. What is the most significant risk?","opts":["Progression to end-stage kidney disease during pregnancy","Preeclampsia superimposed on CKD","Development of nephrotic syndrome","Acute tubular necrosis from hyperemesis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Women with CKD have a 25-50% risk of developing superimposed preeclampsia. While CKD may progress, the most common and significant complication is preeclampsia, which increases maternal and fetal morbidity.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the recommended target blood pressure for pregnant patients with chronic kidney disease?","opts":["Below 120/80 mmHg","Below 135/85 mmHg","Below 140/90 mmHg","Below 150/100 mmHg"],"ans":2,"expl":"Current guidelines recommend maintaining BP below 140/90 mmHg in pregnant patients with CKD. Aggressive lowering below 120/80 may compromise placental perfusion.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"In a pregnant patient whose baseline serum creatinine was 0.6 mg/dL, which finding is most concerning for acute kidney injury?","opts":["Serum creatinine of 0.6 mg/dL, unchanged","Serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (a rise of 0.4 mg/dL)","Urine output of 1,200 mL/day","BUN of 12 mg/dL"],"ans":1,"expl":"Normal pregnancy creatinine is about 0.4-0.8 mg/dL because GFR increases. AKI is defined by a rise in creatinine of >=0.3 mg/dL within 48 hours or >=1.5x baseline within 7 days, so a rise from 0.6 to 1.0 mg/dL qualifies, whereas stable creatinine and a normal BUN are reassuring.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the most common cause of acute kidney injury in pregnancy?","opts":["Acute tubular necrosis from sepsis","Preeclampsia/HELLP syndrome","Posterior urethral valves","Renal vein thrombosis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Preeclampsia and HELLP syndrome are the most common causes of AKI in pregnancy, accounting for approximately 50-60% of cases. Other causes include septic abortion, hemorrhage, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"A pregnant patient at 32 weeks presents with fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness. What is the most appropriate initial management?","opts":["Oral nitrofurantoin and outpatient follow-up","IV ceftriaxone and hospital admission","Wait for urine culture before starting antibiotics","Renal ultrasound only, no antibiotics"],"ans":1,"expl":"This presentation indicates pyelonephritis, which requires hospitalization and IV antibiotics in pregnancy due to high risk of complications including preterm labor, sepsis, and respiratory distress.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Which antibiotic is safest for treating urinary tract infection in the first trimester of pregnancy?","opts":["Ciprofloxacin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Cephalexin","Doxycycline"],"ans":2,"expl":"Cephalexin (a cephalosporin) is safe throughout pregnancy. Fluoroquinolones affect fetal cartilage, TMP-SMX is contraindicated in first trimester (folate antagonist) and near term, and tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration.","obj":5},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the minimum dialysis prescription for pregnant patients with end-stage kidney disease?","opts":["Standard 3 times weekly, 3-4 hours per session","Daily or near-daily dialysis totaling 20+ hours weekly","Peritoneal dialysis only, no hemodialysis","Twice weekly, 2 hours per session"],"ans":1,"expl":"Pregnant patients on dialysis require intensive regimens (daily or near-daily, 20+ hours/week) to maintain BUN below 50 mg/dL and minimize fluid shifts, which improves fetal outcomes.","obj":3},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Why is intensive dialysis required during pregnancy in patients with ESKD?","opts":["To prevent maternal hypertension and limit weight gain only","To maintain low BUN levels for fetal growth and prevent polyhydramnios","To increase maternal weight gain and improve the appetite during pregnancy","To reduce the need for erythropoietin and iron supplementation"],"ans":1,"expl":"High BUN levels are associated with poor fetal outcomes including intrauterine growth restriction and polyhydramnios. Intensive dialysis maintains BUN <50 mg/dL, mimicking the low BUN of normal pregnancy.","obj":3},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the most common obstetric complication in pregnant patients receiving dialysis?","opts":["Placenta accreta","Preterm delivery","Uterine rupture","Postpartum hemorrhage"],"ans":1,"expl":"Preterm delivery occurs in 50-80% of pregnancies in dialysis patients. Other complications include intrauterine growth restriction, polyhydramnios, and spontaneous abortion, especially in first trimester.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Which parameter should be monitored weekly in pregnant patients with CKD?","opts":["24-hour urine protein and blood pressure","Serum albumin level checked once weekly","Kidney ultrasound performed every week","Bone mineral density measured each week"],"ans":0,"expl":"Weekly monitoring of BP and proteinuria is essential to detect superimposed preeclampsia early. Additional monitoring includes fetal growth scans and assessment of kidney function.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"A pregnant patient with lupus nephritis should be counseled that pregnancy is safest when:","opts":["Disease has been quiescent for at least 6 months","She is on high-dose prednisone","Proteinuria is greater than 3 g/day","Serum creatinine is above 2.0 mg/dL"],"ans":0,"expl":"Pregnancy outcomes are best when lupus nephritis has been in remission for at least 6 months before conception. Active disease, significant proteinuria, or elevated creatinine increase risks substantially.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the effect of pregnancy on long-term kidney function in women with mild CKD (stage 1-2)?","opts":["Pregnancy accelerates progression to ESKD in all cases","Most women have no significant acceleration of kidney disease","All women will require dialysis within 5 years","Pregnancy has no effect on kidney disease progression"],"ans":1,"expl":"Women with mild CKD (stage 1-2) generally do not experience accelerated progression of kidney disease from pregnancy. However, those with moderate-severe CKD or significant proteinuria have higher risk of progression.","obj":1},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Which condition carries the highest maternal mortality risk in pregnant women with kidney disease?","opts":["Pulmonary hypertension associated with CKD","Stage 2 CKD with controlled blood pressure","History of urinary tract infection","Nephrolithiasis without obstruction"],"ans":0,"expl":"Pulmonary hypertension carries very high maternal mortality (commonly reported 30-50%) and is generally considered a contraindication to pregnancy, requiring effective contraception and specialist preconception counseling. It is not, however, a universally enforced absolute, and advanced CKD (stage 4-5) and other severe systemic disease also confer high risk. The other listed conditions do not by themselves contraindicate pregnancy.","obj":2},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"What is the role of low-dose aspirin in pregnant patients with CKD?","opts":["Contraindicated due to bleeding risk","Recommended starting at 12 weeks to reduce preeclampsia risk","Only used if platelet count is below 50,000","Started only after delivery"],"ans":1,"expl":"Low-dose aspirin (81 mg daily) starting at 12 weeks gestation is recommended for women with CKD to reduce the risk of preeclampsia, which they are at high risk for developing.","obj":5},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"Postpartum, what should be monitored in women who had preeclampsia with kidney involvement?","opts":["No follow-up needed if delivery was uncomplicated","Blood pressure and kidney function for at least 3 months","Only urine culture at 6 weeks","Kidney biopsy immediately after delivery"],"ans":1,"expl":"Women with preeclampsia need postpartum monitoring of BP and kidney function for at least 3 months, as some may have persistent hypertension or kidney dysfunction. They also have increased long-term CV and CKD risk.","obj":2},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which antibiotic requires significant dose reduction when GFR falls below 30 mL/min due to renal elimination?","opts":["Azithromycin","Vancomycin","Doxycycline","Clindamycin"],"ans":1,"expl":"Vancomycin is primarily eliminated by the kidneys and accumulates in renal failure, requiring dose adjustment based on trough levels and GFR.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Why should metformin be discontinued in patients with eGFR below 30 mL/min?","opts":["Causes hyperglycemia","Risk of lactic acidosis","Increases proteinuria","Causes hypertension"],"ans":1,"expl":"Metformin accumulation in severe renal impairment increases the risk of lactic acidosis, a rare but potentially fatal complication.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which medication class is relatively contraindicated in CKD because it can precipitate acute kidney injury?","opts":["ACE inhibitors","NSAIDs","Calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers"],"ans":1,"expl":"NSAIDs are relatively contraindicated rather than absolutely banned in CKD. Particularly with reduced eGFR, volume depletion, or concurrent ACE inhibitor/ARB/diuretic use, prostaglandin inhibition reduces afferent arteriolar vasodilation and predisposes to AKI. ACE inhibitors are renoprotective in CKD.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Nitrofurantoin is contraindicated in CKD primarily because:","opts":["It causes direct nephrotoxicity in the kidney","It lacks efficacy due to poor urinary concentration","It raises blood pressure and worsens hypertension","It causes dangerous hyperkalemia in patients with CKD"],"ans":1,"expl":"Nitrofurantoin requires adequate urinary concentrations for efficacy, which cannot be achieved when GFR is reduced; it also increases toxicity risk.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which drug is most likely to be removed during hemodialysis?","opts":["Warfarin","Gentamicin","Amiodarone","Diazepam"],"ans":1,"expl":"Gentamicin is water-soluble, has low protein binding, and small molecular weight, making it highly dialyzable.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"When dosing vancomycin in a hemodialysis patient, the typical approach is:","opts":["Daily dosing","Dose after each dialysis session","Dose before each dialysis session","No dose adjustment needed"],"ans":1,"expl":"Vancomycin is partially removed by dialysis, so it is typically dosed after hemodialysis sessions based on trough levels.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which contrast-related precaution is recommended for CKD patients undergoing CT imaging?","opts":["Administer high-dose diuretics before the contrast","Hydrate with IV normal saline before and after","Give prophylactic antibiotics before the procedure","Avoid all contrast imaging permanently thereafter"],"ans":1,"expl":"IV hydration with normal saline before and after contrast administration helps reduce the risk of contrast-induced nephropathy.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which medication requires monitoring of drug levels in renal failure patients?","opts":["Lisinopril","Digoxin","Metoprolol","Amlodipine"],"ans":1,"expl":"Digoxin is renally eliminated and has a narrow therapeutic index, requiring level monitoring and dose adjustment in CKD.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Aminoglycosides in renal failure patients should be dosed:","opts":["At more frequent dosing intervals","With extended dosing intervals","At the usual standard intervals","Only by the intramuscular route"],"ans":1,"expl":"Aminoglycosides have prolonged half-lives in renal failure, requiring extended dosing intervals to prevent accumulation and ototoxicity/nephrotoxicity.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which drug is primarily metabolized by the liver and typically does NOT require renal dose adjustment?","opts":["Gabapentin","Levetiracetam","Diazepam","Pregabalin"],"ans":2,"expl":"Diazepam needs no renal dose adjustment because it is hepatically metabolized, but its active metabolite nordiazepam is renally cleared and accumulates in renal failure, prolonging sedation. Lorazepam and oxazepam are preferred. Gabapentin, pregabalin, and levetiracetam require renal adjustment.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"The primary mechanism by which ACE inhibitors protect kidneys in CKD is:","opts":["Increasing GFR directly","Reducing intraglomerular pressure","Increasing sodium reabsorption","Blocking aldosterone only"],"ans":1,"expl":"ACE inhibitors dilate efferent arterioles, reducing intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria, which slows CKD progression.","obj":2},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which electrolyte abnormality is a concern when using ACE inhibitors or ARBs in CKD?","opts":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia"],"ans":1,"expl":"ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce aldosterone secretion, potentially causing hyperkalemia, especially in advanced CKD.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) in severe renal failure should be:","opts":["Used at the same standard doses as usual","Avoided or dose-reduced with monitoring","Increased in dose to maintain effect","Given only by the subcutaneous route"],"ans":1,"expl":"LMWH accumulates in renal failure, increasing bleeding risk; unfractionated heparin is often preferred, or LMWH with dose reduction and anti-Xa monitoring.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which opioid is safest to use in patients with severe renal impairment?","opts":["Morphine","Meperidine","Fentanyl","Codeine"],"ans":2,"expl":"Fentanyl is hepatically metabolized and does not produce active renally-cleared metabolites, making it safer than morphine, meperidine, or codeine in renal failure.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Allopurinol dosing in CKD should be:","opts":["Increased to achieve uric acid goals","Started low and titrated slowly","Discontinued entirely","Given only during dialysis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Allopurinol and its active metabolite accumulate in CKD; start with low doses (50-100 mg) and titrate slowly to avoid toxicity.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which H2 blocker requires the most significant dose reduction in renal failure?","opts":["Famotidine","Ranitidine","Cimetidine","All require similar adjustment"],"ans":0,"expl":"Famotidine is primarily renally eliminated and requires significant dose reduction; all H2 blockers need adjustment but famotidine is most commonly used with renal dosing.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Baclofen toxicity in CKD patients typically presents as:","opts":["Seizures and confusion","Severe hypertension","Marked hyperglycemia","A diffuse skin rash"],"ans":0,"expl":"Baclofen is renally eliminated and can cause severe CNS toxicity including seizures, confusion, and coma in CKD patients; avoid or use extreme caution.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Which statin requires the least renal dose adjustment?","opts":["Atorvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pravastatin","Simvastatin"],"ans":0,"expl":"Atorvastatin is primarily hepatically metabolized and does not require significant dose adjustment in renal failure, unlike rosuvastatin and pravastatin.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"The timing of antibiotic dosing relative to hemodialysis depends mainly on:","opts":["Patient preference and daily routine","Drug dialyzability and half-life","Cost of the medication to the unit","The time of day of the session"],"ans":1,"expl":"Antibiotics that are highly dialyzable should be dosed after hemodialysis; those with long half-lives may not require timing adjustment.","obj":5},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"Regarding metformin and iodinated contrast, which statement reflects current guidance?","opts":["Metformin must be held on the day of the contrast study in every CKD patient, regardless of eGFR","Metformin does not need to be held when eGFR is at least 30; hold only if eGFR is below 30 or AKI develops","Metformin must be stopped permanently after any contrast exposure, regardless of kidney function","Metformin has no relationship to contrast exposure or to kidney function at any eGFR level"],"ans":1,"expl":"Current guidance (ACR 2018 manual, FDA 2016 labeling) is that metformin does not need to be withheld for iodinated contrast when eGFR is at least 30 mL/min/1.73 m2. Hold metformin if eGFR is below 30, if AKI occurs, or before intra-arterial studies with significant renal exposure, and recheck renal function before restarting. The concern is lactic acidosis if contrast-induced nephropathy causes metformin accumulation.","obj":5},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis presents with pH 7.25, HCO3- 14 mEq/L, and PaCO2 30 mmHg. What is the primary acid-base disorder?","opts":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Low pH and low bicarbonate indicate metabolic acidosis. The low PaCO2 represents appropriate respiratory compensation.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Which formula is used to calculate the anion gap?","opts":["Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-)","Na+ + (Cl- + HCO3-)","(Na+ + K+) - Cl-","Cl- - (Na+ + HCO3-)"],"ans":0,"expl":"Anion gap = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-). Normal range is typically 8-12 mEq/L, though this varies by lab.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"A patient has pH 7.50, HCO3- 32 mEq/L, and PaCO2 45 mmHg. What is the primary disorder?","opts":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Elevated pH and elevated bicarbonate indicate metabolic alkalosis. The PaCO2 is not low enough to suggest primary respiratory alkalosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"In respiratory acidosis, what is the expected compensatory response?","opts":["Increased renal bicarbonate reabsorption","Decreased renal bicarbonate reabsorption","Increased respiratory rate","Decreased carbonic anhydrase activity"],"ans":0,"expl":"In chronic respiratory acidosis, the kidneys compensate by increasing bicarbonate reabsorption to buffer the acidemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Which condition is associated with a high anion gap metabolic acidosis?","opts":["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Diarrhea","Renal tubular acidosis type 1","Excessive antacid use"],"ans":0,"expl":"DKA produces ketoacids (beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate) that increase the anion gap. Diarrhea and RTA cause normal anion gap acidosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Winter's formula is used to assess compensation in which disorder?","opts":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Winter's formula (expected PaCO2 = 1.5 × HCO3- + 8 ± 2) predicts appropriate respiratory compensation in metabolic acidosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"A patient with chronic kidney disease stage 4 is most likely to develop which acid-base disorder?","opts":["Normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis","A high anion gap metabolic acidosis with ketones","A metabolic alkalosis with a low serum chloride level","A respiratory alkalosis from chronic hyperventilation"],"ans":0,"expl":"Metabolic acidosis in CKD is usually a normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis and remains the predominant pattern well into advanced disease. The anion gap rises only when GFR is very low (roughly <15 mL/min/1.73 m2, stage 5), as sulfate, phosphate, and urate accumulate, producing a high anion gap or mixed picture. In CKD G4-G5 cohorts, normal anion gap acidosis is at least as common as high anion gap acidosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Type 1 (distal) renal tubular acidosis is characterized by which defect?","opts":["Inability to acidify urine in the distal tubule","Impaired bicarbonate reabsorption in proximal tubule","Aldosterone deficiency","Aldosterone resistance"],"ans":0,"expl":"Distal RTA involves defective hydrogen ion secretion in the distal nephron, preventing urine acidification despite systemic acidosis.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Which electrolyte abnormality is commonly associated with metabolic alkalosis?","opts":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"ans":0,"expl":"Metabolic alkalosis often causes hypokalemia due to intracellular potassium shifts and increased renal potassium wasting.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Lactic acidosis in sepsis is primarily caused by:","opts":["Tissue hypoperfusion and anaerobic metabolism","Excessive lactate administration","Impaired hepatic lactate clearance only","Renal lactate overproduction"],"ans":0,"expl":"In sepsis, tissue hypoperfusion leads to anaerobic metabolism, producing lactate. Impaired clearance also contributes.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"A patient presents with pH 7.30, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 24 mEq/L. What is the primary disorder?","opts":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Low pH with elevated PaCO2 indicates respiratory acidosis. Bicarbonate is normal, suggesting acute presentation without compensation.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Which medication can cause metabolic acidosis by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase?","opts":["Acetazolamide","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Lisinopril"],"ans":0,"expl":"Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, reducing bicarbonate reabsorption and causing metabolic acidosis.","obj":5},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"In a patient with metabolic alkalosis, a urine chloride level less than 10 mEq/L suggests:","opts":["Saline-responsive alkalosis","Saline-resistant alkalosis","Primary hyperaldosteronism","Renal artery stenosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Low urine chloride indicates volume depletion and chloride-responsive alkalosis (e.g., from vomiting or diuretic use).","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis is associated with:","opts":["Hyperkalemia and aldosterone deficiency or resistance","Hypokalemia and distal acidification defect","Bicarbonate wasting in proximal tubule","Normal potassium with severe acidosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"Type 4 RTA involves hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance, causing hyperkalemia and impaired ammonium excretion.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Which is the most appropriate initial treatment for severe metabolic acidosis (pH < 7.1) in DKA?","opts":["IV fluids and insulin therapy","IV sodium bicarbonate bolus","Oral bicarbonate tablets","Mechanical ventilation"],"ans":0,"expl":"DKA is treated with fluids and insulin to stop ketone production. Bicarbonate is rarely needed and may worsen outcomes.","obj":2},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"A patient with anxiety presents with pH 7.55, PaCO2 25 mmHg, HCO3- 22 mEq/L. What is the diagnosis?","opts":["Acute respiratory alkalosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"High pH with low PaCO2 indicates respiratory alkalosis. Normal bicarbonate suggests acute presentation without renal compensation.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"The normal anion gap range is approximately:","opts":["8-12 mEq/L","2-6 mEq/L","15-20 mEq/L","20-25 mEq/L"],"ans":0,"expl":"Normal anion gap is typically 8-12 mEq/L, though reference ranges vary. Albumin correction may be needed in hypoalbuminemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Which condition causes metabolic alkalosis through mineralocorticoid excess?","opts":["Primary hyperaldosteronism","Untreated Addison's disease","Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","Severe chronic diarrhea"],"ans":0,"expl":"Hyperaldosteronism increases sodium reabsorption and hydrogen/potassium excretion, causing metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"In chronic respiratory alkalosis, the expected renal compensation is:","opts":["Decreased bicarbonate reabsorption","Increased bicarbonate reabsorption","Increased acid excretion","No renal compensation"],"ans":0,"expl":"Chronic respiratory alkalosis causes renal compensation through decreased bicarbonate reabsorption, lowering serum bicarbonate.","obj":1},{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"Bicarbonate therapy in CKD patients is indicated when serum bicarbonate is:","opts":["Less than 22 mEq/L","Less than 18 mEq/L only","Greater than 26 mEq/L","Any value below normal"],"ans":0,"expl":"KDIGO suggests oral sodium bicarbonate supplementation when serum bicarbonate is persistently below 22 mEq/L in CKD, with the aim of slowing progression and improving metabolic control; many clinicians titrate to keep bicarbonate at or above 22 mEq/L (some target 22-24). Treating above 26 mEq/L is not indicated.","obj":2},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient with chronic kidney disease presents with peaked T waves and widened QRS on ECG. Which electrolyte abnormality is most likely?","opts":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyponatremia"],"ans":0,"expl":"Peaked T waves and widened QRS are classic ECG findings in hyperkalemia, a life-threatening emergency in CKD patients requiring immediate intervention.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which medication is administered first to stabilize cardiac membranes in severe hyperkalemia?","opts":["Insulin with dextrose","Calcium gluconate","Sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Albuterol"],"ans":1,"expl":"Calcium gluconate is given first to stabilize cardiac membranes and prevent arrhythmias, though it does not lower potassium levels.","obj":5},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient with SIADH typically presents with which sodium level pattern?","opts":["Hypernatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine","Hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine","Hyponatremia with inappropriately dilute urine","A normal sodium level with marked polyuria"],"ans":1,"expl":"SIADH causes hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine due to excess ADH secretion leading to water retention.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia can lead to which serious complication?","opts":["Acute cerebral edema with herniation","Osmotic demyelination syndrome","Severe rebound hyperkalemia","Acute pulmonary edema and hypoxia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis) occurs when chronic hyponatremia is corrected too rapidly, causing irreversible neurologic damage.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which finding is most characteristic of hypercalcemia?","opts":["Trousseau sign","Chvostek sign","Shortened QT interval","Prolonged QT interval"],"ans":2,"expl":"Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval on ECG. Trousseau and Chvostek signs are associated with hypocalcemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient with hypermagnesemia is most likely to exhibit which symptom?","opts":["Increased deep tendon reflexes","Hypotension and bradycardia","Tetany and muscle twitching","Generalized epileptic seizures"],"ans":1,"expl":"Hypermagnesemia causes hypotension, bradycardia, decreased deep tendon reflexes, and can progress to respiratory depression and cardiac arrest.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which intervention is appropriate for a patient with fluid overload and pulmonary edema?","opts":["Administer IV fluids","Place patient in Trendelenburg position","Administer IV loop diuretics","Restrict oxygen"],"ans":2,"expl":"IV loop diuretics like furosemide reduce fluid volume. Position patient upright, provide oxygen, and restrict fluids.","obj":2},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Hyperphosphatemia in renal failure commonly leads to which secondary electrolyte disturbance?","opts":["Hypercalcemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Elevated phosphate binds with calcium, causing hypocalcemia. This triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which ECG change is associated with hypocalcemia?","opts":["Shortened QT","Prolonged QT","Peaked T waves","U waves"],"ans":1,"expl":"Hypocalcemia prolongs the QT interval, increasing risk for ventricular arrhythmias including torsades de pointes.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient with diabetes insipidus would most likely present with:","opts":["Hyponatremia and concentrated urine","Hypernatremia and dilute urine","Hyperkalemia and oliguria","Hypocalcemia and polyuria"],"ans":1,"expl":"Diabetes insipidus causes inability to concentrate urine, leading to polyuria, dehydration, and hypernatremia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which treatment shifts potassium into cells for temporary management of hyperkalemia?","opts":["Calcium chloride","Sodium bicarbonate","Furosemide","Sodium polystyrene sulfonate"],"ans":1,"expl":"Treatments that shift potassium into cells include intravenous insulin with dextrose, inhaled beta-2 agonists, and, when the patient is acidemic, sodium bicarbonate. Calcium chloride does not shift potassium: it stabilizes the myocardium. Furosemide and sodium polystyrene sulfonate remove potassium from the body rather than shifting it. Bicarbonate is relatively weak and should not be used as sole therapy in a severe hyperkalemic emergency.","obj":2},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Severe hypomagnesemia is often associated with which other electrolyte abnormality?","opts":["Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hypernatremia","Hyperphosphatemia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Hypomagnesemia causes renal potassium wasting and impairs PTH secretion, often resulting in concurrent hypokalemia and hypocalcemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which assessment finding indicates worsening hyperkalemia?","opts":["Muscle cramps","Narrow QRS complex","Sinus bradycardia","Increased urine output"],"ans":2,"expl":"Progressive hyperkalemia causes bradycardia, heart block, and can deteriorate to ventricular fibrillation or asystole.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient with hypernatremia and hypovolemia should initially receive:","opts":["Hypotonic saline","Isotonic saline","Hypertonic saline","Oral water only"],"ans":1,"expl":"Isotonic saline restores intravascular volume first. Once hemodynamically stable, free water deficit is corrected gradually.","obj":2},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which condition is a common cause of hyperkalemia in dialysis patients?","opts":["Excessive diarrhea","Missed dialysis treatment","Hyperaldosteronism","Loop diuretic use"],"ans":1,"expl":"Missed dialysis treatments allow potassium to accumulate. Dialysis patients have limited renal potassium excretion.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Trousseau sign is elicited by:","opts":["Tapping the facial nerve","Inflating a blood pressure cuff","Checking deep tendon reflexes","Observing gait"],"ans":1,"expl":"Trousseau sign is carpal spasm induced by inflating a BP cuff above systolic pressure for 3 minutes, indicating hypocalcemia.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which medication can cause hyperkalemia as a side effect?","opts":["Oral furosemide","Spironolactone","Oral albuterol","Regular insulin"],"ans":1,"expl":"Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that can cause hyperkalemia, especially in patients with renal impairment.","obj":5},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient with severe symptomatic hyponatremia (seizures, confusion) requires which immediate action?","opts":["Administer hypertonic (3%) saline","Restrict fluids and repeat the sodium level later","Encourage oral water intake","Administer hypotonic fluids"],"ans":0,"expl":"Severe symptomatic hyponatremia with seizures or altered mental status requires an immediate bolus of 3% hypertonic saline, given under a provider order, with correction limited to <=8 mmol/L per 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination. Fluid restriction alone is too slow for an emergency.","obj":2},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"Which lab value indicates the severity of hypercalcemia crisis?","opts":["Serum albumin","Ionized calcium","Serum phosphate","BUN level"],"ans":1,"expl":"Ionized calcium represents the biologically active form and best reflects severity of hypercalcemia, independent of albumin levels.","obj":1},{"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","q":"A patient receiving rapid blood transfusions is at risk for which electrolyte imbalance?","opts":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyponatremia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Stored blood contains high potassium levels from cell lysis. Rapid transfusion can cause hyperkalemia, especially in renal patients.","obj":1},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which immunosuppressant requires therapeutic drug monitoring due to its narrow therapeutic index and risk of nephrotoxicity?","opts":["Tacrolimus","Mycophenolate mofetil","Prednisone","Basiliximab"],"ans":0,"expl":"Tacrolimus is a calcineurin inhibitor with a narrow therapeutic window requiring regular blood level monitoring to balance efficacy against nephrotoxicity and other adverse effects.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is the primary mechanism of action of tacrolimus?","opts":["Inhibits calcineurin, reducing IL-2 production","Blocks purine synthesis in lymphocytes","Inhibits mTOR pathway","Binds to IL-2 receptor on T-cells"],"ans":0,"expl":"Tacrolimus binds to FKBP-12, inhibiting calcineurin and preventing dephosphorylation of NFAT, which reduces IL-2 transcription and T-cell activation.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which food should transplant patients avoid while taking calcineurin inhibitors?","opts":["Grapefruit","Bananas","Spinach","Orange juice"],"ans":0,"expl":"Grapefruit inhibits CYP3A4 metabolism, significantly increasing blood levels of tacrolimus and cyclosporine, raising toxicity risk.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is a common side effect specific to cyclosporine but less common with tacrolimus?","opts":["Gingival hyperplasia","New-onset diabetes","Tremor","Diarrhea"],"ans":0,"expl":"Gingival hyperplasia occurs in up to 30% of patients on cyclosporine but is rare with tacrolimus, making it a distinguishing side effect.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which antimetabolite immunosuppressant is most associated with bone marrow suppression and leukopenia?","opts":["Mycophenolate mofetil","Tacrolimus (Prograf)","Prednisone (Deltasone)","Cyclosporine (Neoral)"],"ans":0,"expl":"MMF inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, selectively blocking lymphocyte proliferation; leukopenia and anemia are its characteristic toxicities. Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) and corticosteroids are not typically myelosuppressive, so MMF is the best answer.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is the mechanism of action of basiliximab?","opts":["IL-2 receptor antagonist on activated T-cells","A calcineurin inhibitor acting on T-cells","An mTOR inhibitor blocking cell division","Antithymocyte globulin that depletes T-cells"],"ans":0,"expl":"Basiliximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds to the alpha chain of the IL-2 receptor, preventing T-cell activation during induction therapy.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which medication requires monitoring for new-onset diabetes after transplant (NODAT)?","opts":["Tacrolimus","Mycophenolate","Azathioprine","Basiliximab"],"ans":0,"expl":"Tacrolimus impairs pancreatic beta-cell function and insulin secretion, with NODAT occurring in 10-20% of transplant recipients.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is the primary advantage of using antithymocyte globulin (ATG) for induction?","opts":["Potent T-cell depletion","Oral administration","No infection risk","No monitoring required"],"ans":0,"expl":"ATG causes profound T-cell depletion through complement-mediated lysis, providing powerful induction immunosuppression for high-risk patients.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which drug interaction would significantly increase tacrolimus levels?","opts":["Fluconazole","Omeprazole","Metformin","Amlodipine"],"ans":0,"expl":"Azole antifungals like fluconazole are strong CYP3A4 inhibitors that can double or triple tacrolimus levels, requiring dose reduction and close monitoring.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is a key nursing consideration when administering mycophenolate?","opts":["Monitor for signs of infection and bleeding","Administer with grapefruit juice","Check blood glucose before dosing","Give only in the morning"],"ans":0,"expl":"Mycophenolate causes immunosuppression and bone marrow suppression, requiring monitoring for infection signs and bleeding tendencies.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which immunosuppressant is associated with hyperlipidemia as a common side effect?","opts":["Sirolimus","Tacrolimus","Basiliximab","Azathioprine"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sirolimus (an mTOR inhibitor) commonly causes elevated cholesterol and triglycerides, requiring lipid monitoring and possible statin therapy.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is the typical therapeutic trough range for tacrolimus in the early post-transplant period?","opts":["8-12 ng/mL","2-4 ng/mL","15-20 ng/mL","20-25 ng/mL"],"ans":0,"expl":"Early post-transplant tacrolimus trough targets are typically 8-12 ng/mL, though ranges vary by institution and transplant type.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which medication is a prodrug converted to 6-mercaptopurine in the body?","opts":["Azathioprine","Mycophenolate mofetil","Tacrolimus","Cyclosporine"],"ans":0,"expl":"Azathioprine is metabolized to 6-mercaptopurine, which interferes with DNA synthesis in proliferating lymphocytes.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is the most appropriate action when a patient on cyclosporine develops elevated creatinine?","opts":["Notify provider for possible dose adjustment","Immediately discontinue the medication","Increase the dose","Switch to oral administration"],"ans":0,"expl":"Calcineurin inhibitor nephrotoxicity is dose-dependent; elevated creatinine requires provider notification for potential dose reduction.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which statement about steroid therapy in transplant is correct?","opts":["Long-term use causes osteoporosis and weight gain","Steroids are never used in maintenance therapy","Steroids have no metabolic side effects","Steroids should be stopped abruptly"],"ans":0,"expl":"Chronic prednisone use causes bone density loss, weight gain, hyperglycemia, and adrenal suppression requiring gradual tapering.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What monitoring is essential for patients on sirolimus?","opts":["Lipid panel and complete blood count","Blood glucose levels checked weekly","Liver enzyme levels checked monthly","Urinalysis performed every week"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sirolimus causes hyperlipidemia, thrombocytopenia, and anemia, requiring regular lipid panels and CBC monitoring.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which immunosuppressant is most nephrotoxic?","opts":["Cyclosporine","Mycophenolate","Prednisone","Azathioprine"],"ans":0,"expl":"Both cyclosporine and tacrolimus cause dose-dependent nephrotoxicity through afferent arteriole vasoconstriction, but cyclosporine has higher rates.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is the purpose of therapeutic drug monitoring in transplant patients?","opts":["Maintain therapeutic levels while minimizing toxicity","Determine whether the patient is taking the drug as prescribed","Calculate the exact dose the patient should receive","Diagnose rejection episodes before they progress"],"ans":0,"expl":"TDM ensures drug levels stay within the therapeutic window, balancing rejection prevention against toxicity risks.","obj":5},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"Which vaccine should be avoided in transplant patients on immunosuppression?","opts":["Live attenuated vaccines","Inactivated influenza vaccine","Hepatitis B vaccine","Tetanus toxoid"],"ans":0,"expl":"Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, live attenuated influenza) are contraindicated due to risk of vaccine-derived infection in immunosuppressed patients.","obj":6},{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"What is a key difference between tacrolimus and cyclosporine?","opts":["Tacrolimus has higher diabetes risk; cyclosporine causes more gingival hyperplasia","They have nearly identical side effect profiles and equal diabetes risk","Cyclosporine is more potent than tacrolimus at equivalent doses in most patients","Tacrolimus requires no drug level monitoring at any time after transplant"],"ans":0,"expl":"While both are calcineurin inhibitors, tacrolimus carries higher NODAT risk, while cyclosporine more commonly causes gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism.","obj":5},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A nurse explains to a patient that their kidney function has declined to 'stage 4 CKD with an eGFR of 25.' The patient nods, but later asks a family member what 'eGFR' means. Which action by the nurse best reflects health literacy principles?","opts":["Repeat the explanation using the same terms so the patient becomes familiar with them","Re-explain in plain language, such as 'your kidneys are working at about 25 percent,' and confirm understanding","Provide a printed handout defining eGFR and have the patient read it before the next visit","Document that the patient was educated and refer further questions to the nephrologist"],"ans":1,"expl":"Health literacy principles call for plain language and confirmation of understanding, not repeated jargon. Naming a number the patient cannot interpret does not transfer meaning. A handout the patient must decode alone adds burden, and deferring the patient's questions to the physician does not meet the nurse's teaching responsibility.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A hemodialysis patient who speaks limited English needs teaching about fluid restriction. The patient's adult child offers to interpret. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Accept the offer, since family members know the patient's medical history best","Use a professional medical interpreter and speak directly to the patient","Provide written materials in English for the patient to review at home","Teach the adult child, who can then explain the information to the patient later"],"ans":1,"expl":"Teaching must reach the patient, which requires a qualified medical interpreter; family members may filter, omit, or soften information and may not know medical terminology. Speaking to the patient directly with the interpreter present preserves the nurse-patient relationship. Written English materials and teaching the family member alone leave the patient without direct comprehension.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"After teaching a patient about phosphate binders, the nurse asks, 'Do you have any questions?' The patient says no. Which approach would more reliably confirm that the patient understood?","opts":["Asking the patient to explain when and how they will take the binder","Repeating the same information more loudly and slowly","Giving the patient a written quiz to complete at home","Documenting that the patient verbalized understanding"],"ans":0,"expl":"Asking whether the patient has questions invites a yes or no answer that may reflect politeness or uncertainty rather than comprehension. Having the patient restate the plan in their own words reveals what was actually understood and where correction is needed. Repeating verbatim, quizzing at home, or documenting an unverified statement do not confirm learning.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A nurse is teaching a patient who will perform home hemodialysis to set up the machine. Which teaching approach is most appropriate for a psychomotor skill?","opts":["Provide a detailed written manual and review it with the patient page by page","Demonstrate the setup, then have the patient perform it with supervision and corrective feedback","Explain the steps verbally and then ask the patient to repeat them back aloud","Show a video of the setup and ask the patient to describe the steps afterward"],"ans":1,"expl":"Psychomotor skills are learned through demonstration followed by supervised practice with feedback, which lets the nurse correct technique as it develops. Written manuals, verbal repetition, and watching a video address only the cognitive step; none allows the nurse to observe and correct the patient's actual performance.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A nurse is preparing to teach a patient how to care for a newly created arteriovenous fistula. Which action should the nurse take first?","opts":["Assess what the patient already knows about fistula care and how they prefer to learn","Demonstrate fistula care and then have the patient repeat the steps back","Give the patient a written handout describing how to care for the fistula","Schedule the patient to attend a group class on vascular access care"],"ans":0,"expl":"Teaching begins with assessment, because what the patient already knows, what misconceptions they hold, and how they learn best determine what to teach and how to teach it. Demonstrating, handing over written material, or scheduling a class all deliver content without first establishing the starting point.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"An older hemodialysis patient has a hearing impairment and wears a hearing aid. Which action best supports the patient's learning during teaching?","opts":["Speak loudly and slowly in a raised voice","Face the patient, speak at a normal volume, and reduce surrounding noise","Write all information on paper instead of speaking","Ask a family member to repeat the information at home"],"ans":1,"expl":"Speaking face to face at a normal volume lets the patient use lip reading and avoids the distortion that shouting creates with a hearing aid, and reducing background noise, a real issue in a dialysis unit, improves reception. Shouting, limiting teaching to writing, and delegating to family members all bypass the patient's own comprehension.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with diabetic retinopathy and advanced CKD cannot read standard printed handouts. Which approach is most appropriate for discharge teaching?","opts":["Provide the handout anyway and ask the patient to have someone read it aloud","Use large-print or audio materials and review key points verbally with the patient","Rely on the nephrologist to cover the content at the next visit","Delay teaching until the patient's vision is corrected"],"ans":1,"expl":"Teaching must be delivered in a format the patient can actually use, so materials should be adapted to large print or audio with verbal review to confirm understanding. Handing over a document the patient cannot read shifts the teaching responsibility to others, and waiting for a change in vision may mean the teaching never happens.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient whose traditional diet includes several high-potassium foods is reluctant to follow the renal diet. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Tell the patient that these foods must be eliminated entirely","Explore which foods matter most to the patient and work with the dietitian to adapt the diet around them","Refer the patient to the social worker for nonadherence","Provide a standard renal diet handout and review it again at the next visit"],"ans":1,"expl":"Culturally responsive teaching begins by understanding what foods mean to the patient and negotiating an achievable plan with the renal dietitian, which is far more likely to be followed than a blanket prohibition. Treating food preferences as nonadherence, or repeating a standard handout that ignores the patient's diet, leaves the real barrier unaddressed.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient starting peritoneal dialysis wants their spouse to help with exchanges at home. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Teach the spouse instead of the patient, since the spouse will perform the procedure","Ask the patient's permission, then include the spouse in the teaching and practice sessions","Explain that only the patient may be taught because of privacy rules","Send written instructions home with the spouse to review"],"ans":1,"expl":"Including a caregiver is valuable, but it requires the patient's consent, and the patient should remain a learner rather than being bypassed. Teaching both together lets them practice as they will at home. Privacy rules do not prevent a caregiver from learning with the patient's permission, and written instructions alone do not build the skill.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which documentation best reflects the outcome of patient teaching about a low-sodium diet?","opts":["'Diet education about sodium was provided today, and the patient verbalized understanding of the teaching.'","'Reviewed low-sodium diet; patient named three foods to avoid and correctly described how to read labels for sodium.'","'Patient was taught about sodium restriction according to the standard unit protocol during this visit.'","'Patient was counseled regarding diet, and teaching will be reinforced at the next scheduled visit.'"],"ans":1,"expl":"Documentation of teaching should record the content, the method, and evidence of the patient's learning. The entry describing what the patient named and demonstrated shows the teaching took effect, while 'verbalized understanding,' 'taught per protocol,' and 'will reinforce' record only that teaching occurred, with no evidence that learning happened.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with stage 4 CKD received education about slowing progression 6 months ago. At today's visit the nurse notes the patient has stopped following the plan. Which action is most appropriate?","opts":["Document nonadherence and continue with the current plan","Reassess the patient's understanding and barriers, then reinforce teaching as needed","Tell the patient that worsening labs are the consequence of their choices","Refer the patient to another nephrologist for a second opinion"],"ans":1,"expl":"Retention fades and circumstances change, so education is an ongoing process rather than a one-time event. Reassessing understanding and asking what has made the plan difficult identifies whether the obstacle is knowledge, resources, or motivation. Labeling the patient nonadherent, or attributing worsening labs to their choices, closes off the conversation without addressing the cause.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A patient on hemodialysis tells the nurse, 'Once I finish dialysis a few times, my kidneys will start working again and I can stop.' Which response by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["'That is not correct. Dialysis is permanent, so you need to accept that reality and adjust your expectations for the future.'","Acknowledge the hope, gently clarify that dialysis replaces kidney function rather than restoring it, and check what the patient understood","Change the subject for now and revisit the topic later, when the patient seems more receptive to discussing it further","Refer the patient to the nephrologist so that the physician can answer that question fully at the next clinic visit"],"ans":1,"expl":"The patient holds an inaccurate belief that could lead to dangerous decisions about treatment, so it needs correcting, but correcting it in a way that preserves the relationship keeps the patient open to further learning. A blunt statement shuts down discussion, avoiding the topic leaves the misconception in place, and the nurse is responsible for teaching rather than deferring every question.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient learning peritoneal dialysis says, 'I will never be able to do this on my own.' Which action by the nurse best supports learning?","opts":["Reassure the patient that everyone eventually learns the technique with enough time and practice","Break the procedure into small steps the patient can master one at a time, acknowledging each success","Offer to have the patient's family perform the exchanges for them until they feel ready","Extend the training schedule week after week until the patient finally stops expressing doubt"],"ans":1,"expl":"Confidence is built by succeeding at manageable steps, which is the essence of supporting self-efficacy and removes the sense of being overwhelmed by the whole procedure. Vague reassurance does not build skill, arranging for someone else to take over confirms the patient's doubt, and simply adding time without changing the approach does not address it.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"An older hemodialysis patient has mild cognitive impairment and needs teaching about fluid restriction. Which approach is most appropriate?","opts":["Teach the same content as for any other adult patient, to avoid appearing condescending to them","Simplify the content, involve a caregiver with the patient's consent, and repeat teaching across several sessions","Provide a detailed written handout and ask the patient to read and follow it carefully at home","Ask the physician to decide whether teaching the patient about their care is worthwhile at this point"],"ans":1,"expl":"Teaching is adapted to the learner, so mild cognitive impairment calls for simpler content, repetition over time, and a caregiver's support with the patient's consent. Delivering complex content at full pace, relying on a written handout the patient may not retain, or treating the patient as unable to learn all reduce the chance that learning occurs.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient becomes hypotensive and fatigued toward the end of hemodialysis treatments. When should the nurse plan most of this patient's teaching?","opts":["During the last 30 minutes of the treatment","On a non-treatment day or earlier in the session, when the patient is alert and comfortable","Immediately after the patient comes off the machine","At the start of a treatment on a day when the patient skipped breakfast"],"ans":1,"expl":"Learning requires the patient to be alert and comfortable, so teaching should be timed when the patient is not depleted by post-treatment fatigue or intradialytic symptoms. Teaching at the end of treatment, immediately afterward, or when the patient is hungry all reduce attention and retention regardless of how good the content is.","obj":4},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A patient who received a kidney transplant is being discharged tomorrow. Which content should the nurse prioritize for teaching?","opts":["The history of the organ allocation system and how the national waiting list works","Recognizing the warning signs of rejection and infection, and when to seek help immediately","Community support group options and where to find them locally after discharge","Long-term meal planning and the patient's food preferences after discharge"],"ans":1,"expl":"When teaching time is limited, the content that must be covered is what causes harm if it is missed, which for a new transplant recipient is recognizing rejection and infection and knowing when to act. Allocation history, support groups, and long-term dietary planning are all legitimate topics that can be taught after discharge without immediate risk.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient received teaching about phosphate binders and dietary phosphorus restriction 2 months ago. Which finding best indicates the teaching was effective?","opts":["The patient can name the phosphate binder they take and the exact dose they were prescribed","The patient's serum phosphorus is within the target range and they describe taking the binder with meals","The patient states that they never miss a scheduled clinic appointment or a routine laboratory draw","The patient requests an early refill of the phosphate binder prescription from the clinic pharmacy"],"ans":1,"expl":"Evaluating teaching looks at whether behavior changed and the clinical outcome improved, so a phosphorus level at target combined with a correct description of when to take the binder shows the teaching took hold. Naming the medication, keeping appointments, or refilling the prescription show engagement but not that the teaching changed what the patient does.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A CKD clinic offers group education sessions in addition to individual teaching. Which is the main advantage of group teaching for these patients?","opts":["It reduces the amount of clinical content that must be taught","Patients learn from one another's experiences and questions, which can normalize common concerns","It removes the need to assess each patient's individual learning needs","It guarantees that all patients receive identical information at the same pace"],"ans":1,"expl":"Group sessions add value through peer exchange, letting patients hear how others handle the same problems, which reduces isolation and prompts questions they might not ask alone. Group teaching does not reduce the content needed, does not replace individual assessment of learning needs, and cannot assume that every patient learns at the same pace.","obj":4},{"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","q":"A 6-year-old child is starting hemodialysis and the nurse is planning teaching. Which approach is most appropriate?","opts":["Teach the child using the same detailed materials used for adult patients","Teach the parents as the primary caregivers and give the child simple, age-appropriate explanations","Defer all teaching until the child is old enough to understand","Ask the child to demonstrate the procedure before discharge"],"ans":1,"expl":"Teaching is matched to developmental stage, so caregivers carry responsibility for the child's care while the child receives explanations suited to their understanding. Adult-level materials or expecting a 6-year-old to demonstrate the procedure exceed the child's developmental capacity, and postponing all teaching leaves both the child and family unprepared.","obj":4},{"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","q":"A patient with CKD asks the nurse whether it is safe to become pregnant. Which approach best reflects appropriate patient teaching?","opts":["Advise the patient that pregnancy is too dangerous with CKD and should be avoided entirely for her safety","Provide balanced information about the risks, confirm the patient's understanding, and involve the nephrology and obstetric teams","Tell the patient that the decision belongs entirely to the physician and then end the discussion about pregnancy","Provide statistics about pregnancy outcomes in CKD and then let the patient decide entirely on her own without further guidance"],"ans":1,"expl":"Teaching in this situation means giving balanced information the patient can understand and connecting them to the specialists who can counsel them in detail, which supports an informed decision. Directive advice removes the patient's role in the decision, refusing to discuss it abandons the teaching responsibility, and presenting statistics without confirming understanding leaves the patient to interpret complex risk alone.","obj":4},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A patient's dose of a renally cleared medication has been reduced. Which approach best confirms that the patient understands why?","opts":["Give the patient the new prescription and assume that the pharmacy staff will explain the reason for the change to them","Explain in plain terms that the kidneys clear the drug more slowly now, then ask the patient to describe in their own words why the dose changed","Tell the patient that the dose changed and that they should not worry about the details or the reason for the adjustment","Provide the patient with the package insert, highlight the dosing section, and ask them to read it carefully at home"],"ans":1,"expl":"Understanding the reason behind a change supports adherence and helps the patient recognize a dosing error, and teach-back is what verifies that understanding. Relying on the pharmacy, dismissing the details, or handing over a package insert written for professionals does not confirm that this patient understands their own regimen.","obj":4},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"A patient in the intensive care unit is receiving continuous renal replacement therapy. The family asks the nurse what the machine is doing. Which response is most appropriate?","opts":["Explain in plain language that the machine is doing the work of the kidneys, and check what they understood","Tell the family that the physician will explain everything later when there is more time","Provide a detailed technical explanation of dialysis principles and fluid balance to the family","Explain that the family should limit their questions for now to avoid disrupting the patient's care"],"ans":0,"expl":"Families facing a critical illness need clear, plain explanations to reduce anxiety and to participate in decisions, and confirming understanding ensures the teaching landed. Deferring every explanation, using technical language, or discouraging questions all leave the family uninformed at the moment they most need orientation.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient on peritoneal dialysis asks the nurse, 'Can I go swimming at the lake this summer?' Which interpretation and action is most appropriate?","opts":["The question shows the patient understands PD well enough to plan activities, so no teaching is needed","The question reveals a knowledge gap about PD precautions, so the nurse should teach about protecting the catheter and preventing peritonitis","The nurse should tell the patient that all swimming is prohibited","The nurse should refer the question to the physician and document that it was asked"],"ans":1,"expl":"A patient's question is a cue about what they do not yet know, and this one points to a gap around catheter care and infection risk. Assuming the question means the patient is well informed misses that cue. A blanket prohibition is inaccurate and unhelpful, since guidance depends on the type of water and on catheter care, and deferring the question leaves the teaching undone.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient has many questions but the dialysis session is ending. Which approach best supports the patient's learning?","opts":["Answer all of the patient's questions as quickly as possible so that none of them are left unaddressed at all","Ask the patient which concerns matter most right now, address the most important one, and plan to cover the rest at a set time","Hand the patient a written handout covering all of the topics and then end the teaching session for the day","Tell the patient to write all of the questions down and bring the list to the physician at the next visit"],"ans":1,"expl":"Setting priorities with the patient respects their concerns and ensures the most important content is covered well rather than all of it covered poorly. Rushing through everything, substituting a handout for teaching, or redirecting the patient's questions to the physician all leave the patient's actual concerns unaddressed.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"During a hemodialysis treatment a patient becomes hypotensive and the nurse needs to notify the nephrologist. Which communication approach is most appropriate?","opts":["Report the patient's blood pressure alone and wait for instructions","Use a structured handoff such as SBAR: state the situation, the background, the nurse's assessment, and a specific recommendation","Send a message through the electronic record and continue monitoring until the physician responds","Ask the charge nurse to call the physician so the message comes from a supervisor"],"ans":1,"expl":"Structured communication such as SBAR gives the physician the situation, relevant background, the nurse's assessment, and a clear recommendation, which supports a safe and timely decision. Reporting a number without context, relying on an asynchronous message, or routing the concern through a supervisor all delay a decision that needs to be made now.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A dialysis unit is short staffed and the charge nurse asks a dialysis technician to help. Which task is outside the technician's scope and must remain with the registered nurse?","opts":["Reinforcing a patient's fluid restriction using the plan the nurse already established","Assessing a newly created arteriovenous fistula for a thrill and bruit","Obtaining the patient's pre-treatment weight","Setting up the machine and priming the extracorporeal circuit"],"ans":1,"expl":"Assessment, including evaluating a vascular access, is a registered nurse responsibility that cannot be delegated. Reinforcing teaching already planned by the nurse, obtaining weights, and machine setup are tasks that may be assigned to trained assistive personnel under the nurse's supervision. Right task, right circumstance, right person, and right supervision govern every delegation decision.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A nurse notices that the dialysate potassium concentration was prepared incorrectly but catches the error before the patient is connected. Which action best reflects a culture of safety?","opts":["Correct the error and say nothing, since the patient was not harmed","Report the near miss through the unit's event reporting system so the process can be examined","Mention it informally to a colleague to warn them to be careful","Wait to see whether the same mistake happens again before reporting"],"ans":1,"expl":"Near misses are reported precisely because no harm occurred; they reveal a process weakness while there is still time to fix it, and a just culture depends on staff reporting without fear of blame. Staying silent, warning only a colleague, or waiting for a recurrence discards the most useful safety information the unit has.","obj":7},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"A medication error occurred in the dialysis unit and the nurse involved is asked to participate in a root cause analysis. Which contribution is most valuable?","opts":["Identifying which individual staff member was responsible for making the medication error that occurred","Describing the sequence of events and the system factors, such as staffing, labeling, and workflow, that allowed the error","Recommending that the nurse involved receive a formal written warning and additional remedial training","Confirming that the unit will not report the medication event externally to any outside regulatory agency"],"ans":1,"expl":"Root cause analysis looks past individual blame to the conditions that made the error possible, so the most useful contribution is a factual account of the events and the system factors such as look-alike labeling, workload, and interruptions. Naming an individual, recommending discipline, or discussing reporting requirements does not change the process that produced the error.","obj":7},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A PD program wants to shorten the time patients wait for training after their catheter is placed. Which approach reflects the Plan-Do-Study-Act improvement model?","opts":["Change the entire training schedule at once and evaluate after six months","Test one small change with a few patients, study the result, then adapt and test again on a larger scale","Assign one nurse to complete all training regardless of scheduling barriers","Survey patients about satisfaction with training and take no further action"],"ans":1,"expl":"Plan-Do-Study-Act works through small, rapid tests of change that are studied and refined before wider adoption, which limits the risk of a failed large-scale change. Reorganizing everything at once, relying on one person, or collecting satisfaction data without acting on it are not improvement cycles and leave the underlying delay unaddressed.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A dialysis unit is preparing for a survey of the Conditions for Coverage. Which action by the staff nurse best supports readiness?","opts":["Review the unit's own practices, documentation, and adherence to its written policies in daily care","Memorize the regulations so they can be recited during the survey","Ask the medical director to handle all survey questions from staff","Focus on the areas that were cited in the last survey and leave the rest unchanged"],"ans":0,"expl":"Survey readiness is a byproduct of consistently following written policies and documenting care, so day-to-day adherence is what a survey measures. Reciting regulations, deferring every question to the medical director, or addressing only previously cited items leaves the rest of practice unexamined and unready.","obj":7},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with advanced CKD tells the nurse they have been skipping a prescribed medication because they cannot afford it. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Explain the importance of the medication again and encourage the patient to find a way to pay for it","Refer the patient to the social worker and explore assistance programs, while continuing to involve the team","Document the patient's nonadherence and notify the physician","Suggest that the patient take the medication every other day to reduce the cost"],"ans":1,"expl":"Cost is a barrier that the clinical team can often resolve through social work, manufacturer assistance, or alternative coverage, so referral rather than persuasion is the effective step. Repeating the rationale, simply documenting nonadherence, or inventing an unverified dosing change does not remove the obstacle and may cause harm.","obj":7},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with CKD takes medications prescribed by several specialists. Which collaboration best improves the safety of this patient's regimen?","opts":["Having the dialysis nurse rewrite the medication list independently","Involving the pharmacist in a shared medication reconciliation with the patient and the team","Asking the patient to decide which medications to continue","Waiting for the nephrologist to review the list at the next scheduled visit"],"ans":1,"expl":"Medication reconciliation is strongest when the pharmacist reviews the complete list with the patient and the care team, which catches duplications, interactions, and renally inappropriate doses. Rewriting the list alone, leaving decisions to the patient, or postponing review until the next visit leaves the patient exposed to avoidable risk in the meantime.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A patient with end-stage kidney disease decides to stop dialysis and asks about comfort-focused care. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Explain that dialysis is the only option that will keep the patient alive and revisit the decision later","Honor the decision, involve the interdisciplinary team including palliative care, and ensure symptom management and support","Ask the family to persuade the patient to continue dialysis","Tell the patient that the decision cannot be made until the physician raises it"],"ans":1,"expl":"A capable patient may decline treatment, and the nurse's role is to support that decision by bringing in palliative care and the interdisciplinary team to manage symptoms and address the family's needs. Pressuring the patient, enlisting the family to change the decision, or requiring the physician to raise the topic first all override the patient's right to decide.","obj":7},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A patient is being evaluated for kidney transplantation and the consent form is presented. Which action reflects the nurse's role in informed consent?","opts":["Obtain the patient's signature on the form and explain the risks of the procedure in detail","Verify that the patient understands the information and is deciding voluntarily, then witness the signature","Decide whether the patient is a suitable candidate to receive a kidney transplant at this time","Sign the consent form on the patient's behalf whenever the patient seems unsure or hesitant"],"ans":1,"expl":"Explaining risks and obtaining consent is the responsibility of the practitioner performing the procedure; the nurse verifies understanding, confirms that the decision is voluntary, and witnesses the signature. Explaining in detail, judging candidacy, or signing for the patient all exceed the nurse's role in the consent process.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A nurse overhears a colleague belittling a patient who missed several treatments. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Ignore the remark, since the patient did not appear to hear it","Address the behavior privately with the colleague and report it if it continues","Complain about the colleague to other staff members during the shift","Tell the patient that the colleague did not mean what was said"],"ans":1,"expl":"Disrespectful behavior toward patients undermines trust and safety, so the nurse addresses it directly and privately first, then uses reporting channels if it persists. Ignoring the remark, discussing it with uninvolved coworkers, or explaining the colleague's words to the patient all leave the behavior unaddressed and the environment unchanged.","obj":7},{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A nurse believes a prescribed dose is unsafe for a patient with a low eGFR. Which action is most appropriate?","opts":["Administer the dose exactly as prescribed because the prescriber is ultimately responsible for the safety of the order","Hold the dose, discuss the concern directly with the prescriber, and escalate through the chain of command if the concern is not resolved","Administer a lower dose on your own and document the rationale for the change clearly in the patient's medical chart","Ask the patient whether they usually take that dose at home before administering the medication exactly as written"],"ans":1,"expl":"The nurse is accountable for safely administering a medication, which means questioning an order that appears unsafe and escalating through the chain of command if needed. Following the order unchanged, adjusting the dose independently, or asking the patient to resolve it all leave the safety concern unaddressed in the right hands.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"An experienced nurse is assigned to precept a new graduate in the dialysis unit. Which approach best supports the new nurse's development?","opts":["Assign the new nurse a full patient load independently in the first week to build confidence","Assess the new nurse's current level of competence and provide gradually increasing responsibility with ongoing feedback","Have the new nurse observe for several weeks before touching any equipment","Restrict the new nurse to machine setup and cleaning until they request more responsibility"],"ans":1,"expl":"Precepting begins with assessment of the learner and then builds responsibility as competence is demonstrated, with feedback that makes progress visible. Overloading a new nurse, extending observation indefinitely, or narrowing the role to tasks leaves development to chance and can hide gaps that later affect patient safety.","obj":7},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient on peritoneal dialysis is not meeting adequacy targets. Which action by the nurse best reflects interdisciplinary collaboration?","opts":["Recommend increasing the dwell volume and then change the dialysis prescription for the patient yourself","Bring the case to the interdisciplinary team so the physician, dietitian, and nurse can review the adequacy data together","Advise the patient to perform an extra exchange every day at home in order to improve their clearance","Wait for the next scheduled routine clinic visit to discuss the adequacy results with the patient"],"ans":1,"expl":"Adequacy depends on the prescription, the patient's adherence and nutrition, and residual kidney function, so reviewing the data together as a team draws on the expertise each discipline contributes. Changing the prescription, adding exchanges, or postponing discussion all exceed the nurse's role or delay a decision that needs several viewpoints.","obj":7},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A dialysis patient has been referred to a transplant center for evaluation. Which action by the nurse best supports continuity of care?","opts":["Assume that the transplant center will request any records and dialysis history that it needs on its own","Confirm the referral was received, send the relevant records and dialysis history, and follow up on the patient's status","Advise the patient to contact the transplant center themselves and then report back to you on the outcome","Wait until the patient asks you about the referral before taking any action to coordinate the transfer"],"ans":1,"expl":"Coordination means the nurse actively verifies that the referral arrived, supplies the information the center needs, and tracks the patient's progress rather than assuming the system will connect on its own. Leaving it to the center, to the patient, or to the patient's initiative creates gaps where referrals are lost and evaluations delayed.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A patient with progressive CKD must choose among hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis, and transplant. Which action by the nurse best supports an informed choice?","opts":["Recommend the modality that has the shortest waiting time so that the patient can begin treatment as soon as possible","Provide balanced information about each option, including the demands and benefits, and support the patient's deliberation with the team","Present only the modality that the nephrologist prefers, in order to avoid confusing the patient with too many options","Defer the whole discussion until the patient requires dialysis urgently and a decision can no longer be delayed"],"ans":1,"expl":"Modality choice belongs to the patient, and the nurse supports it by presenting each option fairly and connecting the patient with the team members who can answer detailed questions. Steering the patient toward one modality, limiting information, or waiting until treatment is urgent deprives the patient of the chance to plan.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient falls in the dialysis unit without injury. Which action by the nurse best supports both the patient and the unit's safety processes?","opts":["Assist the patient, assess for injury, and complete an event report so the circumstances can be reviewed","Document that no injury occurred and take no further action or reporting steps afterward","Move the patient back to the treatment chair quickly so that the dialysis session can continue","Discuss the fall with the other staff only at the next regularly scheduled unit staff meeting"],"ans":0,"expl":"The nurse addresses the patient first, then reports the event so the unit can examine what contributed to the fall, such as staffing, equipment, or environment. Skipping the report, rushing the patient back to treatment, or waiting for a meeting all delay assessment and discard information the unit needs to prevent the next fall.","obj":7},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"A family requests that everything possible be done for a patient with multiorgan failure, while the team believes further aggressive therapy offers no benefit and is causing suffering. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["Support the family's request without any question and continue all of the current aggressive interventions","Advocate for the patient, clarify the goals of care with the team, and involve the ethics committee or palliative care as needed","Tell the family that the team has already decided to stop treatment and that there is nothing further to discuss","Avoid discussing the disagreement about the goals of care with either the family or the rest of the care team"],"ans":1,"expl":"When the family's goals and the team's judgment diverge, the nurse advocates for the patient while bringing the disagreement into a structured process, which includes clarifying goals of care and consulting ethics or palliative care. Unquestioning continuation, announcing a decision the team has not made, or avoiding the conflict leaves the patient without an advocate in the discussion.","obj":7},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A PD program's peritonitis rate has improved after a change in training. Which action by the nurse best supports continued improvement?","opts":["File the results with the program records and return to the previous routine","Share the outcome data with staff and patients and continue monitoring the rate over time","Assume the problem is solved and discontinue tracking the peritonitis rate going forward","Report the improvement only to the medical director and no one else in the program"],"ans":1,"expl":"Improvement holds when results are visible to the people whose practice produced them, so sharing the data and continuing to monitor makes the gain durable and reveals any drift. Filing the results, stopping the measurement, or restricting the information to leadership removes the feedback that sustains the change.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The nurse is confirming that a patient newly started on hemodialysis understands the fluid restriction. Which approach best confirms understanding?","opts":["Ask the patient, 'Do you understand your fluid limit?' and move on when he says yes","Hand him a printed fluid-education handout and note that it was received","Ask him to state in his own words his daily fluid amount and what counts as fluid","Check his recall of fluids at the next month's interdisciplinary meeting"],"ans":2,"expl":"Teach-back - the patient states the plan in his own words - is the standard way to confirm understanding (AHRQ Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit). 'Do you understand?' invites a polite yes, a handout verifies nothing, and waiting weeks forfeits the chance to correct the misunderstanding now.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"An experienced adult patient on in-center hemodialysis tells the nurse he feels the diet lectures 'talk down' to him. Which adult learning principle should the nurse apply when re-planning the teaching?","opts":["Repeat the full curriculum from the start so nothing is missed","Wait until he requests a teaching appointment","Keep the lecture format; adults retain formal structure best","Anchor the teaching to his daily decisions and past experience, with problem-centered discussion"],"ans":3,"expl":"Adults learn best when content is problem-centered, immediately relevant, and built on their accumulated experience (Knowles' andragogy). Repeating the same scripted curriculum ignores his experience, and putting teaching off indefinitely abandons the plan. Anchoring teaching to the decisions he makes daily - fluids, binders, missed sessions - engages the adult learner.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which teaching example pairs the correct learning domain with the correct teaching method?","opts":["Cognitive - the patient washes hands before self-connecting a home PD catheter","Psychomotor - the nurse explains the three fluid compartments in CKD","Psychomotor - the patient performs a return demonstration of fistula self-cannulation","Affective - the patient lists the four food groups highest in potassium"],"ans":2,"expl":"Self-cannulation is a hands-on skill, so it belongs to the psychomotor domain and is taught and verified by return demonstration. Listing foods or explaining concepts are cognitive tasks, and belief or attitude change (such as valuing adherence) is the affective domain. Matching domain to method keeps teaching plans teachable and testable.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A patient with newly diagnosed stage 3b CKD asks the nurse, 'What is my kidney number?' Which teaching response is most accurate per current CKD staging?","opts":["Stage 3b means an eGFR of about 30-44 mL/min/1.73 m2, described best when the albuminuria category is added","Stage 3b means an eGFR of about 15-29 mL/min/1.73 m2","Kidney stage is based on creatinine alone and needs no other measurement","Stage 3b means dialysis will be needed within the year"],"ans":0,"expl":"Stage 3b CKD is an eGFR of 30-44 mL/min/1.73 m2, and current KDIGO staging combines the eGFR category with the albuminuria category (A1-A3) because both drive risk of progression. Stage 4 begins at eGFR 15-29. Staging on creatinine alone is outdated, and dialysis timing cannot be read off a stage.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"In shared decision-making about dialysis modality before kidney failure, which teaching approach is most consistent with current nephrology guidance?","opts":["Steer him toward in-center hemodialysis because supervised care makes it the safest choice","Let the patient pick a modality before the team reviews his medical suitability, to preserve autonomy","Present in-center HD, home HD, PD, and conservative kidney management as options matched to his goals","Tell the patient the team will decide at kidney failure because the data are too complex for patients"],"ans":2,"expl":"Modality education is patient-centered and complete: in-center HD, home HD, PD, and conservative kidney management are all presented, and choice is guided by the patient's goals, home situation, and medical factors. Steering everyone to in-center HD and deciding 'for' the patient both violate shared decision-making, and full autonomy does not mean skipping the team's medical review.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A frail 82-year-old with advanced CKD, multiple comorbidities, and no interest in dialysis asks about alternatives. Which teaching is most accurate?","opts":["Dialysis is legally required for anyone who qualifies, so there are no other options","Palliative care completely replaces kidney-focused care once dialysis is declined","Transplantation is available on request at any age without evaluation","Conservative kidney management treats symptoms, blood pressure, anemia, and advance care planning without dialysis"],"ans":3,"expl":"Conservative kidney management is a recognized alternative in KDIGO guidance: it manages CKD complications and symptoms, supports the patient and family, and includes advance care planning, with hospice or palliative services as needed. Age alone does not bar transplant evaluation, dialysis is never legally required, and kidney palliative care is explicitly designed to combine with ongoing kidney care when dialysis is chosen.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The nurse is teaching a home hemodialysis candidate. Which content is essential to include in the training plan before the patient trains at home?","opts":["Only the vascular access cannulation steps; alarm handling and machine care are the vendor's field-service staff's job","How to phone the unit first for every alarm rather than learning to troubleshoot simple ones at home","Self-cannulation with return demonstration, machine alarms and responses, emergency disconnect, and the water treatment system used with the machine","Nothing formal - home patients learn by doing after the first solo run"],"ans":2,"expl":"Home HD training is structured and competency-based: access self-care with return demonstration, alarm recognition and response, the emergency disconnect procedure, and water treatment used with the system. Home machines still require water meeting treatment standards, and 'figure it out solo' training is unsafe. Vendor support supplements, but never replaces, documented patient competency.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A home PD patient calls the unit to say this morning's drained fluid looks cloudy and he feels abdominal cramping. Which teaching was most essential to prevent a treatment delay?","opts":["Cloudiness overnight is settled dialysate sediment; it only matters when the fluid smells foul and fever develops with it","Cloudy effluent, often with pain, is the classic first sign of peritonitis - call the PD unit the same day and follow instructions, usually bringing the cloudy bag for testing","Run two clear exchanges on your usual schedule and call the unit the next business day only if the cloudiness persists","Peritonitis always causes high fever and vomiting as the first sign, so cramping alone can wait"],"ans":1,"expl":"Cloudy or turbid effluent, frequently with abdominal pain, is the classic first presentation of PD peritonitis in the ISPD 2022 guidance, and patients are taught to report it promptly and bring the cloudy bag for cell count and culture. Waiting for fever, vomiting, or a second day risks treatment delay; fever is a later or absent finding.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which exit-site care teaching matches ISPD 2023 guidance for a healed PD catheter exit site?","opts":["Cleanse with soap and water at least twice weekly and after showers, keep the catheter anchored, and avoid tub baths","Scrub the exit site daily with povidone-iodine ointment on a gauze wrap","Soak the exit site in a tub bath twice a week to soften crusting","Leave the catheter loose so it falls outward without tunnel trauma"],"ans":0,"expl":"ISPD 2023 recommends cleansing a healed exit site at least twice weekly and after every shower or vigorous exercise (1C); many programs cleanse daily, which exceeds the minimum rather than conflicting with it. Use soap and water, shower rather than tub soak until well healed, and keep the catheter anchored (1C). ISPD 2023 also recommends daily topical mupirocin or gentamicin at the exit site to prevent catheter-related infection (1C) where the program adopts it - not the outdated 'no routine ointment' stance.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A PD patient who is otherwise doing well wants to swim in a lake on vacation. Which teaching is most consistent with ISPD exit-site and peritonitis prevention guidance?","opts":["Lakes and hot tubs are acceptable as long as the exit site looks clean and dry afterward","Swimming is permitted only after at least one episode of peritonitis, which builds protective immunity against future contamination","Avoid ponds, lakes, and hot tubs; if swimming, wear a waterproof occlusive dressing, then shower and change it afterward - salt water is lower risk than freshwater","Swim with no dressing at all so the exit site dries fully between swims and stays clean"],"ans":2,"expl":"ISPD-related patient guidance advises avoiding ponds, lakes, rivers, and hot tubs because of high microbial loads; if the patient swims, a waterproof occlusive dressing is worn and the site is cleansed and the dressing changed afterward. Salt water is considered less risky than freshwater. Swimming with no dressing, or trusting a 'clean look,' invites peritonitis.","obj":4},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"During home PD cycler teaching, the machine alarms with a drain failure. Which self-troubleshooting step should the patient be taught to try first?","opts":["Disconnect from the cycler and finish the night manually on the floor","Increase dwell volume to force the fluid out past the obstruction","Restart the cycler from the beginning without inspecting tubing","Check the tubing for kinks, keep the drain bag below the abdomen, and adjust position to help drainage"],"ans":3,"expl":"Drain-failure troubleshooting starts with the mechanical basics: trace every line for kinks, check that the drain bag sits below the abdomen, and change position to promote outflow (ISPD cycler teaching basics). Manual disconnection mid-treatment, blind restarts, or raising dwell volume to override an obstruction are unsafe first steps.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which home-monitoring routine should the nurse teach every patient with an arteriovenous fistula?","opts":["Access assessment is the staff's job; the patient keeps the arm still between visits","Check daily for a thrill by touch over the access and report loss of the vibration or bruit immediately","Check the thrill only on treatment days, since it disappears overnight normally","Take the access arm pulse with a home automatic cuff twice daily"],"ans":1,"expl":"Patients are taught a daily access check - feel the thrill (a vibration over the fistula) and listen for the bruit - and to call immediately if either stops, since abrupt loss signals thrombosis, a time-critical event. Automatic cuff readings on or near the access arm are prohibited. Access assessment continues at home, not only in the chair.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient with a new arteriovenous fistula asks when it will be ready for needles. Which teaching best reflects KDOQI vascular access guidance?","opts":["It can be cannulated on the same day it is created whenever the thrill feels strong at the anastomosis","Needle gauge matters more than maturity, so a 16-gauge needle is fine to use as soon as the arm heals","It is ready once the skin over the arm stops bruising from trial needle sticks","Around 6 weeks: diameter at least 6 mm, flow near 600 mL/min, depth 6 mm or less - the classic 'rule of 6s' benchmark before routine cannulation"],"ans":3,"expl":"The 'rule of 6s' (about 6 weeks; diameter at least 6 mm, depth no more than about 6 mm, flow about 600 mL/min) remains the standard KDOQI teaching benchmark for fistula maturity; KDOQI 2019 adds individualized assessment with selective duplex rather than cannulating on elapsed time. Same-day cannulation is unsafe, and bruising or needle gauge does not determine readiness.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Interdialytic weight gain is commonly targeted in patient teaching. Which goal statement best reflects the usual benchmark?","opts":["Keep interdialytic weight gain at or under about 4% of dry weight, with individual goals set with the team","Gain up to 10% of dry weight as long as blood pressure readings stay normal","Lose weight between treatments to offset the fluid gained","Fluid restriction is unnecessary in anuric patients taking loop diuretics"],"ans":0,"expl":"Common teaching targets interdialytic weight gain at or below roughly 4% of dry weight because larger gains track with hypertension, left ventricular hypertrophy, and intradialytic symptoms; goals are individualized with the team. Large gains are harmful even when office blood pressure looks acceptable, and anuric patients have no diuretic-based way to lose the fluid.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which teaching point explains why missed hemodialysis treatments matter?","opts":["Missed sessions mainly raise cholesterol, which the statin handles between visits","Missed treatments simply lengthen the next session slightly and have no other effect between treatments","Missed sessions let fluid, potassium, and acid build up between treatments, risking hyperkalemia, fluid overload, and hospitalization","Missed treatments only matter for patients who still urinate"],"ans":2,"expl":"Dialysis replaces lost kidney clearance on a schedule, so skipped sessions let fluid, potassium, and acid load build: hyperkalemia and volume overload with dyspnea become the realistic risks, along with hospitalization. Effects are not limited to a longer next run and do not depend on residual urine output.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The unit nurse demonstrates blood-pressure self-monitoring to prepare a patient for home readings. Which teaching is consistent with home blood pressure measurement guidance?","opts":["Measure right after the treatment ends, while walking to the car, for accuracy","Take readings only when symptoms occur, and log them the following week from memory","Use a wrist cuff as the primary device because it is easier to position","Use a validated upper-arm cuff, sit quietly about 5 minutes with feet flat and back supported, and measure at consistent daily times"],"ans":3,"expl":"Validated upper-arm cuff devices, quiet seated rest for about 5 minutes with feet flat and back supported, and consistent daily measurement times are the core home BP teaching points. Wrist cuffs are less reliable, walking-immediately readings and symptom-only readings distort trends, and reconstructing values from memory defeats the log.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient on an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent says he plans to double his dose 'when I feel weak.' Which teaching response is most appropriate?","opts":["That is reasonable; weakness reliably tracks the hemoglobin level, so adjusting to feel makes sense","Unsafe: hemoglobin is checked on schedule and the prescriber changes doses - the target band is narrow, and higher dosing raises risk","Patients may adjust ESAs freely but never iron supplements","Doubling is fine once the hemoglobin is above 13 g/dL"],"ans":1,"expl":"ESA doses are prescribed and adjusted by the prescriber against scheduled hemoglobin checks; KDIGO targets roughly 10-11.5 g/dL and cautions against higher targets because of cardiovascular and thrombotic risk. Symptoms alone do not justify dose changes, and 'above 13' is not a patient-manageable trigger.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"When teaching about phosphate binders, which instruction covers a dose missed mid-meal?","opts":["Take the missed binder now, while food is still being eaten, at the usual dose - never double at the next meal","Skip the missed dose entirely; a late binder has no effect once the snack started","Take a double dose now to make up for the phosphorus at dinner","Wait and take the missed dose at bedtime on an empty stomach, where it works faster"],"ans":0,"expl":"Binders bind dietary phosphorus in the gut, so a dose taken while food is still being eaten does nearly the same work as an on-time dose: take the usual dose now, never double (extra binder cannot remove phosphorus already handled), and avoid empty-stomach dosing, which binds almost nothing. With-meal timing remains the core rule; missed-dose rescue follows the same logic.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A dialysis patient asks why the team checks his hemoglobin, iron studies, and PTH on a regular schedule. Which teaching best explains the purpose?","opts":["The schedule applies only to patients who already have heart failure","The tests exist mainly to document compliance for the dialysis company","Blood counts predict whether the patient will miss treatments","Regular monitoring lets the team catch anemia and mineral bone disease trends and adjust therapy before symptoms develop"],"ans":3,"expl":"Scheduled anemia panels (hemoglobin, iron indices) and mineral-bone panels (calcium, phosphorus, PTH) drive titration of ESAs, iron, vitamin D analogs, binders, and calcimimetics before symptomatic deterioration - proactive management rather than documentation for its own sake, and it applies across the dialysis population.","obj":4},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which teaching about kidney transplant referral timing is most accurate for a patient approaching advanced CKD?","opts":["Referral happens only after dialysis has started and the patient proves reliable attendance","Refer in advanced CKD, before dialysis is needed - evaluation takes months, and earlier referral allows preemptive transplant to be considered","Every patient is automatically placed on the waitlist at diagnosis without an evaluation","Referral is deferred until the patient is over 65, since younger patients receive dialysis first"],"ans":1,"expl":"Transplant referral belongs early in advanced CKD: evaluation takes months, and patients already referred can be considered for preemptive transplant (transplant before or shortly into dialysis), which avoids time on dialysis. Referral is an evaluation process that includes testing and candidacy review - never automatic, and not tied to age or attendance.","obj":4},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A kidney transplant recipient on tacrolimus reports drinking grapefruit juice each morning with the dose. Which teaching is most important?","opts":["Grapefruit interacts with tacrolimus (CYP3A4) and can raise drug levels unpredictably, increasing toxicity; avoid it and choose a different beverage","Grapefruit juice actually improves tacrolimus absorption in a predictable way, so it can safely be continued","Only grapefruit seeds interact with the drug, so pulp-free juice is safe to drink","Use double-strength juice with the dose, since higher juice intake prevents rejection episodes"],"ans":0,"expl":"Grapefruit and related citrus inhibit CYP3A4, raising tacrolimus (and cyclosporine) levels unpredictably - the classic diet-drug interaction in transplant teaching. Toxicity risk rises with levels, so avoidance, not dose juggling, is the correct instruction.","obj":4},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which statement best reflects teaching around post-transplant adherence?","opts":["Adherence demands are heaviest in the first month and taper off once creatinine stabilizes","Immunosuppressants can be stopped once creatinine returns to normal levels, since the graft is then secure","Lifelong consistent daily immunosuppression is required; missed or late doses raise rejection risk, so the regimen anchors to fixed daily routines","Blood levels only matter when the patient begins to feel rejection symptoms such as fever or graft tenderness"],"ans":2,"expl":"Nonadherence to immunosuppression is a leading cause of late rejection and graft loss, so teaching anchors the regimen to fixed routines (same times daily, tied to established habits) for life. Rejection can be clinically silent, normal creatinine does not license dose changes, and adherence demands do not end after month one.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which statement by a nurse best applies the health literacy 'universal precautions' approach in a dialysis unit?","opts":["Profile each patient's literacy first with a formal test, then simplify speech only for those who score low","Substitute English-language video links for any patient whose English is limited, so materials stay consistent","Speak faster during teaching so shortened sessions still cover the schedule","Communicate in plain language with every patient, avoid unexplained jargon, use teach-back routinely, and write materials at about a 5th-6th grade reading level"],"ans":3,"expl":"Universal precautions for health literacy mean assuming anyone may struggle to understand: plain language for all, defined terms instead of jargon, routine teach-back, and materials written around a 5th-6th grade level (AHRQ toolkit approach). Screening-first approaches miss patients and stigmatize, and speed works against comprehension.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A qualified professional interpreter is present for a non-English-speaking patient's predialysis modality teaching. Which approach is correct?","opts":["Speak to the interpreter and watch the interpreter, so cues pass along naturally","Speak to the patient, break teaching into short segments, and finish with teach-back through the interpreter","Use the interpreter only for the consent form; teach the rest with English handouts","Ask the patient's teenage son to double-check the interpreter's translation at the end"],"ans":1,"expl":"Interpreter-mediated teaching technique: talk to the patient rather than the interpreter, chunk content into short segments, and end with teach-back through the interpreter. The technique exists because ad hoc interpreters (especially family, and children especially) omit, editorialize, or soften critical content, and Section 1557/Joint Commission require qualified professional interpreters for significant education.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which approach reflects motivational interviewing when a patient seems ambivalent about home dialysis?","opts":["Confront the ambivalence directly and list the consequences of refusing home training","Tell him his kidney team will be disappointed if he refuses","Provide a printed ultimatum that he choose between home and in-center by Friday","Use open questions, affirm the patient's strengths, reflect his statements back, and elicit his own reasons for considering change"],"ans":3,"expl":"Motivational interviewing works by evoking the patient's own reasons for change: open questions, affirmations, reflective listening, and summaries, in a collaborative, non-judgmental spirit. Confrontation, deadlines, and shame are anti-MI and typically harden ambivalence.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"The nurse is evaluating the effectiveness of home PD teaching. Which evidence best demonstrates that the teaching achieved its outcome?","opts":["The patient thanked the nurse warmly at the end of the session","The teaching checklist is signed by the nurse who delivered the session","She performs three consecutive exchanges with aseptic technique and verbalizes the first sign of peritonitis and what to do","The patient reports feeling generally more confident about kidney disease"],"ans":2,"expl":"Evaluation is measured against the learning outcome: observable correct performance (return demonstration) and recall of the safety-critical content. Politeness, instructor signatures, and vague confidence are not evidence of learning; competencies are demonstrated and verbalized against defined criteria.","obj":4},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which teaching plan best applies the principle of spaced reinforcement for a patient who must remember multiple new self-care skills?","opts":["Chunk teaching into short sessions across several visits, with demonstration and increasing patient practice each time","Teach every skill in one 90-minute session the day before discharge home","Repeat each skill only when the patient makes an error","Limit teaching to handouts so the patient can reread them endlessly"],"ans":0,"expl":"Short, repeated, increasingly hands-on sessions (spaced learning with demonstration and guided practice) outperform single long sessions for retention and skill acquisition. Self-correcting only on error and print-only teaching offer too few reinforcement cycles for multiple new skills.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"In an emergency kit teaching plan for home dialysis patients, which content aligns with national emergency preparedness guidance?","opts":["Nothing - home units guarantee truck delivery of supplies within hours of any disaster","A one-week supply of phosphate binders only, since diet is optional in emergencies","A 3-day emergency kidney-diet supply, a 3-day medication supply, contact information for the home unit and backup facility, and emergency identification","A written commitment that the patient will present to the emergency department for dialysis during any declared disaster"],"ans":2,"expl":"Emergency preparedness teaching (Kidney Community Emergency Response framework) covers a 3-day emergency diet and medication supply, treatment and contact information for the home and backup facility, and personal emergency identification. Emergency kits are broader than binders, supply delivery is not guaranteed in disasters, and the ED is not a routine substitute for the planned backup pathway.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient on home PD uses a caregiver to help with exchanges. Which approach best supports safe caregiver involvement?","opts":["Train the caregiver informally during a routine visit, with no documentation, so the patient retains full independence","Have the caregiver watch a recorded training video and start helping the same evening","Discourage all caregiver involvement because PD safety is a physician responsibility","Formally train the caregiver to the same competency standard, document it, reassess periodically, and check caregiver burden"],"ans":3,"expl":"Caregivers who perform PD tasks deserve the same formal training, documented competency validation, and periodic reassessment as patients - and caregiver burden should be assessed, since overload predicts breakdown of the home plan. Informal or video-only training leaves safety-critical technique unverified.","obj":4},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which statement best describes peer mentoring as a kidney education strategy?","opts":["Trained patient mentors with lived experience complement the team, improving engagement and modality awareness, while clinical questions route to clinical staff","Peer mentors replace the interdisciplinary team in modality education, handling medical questions themselves","Peer mentoring has no supporting evidence behind it, so programs should avoid it entirely","Peer mentors must be certified nephrology nurses to participate, which is why few programs run one"],"ans":0,"expl":"Peer mentoring programs in dialysis are associated with better engagement, adjustment, and awareness of home modalities; they complement - not replace - clinical education, and mentors are trained patients, not nephrology nurses. Medical questions route back to clinical staff.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The nurse notices a patient's understanding of his dry weight is inaccurate, so the weight history conversation is repeated with a visual graph of his target weight. Which teaching principle does this illustrate?","opts":["Teaching is finished once the information has been delivered once","Teaching is a cycle - assess understanding, intervene, re-evaluate - and visual aids support patients who process numbers poorly","Dry weight is a nursing calculation that patients never need to understand","Evaluation of teaching requires the medical director's sign-off before the plan closes"],"ans":1,"expl":"The teaching process is cyclical: assess what the patient actually understands, intervene (here adding a visual), and re-evaluate. 'Delivered once' ignores comprehension gaps, dry weight is central to patient self-management between treatments, and closing a teaching plan is a nursing responsibility, not a physician sign-off ritual.","obj":4},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"During a shift-to-shift handoff in the dialysis unit, which statement best fills the Recommendation element of the SBAR for a patient with a 4.1 kg interdialytic weight gain above his individualized target and blood pressure trending upward?","opts":["I recommend we hold ultrafiltration beyond the last 2 L, notify the nephrologist, and reassess the blood pressure before adjusting further","This is chair 3; the patient has a 4.1 kg interdialytic weight gain today","The patient seems anxious about his weight gain again this week","His history includes heart failure and hypertension for which he takes metoprolol daily"],"ans":0,"expl":"SBAR = Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation. The Recommendation element states what the speaker wants done next - here a hold on ultrafiltration beyond a defined amount, provider notification, and reassessment before proceeding. The chair and weight figure are Situation content, the anxiety quote is Assessment, and the medical history is Background.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Per the CMS ESRD Conditions for Coverage, when must a new in-center hemodialysis patient's initial interdisciplinary plan of care be completed?","opts":["Within 90 days of the first treatment","Only after the patient has stabilized for 6 months","Within 30 days or the patient's 13th outpatient hemodialysis session, whichever is later","Before the first treatment is ever delivered"],"ans":2,"expl":"CMS (42 CFR 494) requires the interdisciplinary team to complete and sign the initial comprehensive assessment and plan of care within 30 days or 13 outpatient HD sessions, whichever is LATER. Requiring it before entry is not the rule, and waiting months leaves the plan of care unaddressed well past the required window.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which statement best reflects the CMS-required interdisciplinary plan-of-care review?","opts":["The nephrologist reviews the plan alone and posts the signed summary for the rest of the team","The full interdisciplinary team reviews measurable goals with the patient's input - monthly when unstable, at least annually when stable","Goals are written by the physician alone so the team can follow them without further discussion","The plan of care is reviewed only when the patient or family files a complaint about care"],"ans":1,"expl":"Under 42 CFR 494.80(d)/494.90(b), the interdisciplinary team reviews the comprehensive plan of care monthly for unstable patients and at least annually for stable patients, with measurable goals and the patient's (or representative's) input as desired. Physician-only goal setting, complaint-triggered-only review, and indefinite delays all fail the requirement.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The RN is assigning a patient care technician to take weights and blood pressures at chairside. Which delegation practice is correct per national guidance?","opts":["Delegate clinical judgment tasks to the PCT to speed up morning setup","Delegate the task and stop supervising, since the PCT is competent and the RN is free for paperwork","Assign the PCT to independently assess shortness of breath and alter the ultrafiltration goal accordingly","Delegate the task, provide specific direction, and retain accountability for the delegation decision and supervision"],"ans":3,"expl":"National delegation guidance (NCSBN/ANA) applies five rights: right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction/communication, and right supervision with evaluation. The RN stays accountable for the delegation decision and supervision. Independent assessment and UF-goal changes remain RN or provider level, and delegation does not end supervision.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"After a wrong-dose medication event is investigated, which systemic change best demonstrates a quality-improvement (rather than blame-focused) response?","opts":["Issue a memo reminding all nurses to double-check doses more carefully","Transfer the nurse involved without any process review","Implement barcoded medication scanning and an independent double-check for high-risk doses, then track the error trend on a unit run chart","Retrain the individual nurse on the dosage calculation and consider the event closed"],"ans":2,"expl":"A just-culture QI response targets the system that allowed the error - barcode scanning, independent double-checks for high-alert doses, trend tracking - rather than shaming individuals. Memos and individual-only retraining do not change system reliability, and both discourage future reporting.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which action best reflects a non-punitive safety culture in a dialysis facility?","opts":["Only events that harm a patient are reported; near-misses stay informal","Send every report directly to the state board without any facility-level review or feedback","Discipline first and investigate second, so staff understand that accountability comes before analysis","Staff are encouraged to report near-misses promptly, the information is analyzed for system causes, and no one is disciplined for honest error"],"ans":3,"expl":"Non-punitive (just) cultures rely on prompt near-miss reporting, system-focused analysis, and no discipline for honest reporting, which is what generates the data that prevent future harm events. Ignoring near-misses or punishing reporters suppresses exactly the information safety depends on.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A facility wants to improve its phosphate binder adherence rate. Which PDSA sequence is correct?","opts":["Plan a small test of change on one hallway of patients, carry it out, study the results, then act to adopt, adjust, or abandon before wider spread","Adopt the change facility-wide first, then plan out how it will be measured going forward","Do the study only after acting, so the data reflect final success rather than process","Skip the small test because a single-unit pilot wastes time the deadline does not allow"],"ans":0,"expl":"PDSA means Plan (define the change and measures), Do (small-scale pilot), Study (analyze results against predictions), Act (adopt, adapt, or abandon before spreading). Small-scale testing first contains risk, and acting facility-wide before any measurement defeats cycle learning.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which aim statement for a fluid-management improvement project is best formed?","opts":["We will improve fluid management soon","Staff will do a better job teaching patients about fluids starting next quarter","Cut the proportion of treatments with IDWG over 4% of estimated dry weight from 38% to 25% within 6 months","The dietitian will try to remind patients about salt as much as feasible"],"ans":2,"expl":"A well-formed QI aim is specific, measurable, time-bound, and population-defined: baseline rate, target rate, metric definition, and time frame. Vague verbs ('improve soon,' 'better job,' 'try to') cannot be tracked or evaluated in PDSA cycles.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"On a unit run chart tracking intradialytic hypotension episodes per week, which pattern signals special-cause variation needing investigation?","opts":["Points bounce randomly above and below the median","Only four consecutive points fall on one side of the median","One isolated point equals the long-run average","Eight consecutive points fall on one side of the median"],"ans":3,"expl":"Per IHI run-chart rules, a shift of 6 or more consecutive points on one side of the median signals special cause (the threshold becomes 8 only on long charts of roughly 20+ points), as does a trend of about 5 consecutive rising or falling points (6 with ≥20 points). Random scatter, single points near the average, and stretches shorter than 6 consecutive points are common-cause variation.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The team has 12 candidate causes of delayed treatment starts and wants to prioritize improvement efforts. Which tool best fits?","opts":["Fishbone diagram listing all causes in a fresh diagram","Pareto chart of the frequency of each cause, to target the vital few that drive most delays","A single-focus PDSA with no data analysis","A new policy memo banning delays"],"ans":1,"expl":"A Pareto chart ranks causes by frequency and highlights the small set of causes ('vital few') that account for most of the problem, matching the 80/20 prioritization principle. Fishbone mapping supports brainstorming causes but does not prioritize them; skipping data analysis and memo bans do not target effort effectively.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"During a root-cause analysis of repeated treatment delays, which use of a fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram is correct?","opts":["Organize candidate causes into categories - people, process, equipment, environment, information - to prompt systematic cause exploration","Rank candidate causes by the cost to fix each, from most to least expensive","Document only the final corrective action plan once leadership approves it","Track the number of delays over time on a run chart separate from the analysis"],"ans":0,"expl":"The fishbone diagram organizes brainstormed candidate causes into categories (commonly people, process, equipment, environment, information/materials) to drive systematic root-cause exploration. Cost ranking, action-plan documentation, and time tracking are other phases or different tools, not the fishbone's job.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which QI tool choice is most appropriate for proactively analyzing the medication administration process in hemodialysis before an error occurs?","opts":["A Pareto chart of past errors","A root-cause analysis conducted after a sentinel harm event","Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA), scoring potential failure points for severity, occurrence, and detectability","A run chart of past medication doses"],"ans":2,"expl":"FMEA is the proactive risk tool: it maps a process, anticipates failure modes, and scores severity, occurrence, and detectability (risk priority number) to target redesign before harm. RCA is reactive post-event analysis, and Pareto or run charts analyze data that already accumulated.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"In the Donabedian quality framework, which classification pairs each measure with the correct category?","opts":["Hand hygiene performed per protocol = process; CRBSI rate = outcome; nurse staffing level = structure","Arteriovenous fistula use rate = structure; nurse-to-patient staffing ratio = outcome; hand hygiene performed per protocol = process","CRBSI rate = process; hand hygiene = outcome; staffing = structure","All three measure types are outcomes because they all describe the patient"],"ans":0,"expl":"Donabedian's model separates structure (resources and organization - staffing levels), process (what is done - hand hygiene performed per protocol), and outcome (results - CRBSI rate). The other options swap categories and conflate inputs, actions, and results.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which practice matches the CDC infection prevention recommendations for hemodialysis catheter connection and disconnection?","opts":["Skip masks at connection and disconnection whenever the patient has no recent infection history and the hub looks clean","Scrub the catheter hubs with antiseptic at every access, wear masks for nurse and patient at connect and disconnect, and use antiseptic-impregnated caps where used","Scrub the hubs only when visible blood is present on the cap, since clean caps carry no risk","Wear the same gloves for the full shift to maintain a continuous sterile field at each chair"],"ans":1,"expl":"Core CDC dialysis-safety practice: hand hygiene and glove change before access procedures, thorough antiseptic scrubbing of catheter hubs at every access, masks for staff and patient during connection/disconnection, and antiseptic-impregnated caps where used. Occasion-based scrubbing ('only if bloody') and single-shift glove use violate the bundle.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which hand hygiene moment is most essential per infection prevention recommendations for dialysis care?","opts":["Hand hygiene is optional whenever gloves are worn for the whole contact","Rely on glove use alone between patients, since hands do not touch skin through gloves","Clean hands only once per shift, before lunch","Perform hand hygiene before and after every patient contact and after glove removal"],"ans":3,"expl":"Hand hygiene is performed before and after each patient contact and after glove removal; gloves reduce contamination but do not replace hand hygiene. Glove reuse or substitution across contacts transmits organisms between chair stations.","obj":7},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A facility's PD peritonitis rate is being discussed at QAPI. Which analytic approach best supports honest internal review?","opts":["Report only episodes confirmed by culture, excluding all clinical diagnoses, to lower the rate on paper","Wait until the rate doubles, then consider reporting it","Compare the facility rate with benchmarks and its own prior year, review organism profiles and technique-related events, and set an achievable target","Change the rate denominator mid-year to show immediate improvement"],"ans":2,"expl":"Sound QAPI analytics compare internal trend with external benchmarks, drill into organisms and technique-related events, and set realistic targets. Excluding clinical diagnoses and shifting denominators inflate apparent performance and misdirect the team - the opposite of improvement analytics.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A nephrologist repeatedly ignores a unit protocol the RN believes is unsafe for access flow monitoring. Which escalation fits the professional chain of command?","opts":["Document the concern, raise it directly and respectfully with the nephrologist, and if unresolved, escalate per policy to the nurse leader or medical director","Stop raising the concern to avoid damaging the working relationship with the physician","Work around the protocol quietly, documenting everything for a potential later report","Post the physician's prescribing pattern on the unit's public board, since transparency improves safety"],"ans":0,"expl":"The chain of command starts with direct, respectful, documented communication with the decision-maker, then policy-based escalation to leadership (nurse manager, medical director) if unresolved. Working around the protocol is a cover-up pattern, not documentation practice; silence and public shaming substitute professional risk for resolution.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which entry best reflects correct documentation practice after a completed treatment note was found missing an element discovered an hour later?","opts":["Backdate a new note to the original treatment time so the record looks complete","Delete the original treatment record entry and rewrite it fully with the missing element included","White-out the original signature and initial over it to show the correction was authorized","Add a late entry labeled with the actual date and time it is being written, referencing the original entry, with objective language"],"ans":3,"expl":"Late entries are labeled as such - dated and timed for when they are actually written and cross-referenced to the original note - with objective, factual language. Backdating, obliterations, and record deletion are documentation falsification risks. Never pre-chart care that has not occurred.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient tells the charge nurse he wants to file a grievance about his treatment assignment. Which response matches CMS patient-rights expectations?","opts":["Tell him grievances can be filed only after he transfers to another facility or changes shifts","Advise him that grievances are handled only by the insurer that covers his employer","Acknowledge the concern, provide the facility's grievance process in writing, document the complaint, and route it promptly through the required channel","Refuse to process any grievance that names a staff member until that member is reassigned"],"ans":2,"expl":"CMS patient rights require that patients be informed of facility policies and the grievance process, including whom to contact; grievances are documented and addressed promptly. Denying, delaying, or conditioning grievances on other actions violates patient rights, including protection from retaliation.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient reports muscle cramps and confusion about which high-potassium foods to avoid. Which interdisciplinary referral is most appropriate?","opts":["Renal dietitian for individualized potassium education and meal planning","Social worker for grief counseling only","Pharmacist, since cramps are always a medication side effect","Nephrologist only, with no additional referrals at this level"],"ans":0,"expl":"Individualized food-choice teaching around potassium (and sodium/phosphorus) is squarely the renal dietitian's role in the interdisciplinary team. The social worker addresses psychosocial and access issues; assuming a medication cause or skipping referrals misses the correct discipline.","obj":7},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A waitlisted transplant candidate is hospitalized with dialysis access infection and IV antibiotics. Which coordination action is most appropriate for the dialysis RN?","opts":["None - the transplant center learns of hospitalizations when the patient calls them","Tell the patient the waitlist is now void and that re-listing takes several years of new evaluation","Update the chart and assume the nephrologist will notify the transplant center when reviewing labs","Notify the transplant center per its protocol of the hospitalization and antimicrobial therapy, so waitlist status and candidacy can be reviewed"],"ans":3,"expl":"Waitlist status is actively maintained: significant clinical events, hospitalizations, and antimicrobial courses are communicated to the transplant center per its protocol, which allows candidacy or status review. Assuming passive notification and telling the patient their listing is void are both wrong.","obj":7},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In the CVICU, a CRRT prescription's anticoagulation rate looks inconsistent with the patient's current coagulation values. Which collaborative action is most appropriate for the nephrology RN?","opts":["Clarify the order with the prescribing provider before running therapy, documenting the clarification for the record","Adjust the anticoagulant rate independently downward to the patient's appearance","Proceed with the last day's rate, assuming it was correct","Ask another nurse to choose the rate instead"],"ans":0,"expl":"Ambiguous or potentially inconsistent CRRT orders are clarified with the prescriber before therapy runs, with documentation of the clarified order. Independently adjusting anticoagulation based on appearance, carrying over yesterday's rate, or deferring the choice to a peer all bypass the required verification step in high-acuity therapy.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which statement best describes the medical director's QAPI accountability in a dialysis facility?","opts":["The medical director delegates all QAPI accountability to unit technicians, who do most audits anyway","The medical director ensures QAPI addresses clinical outcomes and patient-safety data, participates in ongoing review, and is accountable facility-wide","QAPI applies exclusively to nursing documentation audits reviewed by the charge nurse","The social worker chairs QAPI so the patient voice is formally represented"],"ans":1,"expl":"Under CMS, the medical director ensures the facility maintains an ongoing QAPI program covering clinical outcomes and patient safety data, participates in its review, and the interdisciplinary team carries it out facility-wide. Assigning it wholly to technicians or social work, or narrowing it to documentation audits, misstates the requirement.","obj":7},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which elements best reflect an ESRD facility's emergency preparedness plan under CMS expectations?","opts":["A plan that covers staff evacuation only, leaving patient treatment continuity to be handled ad hoc","A plan consisting of the phone number for the fire department, posted by each chair and refreshed yearly","A plan covering staff roles, patient emergency information, backup water and power, drills, and coordination with local emergency management and the ESRD Network","No formal plan is needed if patients sign a statement that they will manage themselves at home"],"ans":2,"expl":"CMS emergency preparedness expectations include an all-hazards emergency plan with patient emergency information tracking, staff roles, arrangements for backup water and power, drills, and coordination with local emergency management and the ESRD Network (KCER resources support this). Staff-evacuation-only plans, fire-department numbers alone, and 'self-management' waivers fall short.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Mid-morning, an RN finds one station's machine alarming with the line visibly bleeding at the connection. Another two patients have routine complaints. Which patient does the RN address first?","opts":["The patient with the loudest complaints to protect the unit's satisfaction scores","The patient with active access bleeding at the needle site, then the others in clinical-risk order","The two patients with routine complaints while waiting for a PCT to address the bleeding","Whichever patient was at the top of the RN's morning schedule list"],"ans":1,"expl":"Safety triage prioritizes the immediate-harm situation - active bleeding from the access - before routine complaints, with the rest addressed in clinical risk order. Satisfaction, convenience, and fixed rosters do not govern clinical prioritization; the RN addresses the bleeding first and ensures the others are not left waiting unsafely.","obj":7},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which practice best builds staff engagement in a unit's quality work?","opts":["Reserve QAPI discussions for the medical director's private files until findings are finalized","Discuss quality results annually at the leadership offsite meeting, where the board reviews trends","Assign improvement work only to new graduate nurses as a structured learning task for their development","Share run charts at staff huddles, invite frontline ideas for PDSA cycles, and post improvement results where staff and patients see them"],"ans":3,"expl":"Frontline engagement in quality work happens through visible data (run charts), staff huddles that invite improvement ideas, and closing the loop by sharing results. Sealed files, annual-only reviews, and assigning QI solely to new staff disconnect the people closest to the work from the improvement process.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A 66-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and CKD stage G4 (eGFR 24 mL/min/1.73 m²), albuminuria 900 mg/g, is on maximally tolerated lisinopril. Per current KDIGO recommendations, which addition is most appropriate to slow kidney disease progression?","opts":["Start dapagliflozin 10 mg daily","Switch lisinopril to losartan","Add insulin glargine and restrict fluids","Refer for permanent hemodialysis catheter placement"],"ans":0,"expl":"SGLT2 inhibitors are recommended for adults with CKD and eGFR ≥20 mL/min with albuminuria (KDIGO 2024 CKD guideline, drawing on DAPA-CKD and EMPA-KIDNEY), and they preserve kidney function independently of glycemia. Switching between ACE blocker and ARB adds no benefit and raises risk; fluid restriction does not treat albuminuric progression; catheter placement is premature at eGFR 24 without a decision to start dialysis.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A 58-year-old woman with diabetic CKD stage G3b (eGFR 38 mL/min/1.73 m²), urine albumin-creatinine ratio 700 mg/g, and serum potassium 4.6 mEq/L is already on an SGLT2 inhibitor and maximally tolerated RAS blockade. Which medication addition best fits current evidence for cardiorenal protection?","opts":["Over-the-counter ibuprofen scheduled doses","Methyldopa","Finerenone with scheduled potassium monitoring","Doxazosin"],"ans":2,"expl":"Finerenone (a nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist) reduced kidney failure and cardiovascular events in FIDELIO-DKD and FIGARO-DKD in patients with diabetic CKD on maximally tolerated RAS blockade; KDIGO 2024 recommends considering it, with potassium checks after initiation and with dose changes (start eGFR ≥25, potassium ≤5.0 mEq/L). Ibuprofen accelerates CKD progression; neither alpha-blockers nor methyldopa provide this cardiorenal protection.","obj":2},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Per KDIGO lipid management recommendations, cardiovascular risk reduction with a statin should be prescribed for which patient?","opts":["A 55-year-old who started maintenance dialysis last month, not previously on a statin","A 62-year-old with CKD G3a not on dialysis","A 39-year-old dialysis patient with LDL 120 mg/dL","A 70-year-old dialysis patient in whom treatment with a statin should now be STARTED"],"ans":1,"expl":"KDIGO lipid guidelines recommend statin treatment (or statin/ezetimibe) for adults 50 years or older with CKD not on dialysis. In contrast, statins are NOT initiated in dialysis patients because trials (4D, AURORA) showed no cardiovascular benefit at that stage; if a dialysis patient already takes a statin on entry to dialysis, it continues.","obj":2},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A maintenance hemodialysis patient has hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL, transferrin saturation 15%, and ferritin 180 ng/mL. Per KDIGO anemia recommendations, what is the most appropriate next step?","opts":["Stop all anemia monitoring because the patient is not yet transfusion-dependent","Start an ESA immediately without iron assessment","Target hemoglobin 13 g/dL with rapid ESA dose escalation","A trial of intravenous iron before starting an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent"],"ans":3,"expl":"KDIGO suggests a trial of IV iron in dialysis patients when a rise in hemoglobin is desired and iron stores are low (TSAT ≤30% and ferritin ≤500 ng/mL) — ESA response is blunted with absolute iron deficiency. The anemia target is the lowest hemoglobin that avoids transfusion, generally not above 11.5 g/dL, not 13. Iron status must always precede ESA decisions.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"An ICU patient with acute kidney injury has a creatinine rising from 1.2 to 3.4 mg/dL over five days and urine output 800 mL/day, with stable potassium, pH, and volume status. Per KDIGO, when should kidney replacement therapy be initiated?","opts":["When urgent indications develop, such as refractory volume overload, hyperkalemia, acidemia, or uremic complications","As soon as the BUN exceeds 60 mg/dL","On the first day of oliguria to prevent fluid overload","Never — once eGFR drops below 25, RRT is automatic"],"ans":0,"expl":"KDIGO AKI guidance initiates RRT emergently for refractory fluid overload, hyperkalemia, acidemia, uremic pericarditis/encephalopathy, or certain intoxications. Neither BUN thresholds nor creatinine values alone trigger RRT, since supportive care may allow recovery; a low eGFR by itself does not mandate initiation.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"A patient on continuous kidney replacement therapy with regional citrate anticoagulation has the following checks due. Which monitoring strategy matches citrate-based anticoagulation requirements?","opts":["Total serum calcium alone every morning","Activated partial thromboplastin time every six hours","Ionized calcium in the circuit (post-filter) and systemically, with calcium infusion titrated to systemic ionized calcium","Platelet count twice daily to titrate the citrate rate"],"ans":2,"expl":"Citrate chelates ionized calcium, so the circuit anticoagulation is judged by post-filter ionized calcium while the systemic level reflects metabolism and the calcium infusion — both are followed (commonly every 6 hours, with the citrate:blood flow ratio and calcium substitution titrated). aPTT applies to heparin regimens; total calcium alone can be deceptively normal in chelation states; platelets do not titrate citrate.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"A 61-year-old man with cirrhosis develops hepatorenal syndrome-AKI: creatinine 2.8 mg/dL, no response to two days of albumin 1 g/kg daily, urine studies consistent with HRS-AKI, no shock. Which intervention is the evidence-based first-line medical treatment?","opts":["High-dose furosemide drip","Terlipressin with continued albumin","Low-dose dopamine infusion","Immediate dialysis catheter placement as definitive treatment"],"ans":1,"expl":"HRS-AKI treatment is a vasoconstrictor plus albumin; terlipressin plus albumin (CONFIRM trial) is the FDA-approved first-line regimen and improved reversal of HRS-AKI. Diuretics including furosemide are stopped in HRS-AKI; low-dose dopamine lacks benefit; dialysis is supportive for those not responding or awaiting transplant, not the medical therapy of HRS-AKI.","obj":2},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD stage G4 patient has serum phosphorus 6.2 mg/dL, albumin-corrected calcium 10.6 mg/dL, and PTH 420 pg/mL. Per the KDIGO CKD-MBD update, which plan is most appropriate?","opts":["Order a parathyroidectomy referral now","Increase a calcium-based binder (calcium acetate) to three tablets with meals","Add active vitamin D analog first regardless of calcium level","Dietary phosphate education plus a non-calcium-based phosphate binder"],"ans":3,"expl":"With hyperphosphatemia AND hypercalcemia, KDIGO 2017 update logic favors limiting calcium-based binder dose and choosing a non-calcium-based binder (e.g., sevelamer), with dietary phosphate counseling. Escalating calcium load worsens hypercalcemia; active vitamin D raises both calcium and phosphorus (used cautiously, typically per PTH trend with calcium watched); parathyroidectomy follows failure of medical therapy in severe refractory disease.","obj":2},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A dialysis patient's intact PTH remains persistently above nine times the upper normal limit with calcium 9.4 mg/dL and phosphorus 5.1 mg/dL despite binder optimization. What intervention aligns with the KDIGO CKD-MBD update approach?","opts":["A calcimimetic (cinacalcet) with close monitoring of serum calcium","Routine daily Vitamin D 50,000 IU","A calcium-based binder increase","Bisphosphonate therapy weekly"],"ans":0,"expl":"KDIGO suggests calcimimetics for dialysis patients with persistently elevated PTH (with calcium and phosphorus monitored, since hypocalcemia is the dose-limiting effect). Megadose vitamin D is inappropriate; adding calcium-based binder raises calcium load further; bisphosphonates address bone turnover phenotypes with low turnover excluded first, not PTH suppression.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient with refractory volume overload needs dialysis urgently, and the team chooses urgent-start peritoneal dialysis after catheter placement. Per ISPD urgent-start practice recommendations, how should PD be performed in the first days?","opts":["Intermittent peritoneal lavage every 12 hours for one week before starting PD","Full-volume 2-liter exchanges immediately after recovery from anesthesia","Low-volume, gradual dwell fills with incremental increase over the first days, using close monitoring for leak or malposition","Avoid any exchange for 14 days to guarantee healing"],"ans":2,"expl":"ISPD urgent-start PD guidance uses low-dwell-volume exchanges begun within days of catheter placement, scaled up gradually to reduce leak risk and preserve residually tolerable comfort; full 2 L fills immediately raise leak and discomfort risk, a lavage-only course is not standard, and total immobilization for two weeks forfeits the benefit of urgent-start PD.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A new arteriovenous fistula has been in place four weeks with adequate thrill and pulse augmentation; the access nurse evaluates first cannulation. Which cannulation strategy fits KDOQI vascular access recommendations?","opts":["Buttonhole technique at two fixed sites from the very first cannulation for every patient","Rope-ladder site rotation, beginning after the fistula is mature (typically with good flow and physical maturity criteria), by an experienced cannulator","Cannulate as close to the anastomosis as possible for the largest segment","Use 15-gauge needles immediately to maximize flow assessment"],"ans":1,"expl":"KDOQI 2019 vascular access guidance favors rope-ladder rotation to preserve the entire access length and reserves buttonhole Technique for select patients with well-defined needs (experienced cannulators, good anatomy), not universal first-line use. Cannulating immediately near the anastomosis risks aneurysm/inflow injury and large-gauge first sticks raise infiltration risk.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A 34-year-old kidney transplant recipient with eGFR 55 mL/min/1.73 m² takes tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) 300 mg daily for HIV. Creatinine has risen from 0.9 to 1.4 mg/dL with subclinical proteinuria over six months. What does current guidance support as the intervention?","opts":["Add nephrotoxic immunosuppression adjustments without an HIV consult","Continue TDF at half the dose with monitoring","Stop all antiretroviral therapy immediately","Switch to tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) after coordination with the HIV team"],"ans":3,"expl":"TDF carries proximal tubulopathy risk; DHHS OI guidelines support switching to the less nephrotoxic prodrug TAF when kidney toxicity signals appear, done with the HIV team to maintain suppression. Arbitrary half-dosing risks resistance, stopping ART abruptly is unsafe, and further nephrotoxic immunosuppression changes compound the injury.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A 60-year-old with bipolar disorder has lithium-associated CKD stage G3a (eGFR 54 mL/min/1.73 m²), stable creatinine. The psychiatrist asks how to proceed with the lithium. Which response best fits current nephrology practice?","opts":["Coordinate a gradual taper or transition to an alternative mood stabilizer rather than stopping abruptly","Stop lithium immediately today","Continue current dosing unchanged and recheck in five years","Add an NSAID to control musculoskeletal pain, which will also protect the kidney"],"ans":0,"expl":"Abrupt lithium cessation risks psychiatric relapse; the standard approach is coordinated, gradual tapering or transition (e.g., to valproate/lamotrigine) with kidney-function trends monitored. Continuing unchanged ignores nephrotoxicity signals, and NSAIDs worsen kidney perfusion (especially with CKD), the opposite of protection.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","q":"A maintenance hemodialysis patient's pre-dialysis serum bicarbonate has run 15-17 mEq/L for four weeks (target ≥22 per KDOQI/ISPD benchmarks). Which intervention is most appropriate first line?","opts":["Add an acetate-containing bath","Prescribe double-dose oral sodium bicarbonate tablets in addition without changing dialysate","Increase the dialysate bicarbonate concentration within the prescribed range and reassess","Increase ultrafiltration to clear excess acid"],"ans":2,"expl":"Persistent metabolic acidosis in hemodialysis is corrected by raising dialysate bicarbonate (within AAMI-safe and individualized prescription limits), since dialysate is the main alkalinization source. Stacked oral bicarbonate adds sodium load and is adjunctive at best; acetate baths are obsolete; ultrafiltration removes fluid, not fixed acid, and can worsen perfusion.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A PD patient presents with cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, WBC 400/µL with 60% neutrophils. The team is choosing empiric antibiotic coverage and route per ISPD 2022 peritonitis guidance. Which choice is correct?","opts":["Intravenous vancomycin only","Intraperitoneal gram-positive coverage (first-generation cephalosporin or vancomycin) PLUS gram-negative coverage (third-generation cephalosporin or aminoglycoside)","Oral ciprofloxacin alone as full empiric coverage","Topical antibiotic ointment at the exit site"],"ans":1,"expl":"ISPD 2022 recommends empiric, gram-positive-and-gram-negative coverage, with the intraperitoneal route preferred over IV for routine peritonitis treatment. Single-agent IV vancomycin omits gram-negative organisms; oral monotherapy is insufficient for empiric treatment of active peritonitis (oral is used for some follow-on therapy with certain agents like fluoroquinolones only after sensitivity data); topical agents are for exit-site prophylaxis, not peritonitis.","obj":2},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"After finishing a 20-minute teaching session about fluid restrictions, the nurse asks the patient to explain, in her own words, how she will limit fluids tomorrow. This best demonstrates which principle?","opts":["Teach-back evaluates whether the teaching worked and where to re-teach, not the patient's intelligence","Teach-back tests the patient's reading comprehension level","Teach-back shifts all responsibility for learning onto the patient","Teach-back documents the session for billing"],"ans":0,"expl":"Teach-back (AHRQ Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit) is an evaluation method: the patient restates the plan in her own words, which reveals what actually landed — if she cannot restate it, the nurse re-teaches differently, rather than blaming the learner. It is explicitly NOT a comprehension or intelligence test, responsibility stays shared, and while the encounter can be documented, documentation is not its purpose.","obj":4},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A clinic adopts health literacy universal precautions for all CKD education. Which practice matches that model?","opts":["Use technical terminology with patients wearing business clothes since they are likely educated","Screen literacy scores only for patients who look uncertain","Use plain language, chunked content, and confirm understanding with every patient, not just those flagged as low literacy","Reserve simplified materials for the elderly only"],"ans":2,"expl":"Universal precautions means EVERYONE receives clear, plain-language, chunked communication with confirmation — because literacy cannot be reliably judged by appearance, age, or education, and shame around asking for clarity is common. Screening only the visibly uncertain, dressing-based assumptions, and age-based simplification all miss patients who will quietly misunderstand.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A Spanish-speaking patient with limited English proficiency needs a detailed home-dialysis training session. Per Section 1557 and CMS expectations, who should provide interpretation?","opts":["The patient's teenage granddaughter who is fluent","A qualified medical interpreter, in person or by video/phone, arranged by the facility at no cost to the patient","A bilingual unit clerk when available between tasks","Written English handouts with a translation app"],"ans":1,"expl":"Section 1557 (and CMS Conditions for Coverage) require qualified interpreters — competent in medical terminology and confidentiality-bound — provided free for significant health interactions. Family (especially minors) may misinterpret, omit, or filter critical content and cannot substitute; untrained bilingual staff lack medical-interpretation competence; machine translation of clinical teaching is unreliable and unaccountable.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"When producing a written handout about fluid and sodium limits for a mixed-literacy dialysis population, which readability and design target is best supported?","opts":["Narrative paragraphs with dense multi-clause sentences to fit more content","Twelfth-grade level for precision","College level for accuracy","Fifth- to sixth-grade reading level with short sentences, white space, and a single key message per section"],"ans":3,"expl":"Adult health-education materials are recommended at about a 5th-6th grade reading level with supportive design, per AHRQ/CDC plain-language standards — this maximizes usability across mixed-literacy populations without dumbing down clinical intent. High reading levels, however precise, correlate with non-use; dense paragraphs defeat comprehension exactly where it matters most.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Before building a home-PD training schedule for a newly admitted patient, the nurse's FIRST step should be to:","opts":["Assess the patient's baseline knowledge, skills, dexterity, vision, home environment, and learning priorities","Order the standard training packets and book the standard calendar","Begin teaching exchange technique on day one to save time","Ask the family what the patient needs and teach accordingly"],"ans":0,"expl":"Effective teaching plans start with an assessment of what the learner already knows and can do (knowledge gaps, manual dexterity, vision, help at home, priorities) — Knowles' adult-learning principle that adults learn best around their own gaps and goals. Skipping to standard packets ignores individual barriers that later derail training; teaching technique before assessing wastes sessions and frustrates learners; families inform but do not replace the learner's own assessment.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"A newly listed transplant candidate must learn a complex immunosuppressant regimen. Which teaching structure best supports retention for this content?","opts":["A single long video with no follow-up","One comprehensive 90-minute lecture covering every drug detail","Short, spaced sessions with cumulative review and hands-on practice with a pill organizer","Mailing the full drug information packet for self-study only"],"ans":2,"expl":"Spaced, repeated, reinforced learning with demonstration and return-demonstration outperforms single long exposures for complex self-management regimens — cognitive load rises steeply in one-shot sessions and recall decays without practice. Immunosuppressant adherence is safety-critical (rejection risk), so cumulative review with hands-on med-organization tasks is the defensible structure; one-shot lectures, single videos, and mail-only packets offer too few reinforcement cycles.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A patient wants home hemodialysis but is visibly hesitant about self-cannulation, saying 'I always faint when staff put needles in.' Which response best reflects motivational interviewing?","opts":["'You will faint the first few times and get used to it — everyone does.'","'Tell me what most worries you about doing the needles yourself.' Then reflect her answer back and explore her own readiness to learn coping strategies","'You have to learn this or you cannot go home.'","'Only a few patients successfully learn self-cannulation; very few can manage it.'"],"ans":1,"expl":"Motivational interviewing works through open questions, reflective listening, affirmations, and eliciting the patient's OWN reasons and readiness — evoking change talk rather than arguing or creating pressure. Minimizing her fear, coercion ('or you cannot go home'), and discouragement all predict hardening of ambivalence, the opposite of MI's goal.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which goal best demonstrates the SMART-goal standard for a home-PD training plan?","opts":["'Family will do the exchanges for the patient permanently'","'Patient will learn PD exchanges when ready'","'Patient will perform exchanges sometime before the first clinic visit'","'Patient will independently complete three consecutive sterile exchanges with technique verified by observation, and verbalize cloud-effluent recognition, within the 7-session training block'"],"ans":3,"expl":"SMART goals are Specific (defined skills), Measurable (three consecutive verified exchanges), Achievable (within training capacity), Relevant (safe independent home PD), and Time-bound (within the 7-session block). Vague 'when ready/sometime' goals cannot show readiness for independent home therapy, and permanently outsourcing exchanges to family contradicts training standards unless the caregiver is formally trained as the designated partner.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which finding is the BEST evidence that exit-site care teaching has succeeded?","opts":["The patient performs the hand-hygiene, cleansing, and assessment sequence correctly on return demonstration and states the signs of exit-site infection","The patient nods when asked if the teaching was useful","The nurse documents 'exit-site care taught' per the checklist","The patient reads the printed instructions aloud fluently"],"ans":0,"expl":"Competency evidence is observed correct performance plus safety-critical verbalization (here: infection signs); nodding, checklist documentation, and fluent reading all measure compliance with paperwork, not learning. ISPD training standards judge home-PD readiness by demonstrated technique, not by attendance or a signed form.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","q":"A week after transplant, the nurse teaches tacrolimus and mycophenolate schedules. Which verification step best proves the patient can manage the regimen at home?","opts":["The patient signs a form stating the teaching occurred","The patient confirms the pharmacy will deliver the medications","Teach-back of dose timing, missed-dose rules, and interaction warnings, plus correctly filling the weekly pill organizer unaided","The spouse repeats the instructions while the patient is quiet"],"ans":2,"expl":"Regimen safety is verified by the patient's own demonstrated capability: teach-back of the safety-critical rules (timing windows, never doubling missed tacrolimus doses, interaction foods/drugs) AND an unaided practical fill of the organizer. Pharmacy delivery, signed teaching attestations, and spouse-repetition without the patient's own accuracy all leave the actual risk (missed/reversed doses) unmeasured.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which charting entry BEST documents successful patient education for a first hemodialysis treatment?","opts":["'Educated patient about dialysis'","'Patient independently stated two reasons for completing the full treatment time and demonstrated pressing the access site correctly after needle removal; teach-back confirmed understanding'","'Patient tolerated education well'","'Teaching done per policy; patient compliant'"],"ans":1,"expl":"Outcome-based documentation records what the patient DID (specific behaviors demonstrated and validated), which is both the clinical and legal standard for proving learning. Vague 'educated/tolerated/compliant' entries describe an activity, not an outcome, and would fail to support safe care continuity or defend against a claim.","obj":4},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A nurse is planning dietary teaching for a CKD patient whose latest labs show potassium 5.8 mEq/L and phosphorus 6.4 mg/dL. Which approach is most appropriate?","opts":["Focus only on sodium because it is the most familiar topic","Give the standard clinic handout the same for everyone","Restrict all fruits and all protein","Individualize content to the current labs: high-potassium foods first, then phosphate sources, with practical substitutions"],"ans":3,"expl":"Adult diet teaching works when it maps to the patient's own numbers and real foods: this patient's current risk is potassium and phosphate, so those come first with swaps she can actually keep. Generic identical handouts waste attention on content she does not need, blanket bans (all fruit/all protein) are unsustainable and nutritionally harmful, and sodium alone ignores the immediate safety issues.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which adaptation best supports teaching a 78-year-old with reduced near-vision and mild memory loss how to self-cannulate at home?","opts":["Chunked short sessions, large-print materials, consistent sequence, return demonstration each session, and involvement of the caregiver if desired","A single long evening session when the clinic is quiet","Standard 10-point font handouts with a take-home quiz","Teaching only the spouse and having them perform the task in future"],"ans":0,"expl":"Older learners with visual and working-memory limits learn self-care through short chunked sessions, large print, an unchanging sequence (procedural memory), and repeated observed practice — the same standards the ISPD-style home-training programs apply before signing off autonomy. One long session overloads retention; standard print defeats a reduced-vision learner; teaching only the spouse sidesteps the patient's own autonomy unless the patient chooses a designated caregiver plan (which then requires formal caregiver training).","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Per Kidney Community Emergency Response (KCER) guidance, which content belongs in a home-dialysis emergency-preparedness teaching plan?","opts":["A plan to present to the emergency department as the routine backup for every storm","An assurance that the commercial supplier will always reach the home within hours of any disaster","A 3-day emergency diet and medication supply, contact information for the home unit and backup facility, and personal emergency identification","Nothing special — emergencies are handled the same as clinic dialysis"],"ans":2,"expl":"KCER emergency kits and plans center on a 3-day kidney-diet and medication reserve, communication trees with the home unit and a backup facility, and emergency identification (and treatment info) so any caregiver can treat the patient. Supplier logistics are NOT guaranteed in disasters; the ED is not a routine substitute for the planned backup pathway; assuming clinic-normal conditions is how home patients end up stranded.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Two weeks after discharge from a kidney transplant, teaching evaluation shows the patient will avoid sick contacts and understands her medication box. Which addition shows the BEST next teaching priority for infection prevention?","opts":["That infection risk disappears after the first month","Food-safety practices: no unpasteurized dairy, undercooked eggs/meat, raw sprouts; and safe produce washing","That she can garden bare-handed as tolerated","That prophylactic antibiotics make food safety unnecessary"],"ans":1,"expl":"Early post-transplant, the highest-yield infection-prevention teaching after contacts and meds is food safety — Listeria and other foodborne organisms are disproportionate risks under immunosuppression (no unpasteurized products, undercooked proteins, raw sprouts; wash produce). Risk does not stop at one month (it tracks net immunosuppression); bare-hand gardening exposes soil organisms (Nocardia, Aspergillus) and glove use is advised; prophylaxis (e.g., PJP) covers specific organisms, not foodborne pathogens.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Before connecting a patient to the dialysis circuit, the nurse prepares the catheter hub. Which sequence matches CDC hemodialysis infection-control requirements?","opts":["Scrub each hub thoroughly with an antiseptic agent for at least 15 seconds and allow it to dry completely before access","Wipe the hub once with an alcohol swab and connect immediately while wet","Rinse the hub with sterile saline and skip antiseptic because the cap was intact","Wipe the connecting tubing with a dry gauze before attaching"],"ans":0,"expl":"The CDC dialysis bundle requires scrubbing catheter hubs and other connector pieces with an appropriate antiseptic for at least 15 seconds AND letting them dry fully (typically ≥30 seconds of air-dry time) before connection — the mechanical scrub plus dwell kills hub organisms, and access on a wet hub defeats the disinfectant. Single quick wipes, saline-only shortcuts, and dry rubbing leave hub biofilm intact, the dominant route of dialysis catheter-related bloodstream infection.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Per CDC infection-prevention recommendations for hemodialysis, who must wear a mask during catheter connection and disconnection?","opts":["Only the patient, since the nurse's PPE covers risk","Only the nurse if the patient has a fever","Both the nurse and the patient, every time","No one, if the session is a routine weekly connect"],"ans":2,"expl":"Connection and disconnection are the moments dialysis catheter bloodstream infection risk peaks (open hubs, patient's oropharyngeal flora over the field), so the CDC bundle specifies masks for BOTH patient and nurse at every connect/disconnect — this interrupts droplet contamination of the open circuit. Patient-symptom or frequency exceptions and one-sided masking all leave a documented contamination pathway and fail facility survey standards.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A hepatitis B-vaccinated dialysis patient (anti-HBs 25 mIU/mL after the series) is on routine maintenance care. How should hepatitis B surface-antibody levels be followed, per CDC dialysis recommendations?","opts":["No further testing is ever needed after initial vaccination","Test anti-HBs annually; booster or revaccination decisions follow the result","Recheck anti-HBs weekly during routine labs","Only test after a known exposure event"],"ans":1,"expl":"CDC dialysis guidance: vaccine responders (anti-HBs ≥10 mIU/mL) should have anti-HBs checked ANNUALLY; when titers fall below 10, a booster is given and non-responders after revaccination are managed as susceptible with isolation-based care. Dialysis patients lose titers faster than healthy hosts because of uremic immune hyporesponsiveness — which is exactly why annual surveillance, not exposure-triggered-only testing, exists.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A new patient screens HBsAg-positive at the dialysis unit. Per CDC recommendations, how should this patient's treatment be managed in the facility?","opts":["No changed practice because vaccination of others prevents spread","Regular stations with a blue tape marking on the chair","Home dialysis only for all HBsAg-positive patients","Dedicated machine, station, equipment, and staff assignment during the shift, plus hepatitis B e-antigen/antibody and HBV DNA follow-up"],"ans":3,"expl":"CDC hemodialysis standards mandate room separation, dedicated machines/supplies/equipment and designated staff for HBsAg-positive patients — dialysis settings historically amplified HBV through shared environments and hands despite vaccination programs (vaccines prevent infection in responders, and some staff/patients remain non-responders). Shared stations with markers do not meet the separation standard; home-only mandates are an inappropriate blanket policy; and 'others are vaccinated' ignores waning titers and confirmed non-response populations.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Monthly dialysis water cultures return 60 CFU/mL. The AAMI limit for standard dialysis water is 100 CFU/mL with an action level at half the maximum. What is the correct response?","opts":["Initiate disinfection of the water-treatment system now, before the limit is exceeded","Document the result and wait for the monthly culture to exceed 100 CFU/mL","Repeat the culture in six months","Double the patient treatment flow rate to dilute the water"],"ans":0,"expl":"AAMI/ISO water-quality systems run on ACTION LEVELS set at half the maximum so corrective disinfection happens BEFORE patients are exposed to out-of-limit water: 60 CFU/mL (above the 50 action level, below the 100 limit) triggers immediate investigation and disinfection. Waiting for the hard limit to breach is a compliance failure; six-month repetition ignores exponential biofilm growth in a contaminated loop; flow-rate dilution does not exist as a control.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Per ISPD 2022 peritonitis guidance, which empiric antibiotic plan is correct for a first episode of community-acquired PD peritonitis before sensitivities return?","opts":["Meropenem monotherapy intravenously","Cephalexin orally for 14 days","Gram-positive coverage (cefazolin or vancomycin) plus gram-negative coverage (a third-generation cephalosporin or aminoglycoside), preferably intraperitoneally","Prophylactic mupirocin at the exit site while observing the effluent"],"ans":2,"expl":"ISPD 2022 empiric therapy must cover BOTH organisms: gram-positive skin flora (S. epidermidis, S. aureus — cefazolin; vancomycin if MRSA history) and gram-negative enterics (cefepime/ceftazidime or aminoglycoside), delivered intraperitoneally as the preferred route. Narrow oral monotherapy before culture results fails empiric-coverage requirements; IV carbapenems bypass the guideline route/stewardship preference; exit-site ointment is prophylaxis, not peritonitis treatment.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A PD peritonitis episode has sterile effluent cultures at day 3 despite appropriate empiric therapy beginning after cloudy effluent and WBC 300/µL. Per ISPD, what is the appropriate response?","opts":["Discontinue antibiotics at day 3 since cultures were negative","Repeat the cell count and use extended-culture techniques (e.g., blood-culture bottle inoculation of effluent plus centrifugation) to pursue the diagnosis","Switch instantly to broad-spectrum antifungal therapy","Remove the catheter immediately in all culture-negative cases"],"ans":1,"expl":"Culture-negative peritonitis (a meaningful minority of episodes) is managed by REPEATING the cell count to confirm resolution trends and by optimized microbiology — inoculating effluent into blood-culture bottles and/or centrifugation — per ISPD, while continuing appropriate therapy guided by clinical response. Stopping antibiotics against a clinical peritonitis risks relapse; reflex antifungals are reserved for fungal isolates or defined non-response; catheter removal is organism- and response-specific, not automatic for culture negativity.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Effluent culture from a symptomatic PD patient grows Candida albicans. Per ISPD guidance, what is the appropriate management?","opts":["Switch the dialysis modality permanently and refer for transplant immediately","Continue antifungal therapy with the catheter in place regardless","Observe for one full week before any intervention","Prompt removal of the PD catheter in addition to appropriate antifungal therapy"],"ans":3,"expl":"ISPD recommends REMOVING the PD catheter promptly when fungi are identified as the cause of peritonitis, alongside systemic antifungals — catheter retention in fungal peritonitis carries high mortality and nearly universal treatment failure. Continuing with the catheter in situ contradicts the mortality data; observation wastes the surgical window while disseminated candidiasis risk rises; permanent modality change is an individualized later decision, not the acute management step.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A stable PD patient asks which daily routine protects the exit site per ISPD 2023 recommendations. Which answer is correct?","opts":["Cleanse the healed exit site with an appropriate agent twice weekly and after every shower or vigorous exercise, keep it dry between sessions, and avoid submerging in baths or hot tubs","Scrub with hydrogen peroxide twice daily for permanent cleanliness","No cleansing is needed for a healed exit site","Swimming pools and hot tubs are fine as long as the site looks normal"],"ans":0,"expl":"ISPD 2023 exit-site recommendations: cleanse a healed exit site at least twice weekly and after showering/vigorous exercise with a suitable cleanser, avoiding agents that impair healing (hydrogen peroxide and povidone-iodine are explicitly discouraged on healed sites — they injure tissue), keeping the site dry between cleansings, and avoiding prolonged submersion (baths, hot tubs, open-water swimming without specific protocols) since soaking macerates the barrier. 'Looks normal' does not neutralize immersion risk; no-cleansing at all contradicts the twice-weekly minimum.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"During a routine home-PD visit, the patient describes accidentally touching the tip of the transfer set to her waistband during an exchange. Per ISPD practice recommendations, what should she have done?","opts":["Abort the session and call only if fever develops over the next week","Continue the exchange since a new drain bag will filter the fluid","Drain the effluent and discard that exchange's dialysate, then start a fresh exchange with a new set as the unit directs, and observe/report symptoms","Flush the abdomen with antibiotic solution immediately"],"ans":2,"expl":"Touch contamination management: the contaminated exchange must be drained and discarded (the bag/set replaced), with the patient observed; symptomatic patients or repeated contaminations get unit evaluation — this prevents the inoculum from dwelling in the peritoneum. Reusing a contaminated exchange is the precise mechanism of touch-contamination peritonitis; waiting-for-fever forfeits the early-treatment advantage; home antibiotic flushing is a prescription decision, not a self-directed routine.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A dialysis patient completes the hepatitis B vaccine series but a post-vaccination anti-HBs is 5 mIU/mL. Per CDC dialysis guidance, what is the correct classification and response?","opts":["Responder: no action needed","Non-responder: re-vaccinate (another series) and continue susceptibility testing per protocol","Immunocompromised failure: document and never re-vaccinate","Proceed to transplant, which makes hepatitis B immunity irrelevant"],"ans":1,"expl":"Anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL after a full series is treated as non-response: CDC dialysis practice gives a repeat (second) vaccine series and continues the susceptible-care protocol (monthly HBsAg, separation-based care if positive) — because dialysis patients have impaired vaccine immunogenicity, repeat series produce response in a meaningful fraction. No-action ignores real risk; 'never re-vaccinate' contradicts the intended algorithm; transplantation does not remove HBV risk (reactivation risk makes immunity MORE relevant).","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"For a tunneled hemodialysis catheter's dressing, which practice matches infection-prevention recommendations?","opts":["Replace dressing tape every three months unless drainage appears","Change the dressing before every treatment regardless of condition","Leave the dressing off between treatments when the patient is at home","Keep the dressing intact and dry; change it routinely (e.g., weekly for transparent dressings, every 24 hours if wet, loose, or soiled)"],"ans":3,"expl":"Central-catheter dressing standards in dialysis keep the site sealed and dry, with scheduled changes (transparent semipermeable dressings ~weekly; gauze every 48-72h per policy) and immediate change when wet, loose, or soiled — moisture under the dressing is a documented exit-site and CRBSI risk factor. Routine pre-treatment changes on a clean intact dressing add handling risk without benefit, uncovered catheters at home are an infection exposure, and months-long tape intervals ignore both standards and the drainage signs that require immediate care.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The unit is monitoring adherence after a VRE cluster on one treatment shift. Which single behavior MOST reduces cross-contamination between patients at shared stations?","opts":["Hand hygiene with glove change between every patient contact, plus dedicated supplies for each station","Cohorting all patients into one large room on every shift","Monthly deep cleaning of the water-treatment room only","Requiring all patients to wear N95 masks during treatment"],"ans":0,"expl":"The dominant transmission vector in dialysis units is hands and shared surfaces between chair stations: CDC's core recommendations center on hand hygiene AND glove change between patient contacts, with station-dedicated supplies (carts, bp cuffs, clamps) preventing indirect transfer — this is what VRE/MRSA outbreak investigations repeatedly identify as the failed step. Cohorting whole rooms, water-room cleaning, and universal masking do not interrupt the glove-to-surface-to-glove pathway that actually moves MDROs between stations.","obj":6},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which immunization plan matches current ACIP recommendations for adults with CKD stage 4?","opts":["Live-attenuated vaccines are preferred because they are stronger","Influenza only, to avoid immune stimulation","Annual influenza vaccine and pneumococcal vaccination per ACIP age/risk intervals, plus hepatitis B vaccination with serologic follow-up","Pneumococcal vaccine is given once in childhood and never repeated"],"ans":2,"expl":"CKD patients have elevated invasive pneumococcal disease, influenza-related complications, and HBV exposure risk from the dialysis pathway — ACIP/CDC recommend annual influenza, age-/risk-based pneumococcal series, and the hepatitis B series with post-vaccination titers (dialysis-specific dosing), alongside the standard adult schedule. Influenza-only leaves pneumococcal and HBV risk open; live-attenuated vaccines are NOT preferred in advanced CKD/probable-transplant futures (contraindicated post-transplant); 'never repeated' contradicts current pneumococcal sequence guidance (PCV followed by PPSV23 intervals).","obj":6},
{"topic":"Transplant","q":"During pre-transplant evaluation of a 47-year-old with ESRD from polycystic kidney disease, from a region with moderate tuberculosis endemicity, which screening approach is standard per transplant-infectious-disease practice?","opts":["No screening — post-transplant antifungal prophylaxis covers tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis screening (IGRA preferred over tuberculin skin testing in BCG-vaccinated individuals) before transplantation, with treatment of latent infection when found","Skin test only if the patient recalls a cough last year","BCG vaccination after transplant"],"ans":1,"expl":"Transplant evaluation includes latent-TB screening (IGRA preferred where available, especially in BCG-exposed persons where PPD cross-reacts) because post-transplant immunosuppression multiplies reactivation risk — latent infection found pre-transplant is treated BEFORE or early after transplant per regimen guidance. Antifungal agents do nothing against mycobacteria; symptom-only testing misses the entire latent reservoir; BCG is a live vaccine and contraindicated after transplantation.","obj":6},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A facility is redesigning its QAPI committee. Per the Conditions for Coverage, which composition requirement is correct?","opts":["The interdisciplinary team chairs QAPI participation, and the medical director remains accountable for the program's clinical and safety outcomes","Technicians may run QAPI alone since they see every treatment first-hand","QAPI is a nursing-only committee with dietitian input twice a year","The medical director delegates all QAPI accountability permanently and attends only annual reviews"],"ans":0,"expl":"42 CFR 494.110 requires an ongoing QAPI program covering clinical outcomes and patient safety, carried out by the interdisciplinary team, with the medical director accountable for the quality-assessment and performance-improvement program's outcomes. Technician-run, nursing-only, or delegated-into-absence structures all fail the CoP — the director's accountability is facility-wide and standing, not an annual appearance.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A facility plans to involuntarily discharge a non-adherent patient whose behaviors have not yet harmed anyone despite documented facility interventions. Per CMS Conditions for Coverage, which requirement applies?","opts":["Discharge with no written explanation to protect the facility's reputation","A same-day discharge letter with no notice period","Documented efforts to rehabilitate the patient, advance written notice (not less than 30 days for non-emergency discharges), an explanation of patient rights including the appeal process, and notification of the ESRD Network","Notification only to the patient's insurance company"],"ans":2,"expl":"Involuntary discharge under 42 CFR 494.180(f) and patient-rights standards requires documented progressive interventions supporting the patient, advance written notice (generally at least 30 days for non-emergency discharges), information on the patient's rights to appeal (per federal/state processes), and ESRD Network notification so continuity of care is protected. Same-day non-emergency discharges, silent discharges, and insurer-only notice are all direct CoP violations — discharge is a last resort after documented rehabilitation attempts.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Mid-treatment, a patient becomes acutely hypotensive after a nurse discovers a teammate gave the wrong potassium-containing medication during the morning routine. What is the FIRST required action?","opts":["Complete the incident report form before touching the patient","Assess and stabilize the patient immediately, initiate the emergency response if indicated, then complete the event reporting","Call the family first, then notify the manager of the delay in care","Continue the treatment and evaluate the patient at the normal end-of-treatment time"],"ans":1,"expl":"Patient stabilization always precedes paperwork: hypotension with a wrong potassium-containing medication is a potential hyperkalemia/arrhythmia scenario — assess (vitals, monitor, symptoms), treat per protocol, escalate the emergency response if indicated, then file the event report and notify per chain. Report-first sequencing inverts the clinical priority; family-first delays stabilization; waiting until treatment ends abandons a monitored acute situation.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"A patient on CRRT is being transferred from the ICU back to the step-down unit. Which communication practice best demonstrates closed-loop communication in the handoff?","opts":["A written summary is dropped at the station for review later","The receiving nurse nods acknowledgment and asks no questions to appear competent","The sending nurse reads the chart aloud without pausing for confirmation","The receiving nurse repeats back the CRRT prescription, filter hours, anticoagulation rates, and last ionized calcium, and the sending nurse confirms each element"],"ans":3,"expl":"Closed-loop communication (AHRQ TeamSTEPPS): the sender states information, the receiver REPEATS it back, and the sender verifies — creating a correction opportunity for the exact elements most likely to harm (citrate rate, ionized calcium, filter hours). Nodding is check-the-box acknowledgment; unidirectional reading aloud without feedback is not closed-loop; a document left for later review is neither a handoff nor a verification.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A discharged kidney-transplant recipient arrives for the first post-hospitalization treatment with a medication list that conflicts with the transplant center's discharge summary. Which collaborative response best protects the patient?","opts":["Reconcile the medication list with the pharmacist and the transplant coordinator before the next immunosuppressant dose is taken","Pick the medication list with the more recent date and follow it as written","Continue the patient's pre-hospital regimen and ask questions at the next monthly visit","Have the patient decide which list to follow at home"],"ans":0,"expl":"Post-discharge medication discrepancies are a leading transition-of-care harm; in transplant patients a wrong immunosuppressant regimen risks rejection (under-immunosuppression) or toxicity (over-immunosuppression). Reconciliation with the pharmacist plus the transplant coordinator — the prescriber of record for those medications — resolves discrepancies BEFORE further doses. Defaulting to a date stamp, deferring to the monthly visit, or delegating the decision to the patient offloads a clinical conflict onto the least-supported party.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Transplant","q":"A potential recipient is being evaluated for a kidney from a deceased donor who met increased-risk criteria. Per OPTN policy expectations, what must occur before the patient is transplanted with this organ?","opts":["The family signs the consent if the patient is asleep","The organ may be transplanted since the surgical team already reviewed the risk internally","The patient must be informed of the increased infection risk per Centers for Disease Control guidance and provide explicit consent to accept the offer","Risk can be discussed after transplantation when results are back"],"ans":2,"expl":"OPTN policy requires that candidates be informed of donor risk criteria (based on CDC risk classification) BEFORE accepting the organ, with explicit documented consent — the patient weighs the infection-risk versus waiting-mortality tradeoff herself. Team-internal review, family signature, or post hoc disclosure all violate the informed-consent requirement and the policy's transparency intent.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"A patient believes a dialysis facility harmed her care and is dissatisfied with the facility's internal grievance response. Which entity provides independent patient advocacy and complaint review for dialysis grievances?","opts":["The county health department licensing board","The ESRD Network organization for the facility's region","The patient's primary care physician's office","The hospital's emergency department"],"ans":1,"expl":"ESRD Networks (federally designated, under CMS) independently review dialysis grievances, mediate facility-patient conflicts, and enforce access-to-care protections — patients may file with the Network directly and the facility must give patients that contact information as part of grievance rights under 42 CFR 494.70. County licensing boards, PCP offices, and EDs have no such dialysis-oversight role.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A nephrologist verbally prescribes an urgent change to a CRRT filter replacement schedule. Which documentation practice matches safe verbal-order standards?","opts":["Ask another nurse to remember the order jointly","Write the order down later from memory without a read-back","Implement the verbal instruction but refuse to document anything verbal","Repeat the order back for verification, implement it, and document the read-back with prompt provider co-signature per policy"],"ans":3,"expl":"Safe verbal-order practice: the receiver repeats the order back verbatim (verification), implements, documents the order with the read-back noted, and obtains provider co-signature per the facility policy timeline (prompt — commonly within 24-48h). Memory-only capture risks dose/rate transcription errors; refusing to document verbal orders leaves no legal record; two-nurse recall is not a verification method.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A dialysis patient is discharged from hospitalization. Per CMS discharge-planning and coordination expectations, what should the dialysis facility ensure regarding the discharge documentation?","opts":["The hospital's discharge summary and medication reconciliation reach the dialysis facility promptly (target within one business day) so the next treatment is safe","Documentation is optional because the patient can self-report","The dialysis facility only needs discharge papers at the monthly review","The patient carries all responsibility for delivering records between facilities"],"ans":0,"expl":"Coordinated transitions require the discharge record (summary, medication list, pending tests) to flow to the next treaters in days, not weeks — CMS discharge-planning rules and dialysis care-coordination expectations target next-treatment safety, so the facility actively obtains the record (within about one business day where systems allow) and reconciles orders before the first post-discharge treatment. Self-report-only handoffs, monthly delays, and patient-as-courier are the standard failure modes of transition care.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The QAPI team wants an accurate surveillance denominator and numerator for dialysis-facility bloodstream infections. Which data source is the recognized standard?","opts":["The facility's profit-and-loss statement","Ad hoc staff memory tallies collected during rounds","CDC's National Healthcare Safety Network (NHSN) dialysis-event protocols","An anonymous patient survey about infections"],"ans":2,"expl":"NHSN dialysis-event reporting is the CDC-standard surveillance system for dialysis bloodstream infections, local access infections, and antimicrobial starts, with defined protocols (reporting criteria, denominators of patient-months at risk) that make facility rates comparable to national benchmarks — and it feeds ESRD QIP and CMS requirements. Memory tallies are neither complete nor defensible; financial systems contain no clinical events; perception surveys do not count occurrences clinically.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Per the Conditions for Coverage, when a patient begins dialysis, what treatment-option education is the facility required to provide?","opts":["Only the modality the facility prefers to deliver","Education on all treatment modalities — including home dialysis options and transplantation referral criteria — tailored to the patient's situation","In-center hemodialysis education with transplant mentioned if the patient asks","A brochure only at the 90-day mark"],"ans":1,"expl":"42 CFR 494.90 requires patient education on all available treatment modalities (in-center HD, home HD, PD, and transplantation with referral criteria when appropriate), tailored to the patient — modality choice is an informed shared decision, not a facility marketing outcome. Preference-based education, ask-only transplant talk, or delayed brochures fail the CoP and bias patients against home modalities the evidence supports.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which artifact best demonstrates the STUDY step of a PDSA cycle testing a new catheter-lock protocol?","opts":["The vendor's marketing brochure about the product","A signed memo announcing the protocol is now permanent facility-wide","A budget line showing the cost of the new lock solution","A run chart comparing pre- and post-implementation outcomes against the prediction made in the Plan step"],"ans":3,"expl":"PDSA 'Study' analyzes observed data against the Plan step's predictions — a run chart of outcomes over time (infection events, protocol adherence) directly tests whether the change produced the predicted improvement at the small-test scale. Announcement memos document 'Act' without study, budgets show cost not effect, and vendor materials are not facility data.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Per CMS adequacy-measurement expectations for in-center hemodialysis, which practice is correct for a stable adult patient?","opts":["Measure delivered dialysis adequacy (e.g., Kt/V via pre- and post-treatment BUN) at the intervals CMS requires — monthly-based sampling per facility protocol — and review results in the plan of care","Adequacy is assessed once at admission and never re-measured","Adequacy is inferred from how the patient feels","Kt/V is only relevant for peritoneal dialysis patients"],"ans":0,"expl":"CMS requires ongoing delivered-dose measurement in HD — Kt/V from paired pre/post BUN sampling on the protocol's scheduled (typically monthly) treatment, reviewed and acted on in the interdisciplinary plan of care — because adequacy is both a clinical safety measure and a reporting requirement. Single-measurement-at-admission, symptom-inference, and 'PD-only' all misunderstand the measure (PD uses peritoneal equilibration/weekly Kt/V concepts, but HD delivers its own paired-sample requirement).","obj":7},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A home-PD patient is hospitalized and returns to the unit after discharge. Which collaborative step best prevents the first post-discharge complication?","opts":["Ask the family to bring whatever papers they have next week","Wait for the patient to mention problems during the next monthly clinic","The nurse coordinates a medication and order reconciliation (with the pharmacist/transplant team as applicable) and reviews the discharge summary before or at the first treatment","Trust that the hospital handled everything since the patient is stable now"],"ans":2,"expl":"The first post-discharge treatment is where un-reconciled orders, changed anticoagulants, held antihypertensives, and new antibiotics cause harm — proactive reconciliation against the discharge summary (transplant medications especially) closes the transition gap. Waiting for the monthly clinic, deferring to family paperwork later, or assuming hospital completeness are the documented failure modes that turn discharge into readmission.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A facility's QAPI committee is considering a change to the hypotension-management protocol. Which method best incorporates the patient voice consistent with CMS quality expectations?","opts":["Design the protocol entirely in the medical director's office for consistency","Include patient-family advisory input (e.g., through a patient representative on the QAPI process) when designing the protocol change","Adopt another facility's protocol without adaptation because their outcomes were good","Survey the internet for the most popular protocol"],"ans":1,"expl":"CMS quality expectations push patient-and-family engagement into improvement design (patient representatives or advisory-council input), because hypotension outcomes depend on patient-reported symptoms (cramping, lightheadedness) and adherence behavior that only patient input reveals. Closed-door design ignores experience data; unadapted copying imports another facility's population differences; internet popularity is not evidence.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with CKD stage 4 has new lab results showing potassium 6.1 mEq/L. In the same visit she also wants advice on travel and exercise. How should the nurse sequence this teaching encounter?","opts":["Teach the hyperkalemia warning signs and diet triggers first, defer travel and exercise counseling to a follow-up contact","Cover travel and exercise first since the patient asked for them, then mention potassium briefly at the end","Combine everything into one comprehensive session covering the full CKD lifestyle curriculum","Teach only travel planning because patient motivation drives learning"],"ans":0,"expl":"A potassium of 6.1 mEq/L is an immediate arrhythmia risk, so the safety-critical content (high-potassium foods, salt-substitute danger, when labs get rechecked, symptoms that trigger an urgent call) is taught first and the encounter confirms understanding before moving on. Travel and exercise matter but carry no same-day mortality risk, so they are scheduled as follow-up content rather than competing with survival information.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A Deaf patient who communicates in American Sign Language starts in-center hemodialysis. Per ADA effective-communication standards, which arrangement is correct for her education sessions?","opts":["Lip-reading and written notes are sufficient because dialysis teaching is routine","Her hearing teenage daughter interprets since she signs fluently","A qualified ASL interpreter provided by the facility for significant health discussions, with written supporting materials","A phone captioning service alone satisfies all communication needs"],"ans":2,"expl":"The ADA requires effective communication through qualified interpreters for significant health interactions — a qualified ASL interpreter is competent in both ASL and medical terminology and is confidentiality-bound. Family interpreters (especially minors) filter, omit, and misinterpret medical content; lip-reading captures a fraction of speech; captioning alone cannot carry interactive clinical teaching. Written materials support — not replace — interpreted sessions.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient with ESRD has chosen PD and a catheter placement date is set. Per ISPD-based pre-insertion education practice, what is the BEST timing and content for the catheter-care teaching session?","opts":["After the catheter is placed and healed, when teaching is more accurate","Before placement: cover the procedure basics, exit-site care expectations, healing timeline, and what the first exchanges will feel like — so the patient consents and participates in care decisions informed","On the morning of surgery, in the pre-op holding area","Only after the first malfunction occurs, so teaching matches real problems"],"ans":1,"expl":"Pre-insertion education (procedure expectations, exit-site care, healing course, early-exchange sensations) is paired with the informed-consent process and improves preparedness, anxiety control, and post-placement participation; it also sets expectations about leaks and immobilization so early complications do not panic the patient. Teaching only after healing or after problems forfeits the preparation benefit entirely.","obj":4},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient started on sevelamer asks why the pills must be taken with meals instead of between meals. Which teach-back response shows she understood the reason?","opts":["'With meals it prevents the nausea that happens on an empty stomach'","'Taking it with food helps me remember my other pills too'","'Food dissolves the tablet coating so it works faster'","'The binder has to be in my gut with the food so it can grab the phosphorus as I digest it'"],"ans":3,"expl":"Phosphate binders work by mixing with dietary phosphate IN the intestinal lumen during digestion — taken without food they bind nothing, which is why binder timing with meals and snacks is the single most important adherence teaching for these drugs. Memory tricks and coating theories do not explain the pharmacology, and while GI tolerance matters, tolerability is not the mechanism being verified.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Transplant","q":"One month after kidney transplant, the nurse checks that the patient can recognize graft-rejection warning signs. Which statement by the patient is the BEST evidence of understanding?","opts":["'Fever, the graft getting tender or swollen, my urine dropping off, or feeling flu-ish mean I call the team right away — even at night'","'Rejection only happens in the first week, so after that I do not need to watch for anything'","'If my creatinine is high at the monthly lab, I will double my tacrolimus myself'","'Rejection signs show up mainly as a rash over the graft area'"],"ans":0,"expl":"Rejection red flags (fever, graft tenderness/swelling, falling urine output, flu-like symptoms) warrant same-day team contact — patients must know to call, never wait for the next visit, and never self-adjust immunosuppression. Rejection risk spans the whole graft lifetime (not the first week only), it usually presents as lab changes plus systemic symptoms rather than rash, and self-dose escalation above the prescribed target is dangerous.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"After a fluid-management teaching session, the patient's teach-back contains a clear misconception — he says he can 'catch up' his fluid allowance on the weekend. What is the correct immediate response?","opts":["Repeat the identical explanation verbatim and move on since the material was covered","Note the misunderstanding in the chart and address it at next month's visit","Correct the misconception now with a different explanation, then re-verify before he leaves, and document the barrier plus the re-teaching plan","End the session — a wrong teach-back means the patient is unmotivated"],"ans":2,"expl":"An incorrect teach-back is the signal to re-teach DIFFERENTLY immediately (new analogy, demonstration, or visual) and confirm the corrected understanding before the encounter ends, while documenting the specific misconception and the approach that worked — that is the failure path the teach-back method is designed for. Deferring a fluid misconception for a month invites interdialytic weight-gain and pulmonary-edema risk; verbatim repetition repeats the same failure; labeling the patient unmotivated misreads a teaching-method problem.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient struggles to visualize fluid limits between treatments. Which teaching aid BEST makes interdialytic fluid gains concrete?","opts":["A written fluid list with no visuals","A marked measuring container demo: 'these are the drinks that fit your 1-liter spread across three days' with his own cups filled to scale","Telling him the kilogram number from the scale each visit","Comparing him to other patients' weight gains"],"ans":1,"expl":"Concrete, personally-calibrated demonstrations turn abstract prescriptions into visible portions — filling his actual cups to the allowance makes the limit tangible and memorable, which is why dietary counselors use real-vessel demonstrations for fluid education. Lists without visuals and bare numbers stay abstract; comparing patients shames rather than teaches and ignores individual prescriptions.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A patient is extremely anxious about her first in-center dialysis treatment scheduled for tomorrow. Which educational intervention BEST addresses the anxiety?","opts":["Send a detailed written article about dialysis complications to read tonight","Reassure her that everyone gets used to it and skip details because detail increases fear","Reschedule the start until she feels calm on her own","A pre-treatment walk-through: show her the chair, machines, the needle placement plan, and walk through what the first 15 minutes feel like"],"ans":3,"expl":"Procedural preview (environmental familiarization plus a step-by-step walkthrough of the first minutes) reduces the fear of the unknown that drives first-treatment anxiety; controlled exposure of this kind is standard pre-dialysis preparation. Dismissing fears, delaying treatment without a plan, or dumping complication reading material on an anxious patient the night before all amplify the appraisal of threat.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"At monthly reviews, a home-hemodialysis patient's weights log shows a slow upward drift in interdialytic gains over six weeks. Which teaching response is BEST?","opts":["Review the LOG TRENDS with him: which days rose, matched against his food and drink diary, and rebuild the plan around the pattern","Tell him the number is unacceptable and he must try harder","Ignore the trend because today's weight is in range","Change his dry weight target upward to make the numbers look better"],"ans":0,"expl":"Trend-based coaching uses the log pattern (weekend rises, salty meals, missed sessions) to find the driver, then rebuilds the plan with the patient — the whole point of patient-maintained logs. Shaming and ignoring the drift both abandon the opportunity, and raising the dry weight target to manufacture 'good' numbers is a clinical error that shifts risk onto volume status.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","q":"A patient with limited literacy must manage seven daily medications at home. Which labeling strategy is BEST supported for safe self-administration?","opts":["The full medication leaflets with instructions to call if confused","Standard pharmacy tiny-print labels so nothing is 'dumbed down'","Simplified, image-supported labels (morning, with food, blood-pressure pill) plus a teach-back walk-through of each label","Sorting the pills into an unlabeled bag once a week"],"ans":2,"expl":"Plain, image-supported, time-of-day-labeled dosing systems (the evidence-based 'picture-pill' approach) substantially reduce dosing errors in low-literacy patients, verified by walking each label through teach-back. Dense standard labels are a known risk factor for error in exactly this population; full leaflets overwhelm; unlabeled pill bags remove the safety net entirely.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which exchange BEST demonstrates correcting a patient misconception IMMEDIATELY during teaching rather than deferring it?","opts":["Nurse notes the statement and plans to correct it at discharge","Patient says 'dialysis will cure my kidneys' — nurse responds: 'Actually, dialysis does the kidneys' filtering work; let's talk about what that means for your transplant options too'","Nurse ignores it to keep the session positive","Nurse waits for the provider to correct it during rounds"],"ans":1,"expl":"Misconceptions that touch treatment reality (dialysis as cure vs replacement therapy) are corrected in the moment with clear language and a bridge to the connected topic (transplant as the disease-modifying option) — leaving false mental models intact lets patients make choices (skipping visits, dropping waitlist interest) on wrong premises. Deferral to discharge, rounds, or 'staying positive' forfeits the window when the misconception is active in the patient's thinking.","obj":4},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD stage 5 patient who is not yet on dialysis asks whether he needs the hepatitis B vaccine before starting in-center treatment. Which teaching response is accurate?","opts":["'Only after you catch hepatitis B once'","'No — the machine water treatment makes vaccination unnecessary'","'Only if you plan to travel abroad'","'Yes — all dialysis centers vaccinate patients against hepatitis B and track antibody titers, because exposure risk is ongoing and vaccine response is weaker once you are on dialysis'"],"ans":3,"expl":"Hepatitis B vaccination with dialysis-adapted dosing and post-vaccination titer follow-up is standard at dialysis initiation per CDC — response rates are better BEFORE or early into dialysis than in long-established uremia, and the dialysis environment carries ongoing exposure risk regardless of water treatment. Waiting until after infection reverses the entire prevention logic.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"The unit plans a monthly 'new-start' group education class covering diet, access care, and home-modalities overview. Which design choice matches privacy and learning best practices?","opts":["General concepts are taught to the group; personal labs, diagnoses, and questions about one patient's care are taken privately afterward","Everyone's lab values are shared on screen so patients can compare","Patients are asked to state their diagnoses aloud so the group learns the variety","The class is mandatory with attendance recorded as the learning outcome"],"ans":0,"expl":"Group education works when the curriculum is general and personal health information stays private — concepts for the room, individual details and questions in a private follow-up. Displaying identifiable labs or having diagnoses spoken aloud creates HIPAA exposure and social discomfort, and attendance is not a learning outcome — demonstrated understanding is.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A PD patient learns best by watching rather than reading. Which teaching adaptation is MOST appropriate for her exchange training?","opts":["Audio recordings of the trainer describing each step","The standard printed manual alone for homework","Demonstration-first sessions where the trainer performs the step, narrates while doing it, then hands over for return demonstration","Referral to the nephrologist to explain procedures verbally"],"ans":2,"expl":"Matching the modality to the learner's demonstrated preference (visual/observational learning) — demonstration, narration-in-action, and immediate hands-on return demonstration — accelerates procedural skill acquisition compared with text for a watch-first learner. Manuals alone, audio-only, and deferring to the physician all mismatch the demonstrated learning channel and slow training sign-off.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A patient completed home-PD training three months ago. During the routine visit the nurse should do what, per retention principles?","opts":["Assume the original training persists indefinitely","Re-verify the safety-critical skills with an observed spot check, since technique decays without reinforcement and drift predicts infection","Only review the paperwork log, not the technique","Re-train from scratch every visit regardless of performance"],"ans":1,"expl":"Skills decay; spaced re-verification of safety-critical steps (hand hygiene, connection sterility, effluent inspection) with an observed spot check catches drift before it becomes peritonitis — the same spaced-reinforcement logic used in initial training. Assumed persistence and log-only review miss technique decay; wholesale retraining every visit is inefficient and insults demonstrated competence.","obj":4},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"During a treatment, a patient develops an unanticipated severe reaction requiring transfer to the emergency department. Per CMS QAPI expectations, what must the facility do after the patient is transferred?","opts":["Review the event as part of its QAPI process — identify contributing factors, document the review, and act to prevent recurrence","Wait for the hospital's report, then simply file the hospital letter","Record only the transport time and destination","Take no further action because the transfer means the event left the facility"],"ans":0,"expl":"CMS Conditions for Coverage require dialysis facilities to review unanticipated outcomes and adverse events through QAPI (root-cause style analysis, documented findings, and corrective actions), regardless of where the patient was treated afterward. The hospital letter supplements but does not replace the facility's own review — transport records alone and no-action dispositions fail the QAPI standard because the improvement duty follows the event, not the building.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which documentation BEST shows the interdisciplinary plan of care includes genuine patient participation in goal-setting?","opts":["'Goals set per facility protocol'","'Patient was present at the care conference'","'Plan-of-care goals developed with patient: patient chose the 2-hour treatment schedule around her work shift; her self-selected goal is walking 15 minutes after treatment on treatment days'","'Patient agreed with the team's recommended plan'"],"ans":2,"expl":"Patient participation means the plan records THEIR input and self-selected goals in measurable form — here she shaped the schedule around work and chose a specific activity goal. Presence, protocol defaults, and passive agreement document attendance or compliance but no patient voice, which is what 494.90's patient-participation expectations and person-centered-care standards require in the written plan.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"A facility prepares for a CMS survey. Which self-audit practice BEST predicts survey readiness?","opts":["Only verify the waiting room signage and posted certificates","Internal tracer audits: pick random patient charts, walk the actual pathway of care, and check that documentation, QAPI follow-through, and education records match the standards","Rely on the last survey's findings as the current state","Audit only the staff with the fewest years of experience"],"ans":1,"expl":"Tracer methodology — following real patients' pathways through records and practice against Condition-level standards (education, plan of care, QAPI closure, infection-control bundles) — is exactly how CMS surveyors work, so facilities that self-tracer find their own gaps before the survey does. Signage, stale findings, and experience-based sampling all leave the actual pathways unverified.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"CDC publishes a new recommendation for dialysis infection prevention. What is the correct facility process before staff begin following it?","opts":["Practice changes wait until the next federal survey","Each nurse adopts the recommendation individually as they read it","The charge nurse posts a memo and practice changes immediately","Medical Director and infection-control review approve the practice change (assessing fit with policies and physician orders), staff are trained, and QAPI tracks the change"],"ans":3,"expl":"Practice changes in dialysis run through defined change control: the medical director (accountable for clinical policy and physician-order alignment) plus infection-control review confirm the recommendation's applicability, policies and standing orders are updated, staff training occurs, and QAPI measures the implementation — because patient-safety practices are facility policies, not personal nurse preferences. Individual adoption without approval creates practice variation; memo-only and survey-driven timing both skip training and tracking.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"In QAPI review, which event classification and response is CORRECT?","opts":["A near-miss (error caught before reaching the patient) is still reported and reviewed — because the same system that failed once will fail again differently","Near-misses do not require reporting because no patient was touched","Near-misses are tracked only in the reporter's personnel file","Near-miss reporting is voluntary and should be discouraged to keep error statistics low"],"ans":0,"expl":"Near-misses (caught before reaching the patient) are QAPI gold: they expose system weaknesses with no harm attached, and systems that produced one near-miss reliably produce real adverse events under different conditions — which is why safe-reporting culture actively collects them, analyzes them in QAPI, and protects the reporter. Suppression for statistics is the classic precursor to a serious event; personnel-file tracking converts safety reporting into discipline and kills future reporting.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A facility wants to strengthen its home-PD program. Which metric BEST indicates program health per home-dialysis QI practice?","opts":["Marketing brochures distributed per quarter","Total number of PD catheters placed by surgeons","Training completion rates and early dropout within the first 90 days, tracked with reasons for dropout","The director's opinion of the program"],"ans":2,"expl":"Home-PD program health is measured by whether patients who START training successfully TRAIN, TRANSITION, and REMAIN at home — completion rates and 90-day dropout with cause-coded reasons (inadequate training, caregiver collapse, technique failure) expose exactly where the program loses patients, and each dropout cause maps to a fixable intervention. Catheter counts measure surgery volume; brochures measure marketing; opinion measures nothing — none indicate whether home therapy is being delivered successfully.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A facility considering switching to a biosimilar erythropoiesis-stimulating agent wants to control medication costs safely. Which process is correct?","opts":["Silent one-for-one substitution at the next pharmacy reorder","Medical Director approval with physician-order alignment, staff education on the new product, and QAPI tracking of hemoglobin stability and adverse reaction reports after the switch","Nursing decision at the unit level during a slow week","Immediate facility-wide switch during the busiest treatment week of the year"],"ans":1,"expl":"Medication changes that alter clinical care are physician-policy decisions: the medical director approves (with orders updated), staff receive product-specific education (dose equivalency, storage, administration), and QAPI tracks outcome stability (hemoglobin response, iron status, reaction reports) — the standard medication-change control pathway applied to biosimilars. Silent substitution bypasses order alignment and staff training; unit-level or peak-week timing decisions ignore the coordination the change requires.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"The QAPI committee reviews three patient complaints received this quarter about seating assignment conflicts. Which analytical approach is correct?","opts":["Report the complaining patients to administration for causing conflict","Handle each complaint individually and never look across them","Dismiss the complaints because three is a small number","Aggregate complaint data over time (types, frequency, resolution times) and look for patterns that indicate a systemic issue"],"ans":3,"expl":"QAPI improvement analytics aggregate: individual complaints are resolved individually, but the committee's job is the cross-case pattern (recurring themes, timing, units, resolution delays) that reveals systemic causes — three seating-related complaints in a quarter IS a signal worth trending. Case-by-case handling without aggregation misses the system; dismissing small-N data suppresses early-warning information; retaliating against complainants violates patient-rights protections and chills future reporting.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Per Section 1557 compliance practice, how should interpreter use appear in facility records and QAPI?","opts":["Interpreted interactions are documented (who interpreted, modality used), and QAPI audits interpretation access as part of language-access compliance","Interpreter use is unnecessary to document because it is voluntary","Only interpreter COSTS are tracked for the budget","Staff decide case by case without any documentation standard"],"ans":0,"expl":"Language-access compliance is auditable: records show what interpretation was used (qualified interpreter, VRI, telephonic) for significant interactions, and QAPI reviews access patterns (are LEP patients getting interpreted teaching? are refusal/decline patterns concentrated?) — the same way other patient-rights compliance gets tracked. Undocumented interpretation defeats both the legal record and the QAPI ability to detect access gaps; cost-only tracking measures spend, not access; per-staff discretion invites inconsistent compliance.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A home-PD patient's routine follow-up moves partially to telehealth visits. Which coordination practice keeps the telehealth pathway safe?","opts":["The nurse conducts telehealth without any escalation plan and handles crises by messaging","Telehealth replaces all in-person evaluations permanently","A clear escalation pathway: telehealth covers stable review needs, with defined triggers (cloudy effluent, fever, weight changes) that immediately route the patient to in-person care","Telehealth visits substitute for effluent cell-count analysis when symptoms appear"],"ans":2,"expl":"Safe telehealth in home dialysis pairs remote review with explicit red-flag escalation: defined symptoms (cloudy effluent, fever, significant weight change, exit-site changes) trigger immediate in-person/urgent evaluation — telehealth extends access for stable management, it does not replace the diagnostic pathway for suspected peritonitis. Full replacement and no-plan messaging leave symptom triage improvised, and video chat cannot substitute for effluent cell counts when peritonitis is suspected.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A nurse educator explains to new staff how CMS payment works for dialysis facilities. Which statement about the ESRD Quality Incentive Program (QIP) is accurate?","opts":["Quality never affects Medicare payment; only patient volume does","ESRD facilities can have their Medicare payment for dialysis services adjusted up or down based on performance on specified quality measures reported each program year","The QIP only applies to hospital dialysis units","QIP scores are private and never published"],"ans":1,"expl":"The ESRD QIP links a portion of Medicare payment for dialysis facilities to performance on designated quality measures (adequacy, infection reporting via NHSN, ICH-CAHPS patient experience, and others), with payment adjustment scores published — value-based purchasing applied to dialysis. Volume does not determine the modulation; the program covers freestanding and hospital-based facilities alike; and scores are public, which is their intended transparency pressure.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"The renal dietitian and the nephrologist disagree about a patient's protein prescription (the dietitian plans 1.1 g/kg/day based on nutrition assessment; the nephrologist ordered 0.8 g/kg/day). What is the BEST collaborative resolution?","opts":["Have the patient choose whichever plan sounds easier","Follow the dietitian's plan since she does the assessments","Follow the physician order silently since doctors outrank dietitians","Bring the conflict to the interdisciplinary plan-of-care discussion with both clinicians' evidence and the patient's nutritional status, and agree a documented plan with monitoring"],"ans":3,"expl":"Interdisciplinary disagreements resolve in the plan-of-care forum where each discipline presents its assessment evidence and the team converges on a documented plan with monitoring parameters — the mechanism the care-conference structure exists for. Dietitian-only or hierarchy-default approaches bypass either the nutrition expertise or the medical prescription authority, and delegating a clinical conflict to the patient offloads a team responsibility without removing the underlying disagreement.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"QAPI review shows three patients have depended on tunneled catheters for over eight weeks with no fistula-placement evaluation documented. Which plan-of-care action is correct?","opts":["Each patient's plan of care is reviewed for vascular-access planning — referral/evaluation goals for permanent access (AV fistula preferred where feasible) are established and tracked","Leave the catheters since they are functioning","Document that catheters are acceptable long-term access","Refer every patient for immediate transplant evaluation instead"],"ans":0,"expl":"Catheter dependence carries the highest infection and thrombosis risk among access types, so the plan of care must document access planning — evaluation for permanent access (fistula first where clinically feasible) with dated goals and follow-through tracked in QAPI. 'Functioning' catheters still kill through bacteremia; declaring catheters acceptable long-term care contradicts access standards; blanket transplant referrals address candidacy, not the access hazard.","obj":7},
{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"A home-PD program expands its staffing. Which competency practice matches home-training program standards?","opts":["Competency ends after the first successful patient discharge to home","Trainers teach from their own personal habits without curriculum oversight","PD trainers validate their teaching competency periodically (observed teaching evaluations against the program curriculum standards), not just on initial hire","Any available nurse covers training shifts when short-staffed"],"ans":2,"expl":"Trainer quality is a patient-safety control: home-training programs validate trainers against the program's curriculum standards with periodic observed teaching evaluations — because technique drift in TRAINERS replicates into every patient they train, and initial-hire competency decays as products and protocols evolve. Personal-habit teaching bypasses standardization; discharge-once validation ignores drift; float coverage without validated trainers puts inconsistent technique onto new patients.","obj":7},
{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"QAPI audits discover that 40% of patient emergency-contact records are outdated (old phone numbers, moved relatives). Why does this matter and what is the correct response?","opts":["It is unimportant because staff know the patients well","Current emergency contact/treatment information drives disaster response continuity — so the facility updates records through a standing audit cycle with patient contact at each visit","It matters only for billing records","The fix is to delete the outdated contacts so no wrong numbers exist"],"ans":1,"expl":"KCER and CMS emergency-preparedness readiness depend on CURRENT patient emergency information — contacts, treatment prescriptions, access type, and backup-facility assignments — because disasters strike through stale data (unreachable next-of-kin, wrong dialysis prescriptions in shelters). A standing audit cadence (verify at each visit or monthly per policy) keeps the lifeline data current; staff familiarity fails when patients evacuate; deleting contacts removes the emergency pathway without replacing it.","obj":7}

];
const ORIG_FLASHCARDS = [{"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)", "back": "Sudden decline in kidney function over hours to days, characterized by increased creatinine and/or decreased urine output."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)", "back": "Progressive, irreversible loss of kidney function over 3+ months, classified into 5 stages based on GFR."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Prerenal AKI", "back": "Kidney dysfunction caused by decreased blood flow to the kidneys before the renal tissue itself is damaged."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Intrarenal AKI", "back": "Kidney damage occurring within the kidney tissue itself, often from toxins, ischemia, or inflammation."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Postrenal AKI", "back": "Kidney dysfunction caused by obstruction of urine flow after it leaves the kidneys."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)", "back": "Death of tubular epithelial cells, usually from ischemia or nephrotoxins, causing intrinsic AKI."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR)", "back": "Volume of fluid filtered by glomeruli per minute; best measure of overall kidney function."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Nephrotic Syndrome", "back": "Clinical syndrome with heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Nephritic Syndrome", "back": "Clinical syndrome with hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, and mild-to-moderate proteinuria."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "IgA Nephropathy", "back": "Most common glomerulonephritis; immune complex deposition in glomeruli causing hematuria and proteinuria."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Hyperkalemia (definition and risk)", "back": "Elevated serum potassium (>5.0 mEq/L); dangerous in CKD due to risk of cardiac arrhythmias."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Uremia", "back": "Clinical syndrome of kidney failure with accumulation of nitrogenous wastes causing nausea, confusion, and pericarditis."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Erythropoietin", "back": "Hormone produced by kidneys that stimulates red blood cell production; deficient in CKD."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Metabolic Acidosis in CKD (mechanism)", "back": "Low blood pH from impaired hydrogen ion excretion and bicarbonate regeneration by damaged kidneys."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Renal Replacement Therapy", "back": "Treatments that replace kidney function: hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis, or kidney transplantation."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Muddy Brown Casts", "back": "Granular casts in urine sediment pathognomonic for acute tubular necrosis."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Oliguria", "back": "Reduced urine output (<400 mL/day in adults); common sign of AKI."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Hyperphosphatemia", "back": "Elevated serum phosphate; common in CKD due to decreased excretion, leading to bone disease."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "Diabetic Nephropathy", "back": "Kidney damage from diabetes; leading cause of CKD and end-stage renal disease worldwide."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "front": "K/DOQI Guidelines", "back": "Clinical practice guidelines for CKD evaluation, classification, and management by National Kidney Foundation."}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Diffusion", "back": "Movement of solutes from high to low concentration across a semipermeable membrane; primary clearance mechanism in HD"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Convection", "back": "Solute movement with water flow across membrane (solvent drag); important for middle molecule clearance"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Ultrafiltration (hemodialysis)", "back": "Removal of excess fluid by applying hydrostatic pressure across the dialyzer membrane"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Kt/V", "back": "Measure of dialysis adequacy: K=clearance, t=time, V=volume of urea distribution; minimum 1.2 recommended"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Arteriovenous Fistula (AVF)", "back": "Surgical connection of artery to vein; gold standard access with lowest infection risk and best patency"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Arteriovenous Graft (AVG)", "back": "Synthetic tube connecting artery to vein; matures faster than AVF but higher infection and thrombosis risk"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Dialyzer", "back": "Artificial kidney containing semipermeable membrane where blood and dialysate flow in opposite directions"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Dialysate (hemodialysis)", "back": "Specially formulated solution containing electrolytes and bicarbonate that flows counter-current to blood in dialyzer"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Heparin", "back": "Anticoagulant used during HD to prevent clotting in extracorporeal circuit"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Intradialytic Hypotension", "back": "Most common HD complication; drop in BP during treatment due to rapid fluid removal or autonomic dysfunction"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome", "back": "Neurological complication from rapid solute removal causing cerebral edema; presents with headache, confusion, seizures"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Air Embolism", "back": "Life-threatening complication when air enters circulation; symptoms include chest pain, dyspnea; treat with left lateral decubitus position"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "High-Flux Dialyzer", "back": "Dialyzer with larger pores allowing better middle molecule clearance (e.g., beta-2 microglobulin)"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Thrill", "back": "Vibration felt over functioning AV access; absence suggests thrombosis or stenosis"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Bruit", "back": "Whooshing sound heard over AV access with stethoscope; indicates blood flow through access"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Steal Syndrome", "back": "Complication where access diverts blood flow away from hand, causing ischemia, pain, and coolness"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Dialysate Sodium", "back": "Typically 135-145 mEq/L; matches serum levels to prevent osmotic shifts and cramping"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Cool Dialysate", "back": "Temperature 35-36\u00b0C; promotes vasoconstriction and reduces intradialytic hypotension"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Beta-2 Microglobulin", "back": "Middle molecule toxin cleared better by high-flux dialyzers; accumulates in ESRD causing amyloidosis"}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "front": "Extracorporeal Circuit", "back": "Blood pathway outside body during HD: access \u2192 blood tubing \u2192 dialyzer \u2192 return to patient"}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Tenckhoff catheter", "back": "Most common PD catheter type; silicone tube with one or two cuffs for tissue ingrowth and infection barrier."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Exit-site infection", "back": "Infection at catheter skin exit characterized by erythema, tenderness, and/or purulent drainage."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Peritonitis (PD)", "back": "Inflammation of peritoneal membrane; diagnosed by cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, and/or WBC >100/\u03bcL in effluent."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET)", "back": "4-hour test measuring solute transport rate; classifies patients as high/low transporters to guide prescription."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "High transporter", "back": "Patient with rapid solute equilibration; loses osmotic gradient quickly, needs shorter dwells or icodextrin."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Low transporter", "back": "Patient with slow solute equilibration; benefits from longer dwells for adequate clearance."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "CAPD", "back": "Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis; manual exchanges 4-5 times daily without machine."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "CCPD", "back": "Continuous Cyclic Peritoneal Dialysis; machine-assisted overnight exchanges with daytime dwell."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Ultrafiltration (peritoneal dialysis)", "back": "Fluid removal across peritoneal membrane driven by osmotic gradient from glucose or icodextrin."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Ultrafiltration failure", "back": "Inability to achieve adequate fluid removal despite hypertonic exchanges; may indicate membrane changes."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Icodextrin", "back": "Glucose polymer osmotic agent for long dwells; maintains UF in high transporters and overnight."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Dwell time", "back": "Duration dialysate remains in peritoneal cavity; affects solute clearance and ultrafiltration volume."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Dialysate (peritoneal dialysis)", "back": "Sterile solution containing electrolytes, buffer (lactate/bicarbonate), and osmotic agent (glucose/icodextrin)."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Peritoneal membrane", "back": "Semipermeable membrane lining abdominal cavity; acts as dialysis barrier between blood and dialysate."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Catheter cuff", "back": "Dacron or polyester material promoting tissue ingrowth to secure catheter and prevent infection migration."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Break-in period", "back": "2-4 weeks post-catheter placement before full-volume PD; allows healing and reduces leak risk."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Residual renal function", "back": "Remaining native kidney function; contributes significantly to total clearance in early PD patients."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Peritoneal dialysis adequacy", "back": "Measured by Kt/V (target \u22651.7/week) and creatinine clearance (target \u226550 L/week/1.73m\u00b2)."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Encapsulating Peritoneal Sclerosis", "back": "Rare severe complication with fibrous bowel encapsulation causing obstruction; requires surgery."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "front": "Empiric peritonitis therapy", "back": "Initial antibiotics covering Gram-positive (vancomycin/cephalosporin) AND Gram-negative (ceftazidime/aminoglycoside)."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Hyperacute Rejection", "back": "Rejection occurring within minutes to hours due to pre-formed antibodies against donor antigens; causes immediate graft thrombosis and is prevented by proper crossmatch testing."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Delayed Graft Function (DGF)", "back": "Need for dialysis within the first week post-transplant; more common with deceased donors, prolonged cold ischemia time, and associated with increased acute rejection risk."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Calcineurin Inhibitor Nephrotoxicity", "back": "Dose-dependent kidney injury from tacrolimus or cyclosporine causing afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction; managed by therapeutic drug monitoring and dose adjustment."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "CMV D+/R- Risk", "back": "Highest risk CMV scenario: seronegative recipient receives organ from seropositive donor; requires antiviral prophylaxis (valganciclovir) for 3-6 months minimum."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "BK Virus Nephropathy", "back": "Polyomavirus infection of renal tubular cells causing interstitial nephritis and potential graft loss; screened via urine decoy cells or plasma viral load; treated by reducing immunosuppression."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Triple Immunosuppression", "back": "Standard maintenance regimen: calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus) + antimetabolite (mycophenolate) + corticosteroid; provides synergistic effect with reduced individual drug toxicity."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Transplant Glomerulopathy", "back": "Hallmark of chronic antibody-mediated rejection; characterized by glomerular basement membrane double contours on biopsy, often with C4d deposition."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Panel Reactive Antibody (PRA)", "back": "Percentage of HLA antigens against which a patient has pre-formed antibodies; high PRA (>80%) indicates sensitization and makes finding compatible donors more difficult."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Cold Ischemia Time", "back": "Duration kidney is preserved on ice between procurement and implantation; longer times (>24 hours) increase risk of DGF and may impact long-term graft survival."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Donor-Specific Antibody (DSA)", "back": "HLA antibodies in recipient directed against donor antigens; detected by Luminex assay; associated with antibody-mediated rejection and poorer graft outcomes."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "PJP Prophylaxis", "back": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole given for 6-12 months post-transplant to prevent Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia; also protects against Toxoplasma and some bacterial infections."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Post-Transplant Diabetes Mellitus (PTDM)", "back": "New-onset diabetes after transplantation; risk factors include tacrolimus, corticosteroids, obesity, and family history; requires glucose monitoring and may need insulin or oral agents."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Acute Cellular Rejection", "back": "T-cell mediated rejection typically occurring in first 3-6 months; diagnosed by biopsy showing tubulitis and interstitial inflammation; treated with pulse steroids."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Living Donor Advantage", "back": "Better outcomes than deceased donor due to HLA matching potential, shorter cold ischemia time, elective scheduling, and ability to transplant before dialysis dependence."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "mTOR Inhibitors", "back": "Sirolimus and everolimus block IL-2 signaling pathway; used as alternative to calcineurin inhibitors; side effects include impaired wound healing, proteinuria, and hyperlipidemia."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Crossmatch Test", "back": "Pre-transplant test mixing recipient serum with donor lymphocytes; positive result indicates pre-formed antibodies and contraindicates transplantation due to hyperacute rejection risk."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Death with Functioning Graft", "back": "Leading cause of graft loss in first year; primarily from cardiovascular disease and infection; reflects success of rejection prevention but challenges with immunosuppression complications."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "University of Wisconsin Solution", "back": "Standard preservation fluid for deceased donor kidneys; contains impermeants and substrates that minimize cellular injury during cold storage; allows 24-48 hour preservation."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "front": "Chronic Rejection", "back": "Slow graft deterioration over months to years; characterized by interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and/or transplant glomerulopathy; generally irreversible and leads to graft failure."}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Convection (CRRT)", "back": "Solute removal mechanism using hydrostatic pressure to create solvent drag across membrane; primary in CVVH"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Diffusion (CRRT)", "back": "Solute removal via concentration gradient between blood and dialysate; primary mechanism in CVVHD"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "CVVH", "back": "Continuous Veno-Venous Hemofiltration: uses convection with replacement fluid for solute clearance"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "CVVHD", "back": "Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodialysis: uses diffusion with dialysate flow for solute clearance"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "CVVHDF", "back": "Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodiafiltration: combines convection and diffusion for maximal clearance"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "SLED", "back": "Sustained Low-Efficiency Dialysis: extended intermittent RRT (6-12 hrs) with lower flows than conventional HD"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "KDIGO CRRT Dose", "back": "Recommended delivered dose: 20-25 mL/kg/hr for critically ill AKI patients"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Citrate Anticoagulation", "back": "Regional anticoagulation via calcium chelation in circuit; reduces bleeding risk vs systemic heparin"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Citrate Toxicity Signs", "back": "Hypocalcemia, metabolic alkalosis, elevated total-to-ionized calcium ratio (>2.5); seen in liver failure"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Preferred CRRT Access", "back": "Right internal jugular vein: straight path, lower infection risk, preserves subclavian for future fistula"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Transmembrane Pressure (TMP)", "back": "Pressure gradient across filter membrane; rising TMP indicates filter clotting or fouling"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Effluent Flow Rate", "back": "Total waste fluid rate (ultrafiltrate + dialysate + net removal); determines prescribed CRRT dose"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "CRRT Blood Flow Rate", "back": "Typical range: 100-200 mL/min, lower than conventional HD for hemodynamic stability"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Hyperkalemia Management (CRRT)", "back": "CRRT indication in AKI; monitor for rapid shifts during initiation to prevent arrhythmias"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "CRRT-Associated Hypophosphatemia", "back": "Common during prolonged CRRT due to inadequate phosphate clearance; requires supplementation"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Bicarbonate Buffer (CRRT)", "back": "Standard modern CRRT buffer; more physiologic than lactate, better hemodynamic stability"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "CRRT Downtime Impact", "back": "Circuit clotting, transport, procedures reduce delivered dose 15-30% below prescribed"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Hyponatremia Correction Limit", "back": "Do not increase serum Na+ >10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Hypernatremia Correction Limit", "back": "Do not decrease serum Na+ >10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours to avoid cerebral edema"}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "front": "Sepsis-Induced AKI RRT Indication", "back": "Life-threatening complications: severe acidosis, hyperkalemia, refractory volume overload, uremia"}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Erythropoietin (EPO) deficiency in CKD", "back": "Damaged kidneys produce less EPO, causing normocytic normochromic anemia. Treated with ESAs and iron supplementation."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "CKD-MBD (Chronic Kidney Disease-Mineral and Bone Disorder)", "back": "Systemic disorder of mineral metabolism (calcium, phosphate, PTH, vitamin D) causing bone abnormalities and vascular calcification."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Secondary Hyperparathyroidism", "back": "Elevated PTH due to kidney's inability to activate vitamin D and excrete phosphate, causing low calcium that stimulates parathyroid glands."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Adynamic Bone Disease", "back": "Low bone turnover state with suppressed PTH (<150 pg/mL). Risk factors: overuse of vitamin D analogs, calcium-based binders, diabetes."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Metabolic Acidosis in CKD (features and consequences)", "back": "Normal anion gap acidosis from decreased acid excretion and bicarbonate regeneration. Accelerates CKD progression and causes bone buffering."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Hyperkalemia threshold requiring intervention", "back": "Potassium >5.5 mEq/L requires dietary modification; >6.0 mEq/L is dangerous and may need emergency treatment (calcium, insulin/glucose, dialysis)."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Phosphate binder timing", "back": "Must be taken WITH meals to bind dietary phosphate in the gut. Taking between meals is ineffective."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Calcimimetics (e.g., cinacalcet)", "back": "Medications that increase calcium-sensing receptor sensitivity, lowering PTH, calcium, and phosphate. Used in secondary hyperparathyroidism."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Vitamin D activation pathway", "back": "Skin/supplements (D3/D2) \u2192 Liver (25-OH-D) \u2192 Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase (1,25-OH-D/calcitriol). Kidney step impaired in CKD."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Protein-energy wasting (PEW)", "back": "Loss of protein and energy reserves in CKD. Causes: uremic toxins, inflammation, acidosis, anorexia. Increases mortality risk."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Dyslipidemia pattern in CKD", "back": "High triglycerides, low HDL, normal or mildly elevated LDL. Caused by decreased lipoprotein lipase activity and impaired clearance."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Cardiovascular risk in CKD", "back": "CKD is an independent CVD risk factor. Mechanisms: hypertension, anemia, vascular calcification, inflammation, endothelial dysfunction."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Uremic pruritus", "back": "Generalized itching in advanced CKD/dialysis. Related to uremic toxins, xerosis, immune activation. Treat with emollients, gabapentin, optimized dialysis."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Target hemoglobin with ESA therapy", "back": "10-11 g/dL. Avoid >11.5 g/dL due to increased stroke, thrombosis, and mortality risk without QOL benefit."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Functional iron deficiency", "back": "Adequate iron stores (normal/high ferritin) but unavailable for erythropoiesis (low TSAT). Common in CKD due to inflammation/hepcidin."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Vascular calcification in CKD", "back": "Calcium-phosphate deposition in arterial media. Accelerated by hyperphosphatemia, high calcium load, vitamin D analogs. Increases CV mortality."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Diabetic nephropathy progression", "back": "Hyperglycemia \u2192 glomerular hyperfiltration \u2192 microalbuminuria \u2192 macroalbuminuria \u2192 declining GFR \u2192 ESRD. ACEi/ARB slow progression."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Sodium bicarbonate therapy indication", "back": "Serum bicarbonate <22 mEq/L. Goal: 22-26 mEq/L. Slows CKD progression, improves bone health, reduces protein catabolism."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "FGF23 in CKD", "back": "Fibroblast growth factor 23 rises early to increase phosphate excretion. High levels predict LVH, cardiovascular events, and mortality."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "front": "Osteitis fibrosa cystica", "back": "High bone turnover disease from severe secondary hyperparathyroidism. Features: elevated PTH, bone pain, fractures, brown tumors."}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Nephrotic Syndrome Diagnostic Criteria (Children)", "back": "Heavy proteinuria (UPCR >2.0 mg/mg or >40 mg/m2/hr), hypoalbuminemia (<2.5 g/dL), edema, and often hyperlipidemia"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Most Common Cause of Nephrotic Syndrome in Children", "back": "Minimal change disease (80-90% of cases in children ages 1-10 years)"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Classic HUS Triad", "back": "Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Typical HUS Pathogen", "back": "Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), most commonly O157:H7"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Posterior Urethral Valves (PUV)", "back": "Congenital obstructing membranes in the posterior urethra of males; most common cause of bladder outlet obstruction in male infants"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Preferred Dialysis Modality in Infants", "back": "Peritoneal dialysis - provides gentle continuous ultrafiltration, better growth outcomes, and easier home management"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "CAKUT", "back": "Congenital Anomalies of the Kidney and Urinary Tract - most common birth defects affecting kidneys, including hydronephrosis, renal agenesis, and obstructive uropathies"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Vesicoureteral Reflux (VUR) Grading", "back": "Grade I-V: I (ureter only), II (ureter and pelvis without dilation), III (mild dilation), IV (moderate dilation with tortuosity), V (severe dilation with loss of papillary impressions)"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Steroid-Sensitive Nephrotic Syndrome", "back": "Achieves remission (proteinuria <4 mg/m2/hr or trace/negative dipstick) within 8 weeks of daily prednisone therapy"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Steroid-Resistant Nephrotic Syndrome", "back": "No remission after 8 weeks of daily prednisone; requires biopsy and consideration of alternative immunosuppression"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Growth Failure in Pediatric CKD", "back": "Multifactorial: inadequate nutrition, metabolic acidosis, CKD-mineral bone disorder, growth hormone resistance, and delayed puberty"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Maintenance Fluid Calculation (Holliday-Segar)", "back": "100 mL/kg (first 10 kg) + 50 mL/kg (next 10 kg) + 20 mL/kg (each kg over 20 kg)"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Acute Kidney Injury in Children (KDIGO Criteria)", "back": "Increase in serum creatinine \u22650.3 mg/dL within 48 hours, OR increase to \u22651.5 times baseline within 7 days, OR urine output <0.5 mL/kg/hr for 6 hours"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Hyperkalemia Emergency Treatment (Children)", "back": "Calcium gluconate (membrane stabilization), insulin with glucose (shifts K+ into cells), albuterol, sodium bicarbonate, and dialysis if severe"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Renal Tubular Acidosis Type 1 (Distal)", "back": "Defect in distal acid secretion; causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia, nephrocalcinosis, and kidney stones"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Renal Tubular Acidosis Type 2 (Proximal)", "back": "Defect in proximal bicarbonate reabsorption; causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia, often part of Fanconi syndrome"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Renal Tubular Acidosis Type 4", "back": "Aldosterone deficiency or resistance; causes hyperkalemic normal anion gap metabolic acidosis"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Anemia Management in Pediatric CKD", "back": "Target hemoglobin 11-12 g/dL; treat with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron supplementation (ferritin >100 ng/mL, TSAT >20%)"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "CKD-Mineral Bone Disorder in Children", "back": "Abnormalities in calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and vitamin D metabolism; requires phosphate binders, vitamin D analogs, and dietary management"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "front": "Indications for Dialysis in Children", "back": "Volume overload refractory to diuretics, severe electrolyte abnormalities, uremic symptoms, severe acidosis, or toxin removal"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Normal serum creatinine in pregnancy", "back": "0.4-0.8 mg/dL (lower than non-pregnant due to 40-50% increase in GFR)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Definition of chronic hypertension in pregnancy", "back": "BP \u2265140/90 mmHg present before pregnancy or diagnosed before 20 weeks gestation"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Definition of gestational hypertension", "back": "New-onset BP \u2265140/90 mmHg after 20 weeks gestation without proteinuria or other preeclampsia features"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Diagnostic criteria for preeclampsia", "back": "BP \u2265140/90 after 20 weeks PLUS proteinuria (\u2265300 mg/24hr) OR severe features (thrombocytopenia, elevated creatinine, elevated LFTs, pulmonary edema, cerebral/visual symptoms)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "HELLP syndrome components", "back": "Hemolysis (schistocytes, elevated LDH), Elevated Liver enzymes (AST/ALT), Low Platelets (<100,000/mcL)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "First-line antihypertensives in pregnancy", "back": "Labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Antihypertensives contraindicated in pregnancy", "back": "ACE inhibitors, ARBs, direct renin inhibitors (cause fetal renal toxicity, oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Target BP in pregnant patients with CKD", "back": "Below 140/90 mmHg (avoid aggressive lowering that may compromise placental perfusion)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Risk of superimposed preeclampsia in CKD patients", "back": "25-50% depending on baseline kidney function and proteinuria level"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Most common cause of AKI in pregnancy", "back": "Preeclampsia/HELLP syndrome (50-60% of cases)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Creatinine level concerning for AKI in pregnancy", "back": "\u22651.0 mg/dL or rise of \u22650.3 mg/dL from baseline (remember baseline is lower in pregnancy)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Treatment of pyelonephritis in pregnancy", "back": "Hospital admission + IV antibiotics (ceftriaxone or cefepime) due to high risk of sepsis and preterm labor"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Safe antibiotics for UTI in pregnancy", "back": "Cephalexin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, nitrofurantoin (avoid in first trimester and near term), fosfomycin"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Antibiotics to avoid in pregnancy for UTI", "back": "Fluoroquinolones (cartilage damage), tetracyclines (tooth discoloration), TMP-SMX (first trimester and near term)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Dialysis prescription in pregnancy", "back": "Intensive: daily or near-daily HD totaling 20+ hours/week to maintain BUN <50 mg/dL"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Target BUN in pregnant dialysis patients", "back": "Below 50 mg/dL (ideally <40 mg/dL) to reduce risk of polyhydramnios and improve fetal outcomes"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Preterm delivery rate in pregnant dialysis patients", "back": "50-80% (most deliver before 34 weeks)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "When is pregnancy safest in lupus nephritis?", "back": "When disease has been quiescent for at least 6 months with stable kidney function"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Low-dose aspirin in pregnant CKD patients", "back": "81 mg daily starting at 12 weeks to reduce preeclampsia risk (recommended by ACOG and KDIGO)"}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "front": "Long-term risk after preeclampsia", "back": "Increased risk of chronic hypertension, CKD, and cardiovascular disease - requires lifelong monitoring"}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Renal clearance", "back": "The volume of plasma from which a drug is completely removed by the kidneys per unit time; determines if dose adjustment is needed in CKD."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Dialyzable drug", "back": "A medication that can be removed by hemodialysis, typically water-soluble, low protein binding, and small molecular weight."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Nephrotoxic drug", "back": "A medication that can cause kidney damage, such as NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, or contrast dye."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Loading dose", "back": "An initial higher dose to rapidly achieve therapeutic drug levels; usually unchanged in renal failure since it depends on volume of distribution."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Maintenance dose", "back": "The ongoing dose that maintains therapeutic drug levels; typically reduced in renal failure to prevent drug accumulation."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) - nephrotoxic drugs", "back": "Measuring drug blood levels to guide dosing, essential for narrow therapeutic index drugs like vancomycin, gentamicin, and digoxin in CKD."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Contrast-induced nephropathy (CIN)", "back": "Acute kidney injury occurring 48-72 hours after iodinated contrast administration; prevented with IV hydration in at-risk CKD patients."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Lactic acidosis (metformin in renal failure)", "back": "A rare but serious complication of metformin accumulation in renal failure, with mortality up to 50% if severe."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Hyperkalemia (drug-induced causes in CKD)", "back": "Elevated serum potassium; a risk with ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium-sparing diuretics, and trimethoprim in CKD patients."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Extended-interval dosing", "back": "Giving drugs less frequently but at similar doses in renal failure; commonly used for aminoglycosides and vancomycin."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Protein binding", "back": "The degree to which a drug binds to plasma proteins; highly protein-bound drugs (>90%) are generally not dialyzable."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Volume of distribution (Vd)", "back": "The theoretical volume in which a drug is distributed; drugs with large Vd are less dialyzable as they reside in tissues."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "GFR threshold for metformin", "back": "Metformin should be discontinued when eGFR falls below 30 mL/min/1.73m\u00b2 due to lactic acidosis risk."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Vancomycin trough target", "back": "Typical target trough levels are 10-20 mcg/mL depending on infection severity; monitored closely in renal failure."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Aminoglycoside toxicity", "back": "Ototoxicity (hearing loss, vertigo) and nephrotoxicity (acute tubular necrosis); risk increased with accumulation in renal failure."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "NSAID mechanism of nephrotoxicity", "back": "Inhibition of renal prostaglandins causes afferent arteriole vasoconstriction, reducing renal blood flow and GFR."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Post-dialysis dosing", "back": "Administering medications after hemodialysis to replace drug removed during the dialysis session."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Opioid metabolites in renal failure", "back": "Morphine and codeine produce active metabolites that accumulate in CKD, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Allopurinol hypersensitivity", "back": "Severe rash and organ involvement risk increased in CKD; start low (50-100mg) and screen for HLA-B*5801 in high-risk populations."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "front": "Baclofen in CKD", "back": "Renally eliminated muscle relaxant that can cause severe CNS toxicity in CKD; avoid or use with extreme dose reduction."}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Metabolic acidosis", "back": "Low pH (<7.35) and low bicarbonate (<22 mEq/L) due to acid accumulation or bicarbonate loss"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Respiratory acidosis", "back": "Low pH (<7.35) and high PaCO2 (>45 mmHg) due to inadequate alveolar ventilation"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Metabolic alkalosis", "back": "High pH (>7.45) and high bicarbonate (>26 mEq/L) due to acid loss or bicarbonate gain"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Respiratory alkalosis", "back": "High pH (>7.45) and low PaCO2 (<35 mmHg) due to excessive alveolar ventilation"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Anion gap", "back": "Calculated as Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-); normal range 8-12 mEq/L; helps classify metabolic acidosis"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Winter's formula", "back": "Expected PaCO2 = 1.5 \u00d7 HCO3- + 8 (\u00b12); assesses respiratory compensation in metabolic acidosis"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Type 1 RTA (distal)", "back": "Defective H+ secretion in distal tubule; urine pH >5.5; associated with hypokalemia and nephrolithiasis"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Type 2 RTA (proximal)", "back": "Impaired bicarbonate reabsorption in proximal tubule; urine pH variable; associated with Fanconi syndrome"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Type 4 RTA", "back": "Aldosterone deficiency or resistance; hyperkalemia; impaired ammonium excretion; urine pH <5.5"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "High anion gap metabolic acidosis causes", "back": "MUDPILES: Methanol, Uremia, DKA, Propylene glycol, Isoniazid/Iron, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis causes", "back": "HARDASS: Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, RTA, Diarrhea, Addison's disease, Saline infusion, Spironolactone"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Lactic acidosis (types and anion gap)", "back": "Accumulation of lactate due to tissue hypoxia (Type A) or metabolic causes (Type B); anion gap >12"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)", "back": "Hyperglycemia, ketonemia, metabolic acidosis; anion gap elevated; treated with fluids and insulin"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Compensation", "back": "Physiologic response to primary acid-base disorder; respiratory compensation is rapid, renal compensation takes days"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Acute respiratory acidosis compensation", "back": "Bicarbonate increases 1 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg PaCO2 increase above 40 mmHg"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Chronic respiratory acidosis compensation", "back": "Bicarbonate increases 3-4 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg PaCO2 increase above 40 mmHg"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Acute respiratory alkalosis compensation", "back": "Bicarbonate decreases 2 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg PaCO2 decrease below 40 mmHg"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Chronic respiratory alkalosis compensation", "back": "Bicarbonate decreases 4-5 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg PaCO2 decrease below 40 mmHg"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Bicarbonate therapy in CKD", "back": "Indicated when serum bicarbonate <22 mEq/L; target 22-26 mEq/L per KDIGO guidelines"}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "front": "Saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis", "back": "Urine chloride <10 mEq/L; caused by vomiting, NG suction, or diuretics; treated with normal saline"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hyperkalemia ECG progression", "back": "Peaked T waves \u2192 PR prolongation \u2192 QRS widening \u2192 Sine wave \u2192 Ventricular fibrillation/asystole"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Normal potassium range", "back": "3.5-5.0 mEq/L; levels above 6.5 mEq/L or with ECG changes constitute emergency"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Calcium gluconate in hyperkalemia", "back": "Stabilizes cardiac membranes within minutes; does not lower potassium; effect lasts 30-60 minutes"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Insulin-dextrose protocol", "back": "10 units regular insulin IV with 25g dextrose; lowers K+ by 0.5-1.5 mEq/L in 15-30 min; lasts 4-6 hours"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Osmotic demyelination syndrome", "back": "Neurologic injury from rapid hyponatremia correction; limit sodium increase to 8-10 mEq/L in 24 hours"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "SIADH diagnostic criteria", "back": "Hyponatremia, low serum osmolality, inappropriately high urine osmolality (>100 mOsm/kg), euvolemia, normal thyroid/adrenal function"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypernatremia causes", "back": "Water loss (diabetes insipidus, osmotic diuresis), sodium gain (hypertonic saline), or inadequate water intake"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypocalcemia symptoms", "back": "Perioral paresthesias, muscle cramps, tetany, Chvostek sign, Trousseau sign, prolonged QT, seizures"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypercalcemia emergency treatment", "back": "IV isotonic saline for volume expansion, calcitonin for rapid effect, bisphosphonates for sustained reduction, dialysis if severe"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Normal calcium range", "back": "Total calcium 8.5-10.5 mg/dL; ionized calcium 4.5-5.6 mg/dL; correct total for albumin"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypermagnesemia causes", "back": "Renal failure with magnesium-containing antacids/laxatives, preeclampsia treatment with magnesium sulfate"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypermagnesemia symptoms", "back": "Loss of deep tendon reflexes (first sign), hypotension, bradycardia, respiratory depression, cardiac arrest"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypermagnesemia treatment", "back": "IV calcium gluconate to antagonize effects, IV fluids with loop diuretics, dialysis for severe cases or renal failure"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hyperphosphatemia complications", "back": "Hypocalcemia, metastatic calcification, secondary hyperparathyroidism, increased cardiovascular mortality in CKD"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Phosphate binders", "back": "Calcium acetate, sevelamer, lanthanum; taken with meals to bind dietary phosphate in CKD patients"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Fluid overload assessment", "back": "Weight gain, edema, jugular venous distension, crackles, dyspnea, orthopnea, S3 heart sound, elevated BNP"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Pulmonary edema emergency management", "back": "Sit upright, high-flow oxygen, IV loop diuretics, nitrates for preload reduction, noninvasive ventilation if needed"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hypomagnesemia ECG changes", "back": "Prolonged QT, prolonged PR, ST depression, T wave flattening, predisposition to torsades de pointes"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Diabetes insipidus types", "back": "Central (ADH deficiency), nephrogenic (renal resistance to ADH), gestational, dipsogenic; all cause polyuria and hypernatremia risk"}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "front": "Hyperkalemia risk medications", "back": "ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, beta-blockers, digoxin toxicity, succinylcholine"}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Calcineurin Inhibitors (CNIs)", "back": "Immunosuppressants (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) that block calcineurin, preventing IL-2 production and T-cell activation; require therapeutic drug monitoring due to nephrotoxicity risk."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Tacrolimus (FK506)", "back": "Potent calcineurin inhibitor binding FKBP-12; narrow therapeutic index; side effects include nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, hyperglycemia, and new-onset diabetes after transplant."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Cyclosporine", "back": "Calcineurin inhibitor that binds cyclophilin; distinctive side effects include gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, and hypertension; more nephrotoxic than tacrolimus."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Mycophenolate Mofetil (MMF)", "back": "Antimetabolite inhibiting inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase; blocks purine synthesis in lymphocytes; common side effects include GI upset, leukopenia, and increased infection risk."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Azathioprine", "back": "Prodrug converted to 6-mercaptopurine; inhibits DNA synthesis in proliferating cells; causes bone marrow suppression; largely replaced by MMF but still used in some protocols."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Sirolimus (Rapamycin)", "back": "mTOR inhibitor blocking cell cycle progression; side effects include hyperlipidemia, thrombocytopenia, impaired wound healing, and proteinuria; not nephrotoxic."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Basiliximab", "back": "Chimeric monoclonal antibody against IL-2 receptor alpha chain (CD25); used for induction therapy; prevents T-cell activation without causing cytokine release syndrome."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Antithymocyte Globulin (ATG)", "back": "Polyclonal antibody causing T-cell depletion via complement-mediated lysis; used for induction in high-risk patients or treatment of acute rejection; requires premedication."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Prednisone", "back": "Corticosteroid with broad anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects; long-term use causes osteoporosis, weight gain, hyperglycemia, hypertension, and adrenal suppression."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) - transplant immunosuppressants", "back": "Regular measurement of blood drug levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine, sirolimus) to maintain therapeutic range while minimizing toxicity; trough levels typically drawn 12 hours post-dose."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "CYP3A4 Inhibitors", "back": "Drugs that increase calcineurin inhibitor levels (e.g., fluconazole, clarithromycin, diltiazem, grapefruit); require dose reduction and close monitoring."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "CYP3A4 Inducers", "back": "Drugs that decrease calcineurin inhibitor levels (e.g., rifampin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, St. John's wort); may cause subtherapeutic levels and rejection risk."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Nephrotoxicity (CNI-induced)", "back": "Dose-dependent kidney injury from calcineurin inhibitors via afferent arteriole vasoconstriction; manifests as elevated creatinine; managed by dose reduction or conversion to alternative agents."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "New-Onset Diabetes After Transplant (NODAT)", "back": "Post-transplant hyperglycemia often caused by tacrolimus or steroids; occurs in 10-20% of recipients; requires glucose monitoring and possible medication adjustment."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Induction Therapy", "back": "Intense immunosuppression given at transplant to prevent early acute rejection; agents include basiliximab (IL-2 antagonist) or ATG (T-cell depleting antibody)."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Maintenance Immunosuppression", "back": "Long-term medication regimen to prevent rejection; typically includes a calcineurin inhibitor, antimetabolite (MMF or azathioprine), and often low-dose steroids."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Gingival Hyperplasia", "back": "Overgrowth of gum tissue occurring in up to 30% of cyclosporine patients; rare with tacrolimus; managed with dental care or medication switch."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Hyperlipidemia (Transplant-related)", "back": "Elevated cholesterol and triglycerides common with sirolimus and steroids; requires lipid monitoring and may need statin therapy."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Bone Marrow Suppression", "back": "Reduced blood cell production caused by MMF and azathioprine; manifests as leukopenia, anemia, or thrombocytopenia; requires CBC monitoring and possible dose adjustment."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "front": "Live Vaccines Contraindication", "back": "Live attenuated vaccines (MMR, varicella, nasal influenza) are contraindicated in transplant patients on immunosuppression due to risk of vaccine-derived infection."}];
const ORIG_LESSONS = [{"topic": "Kidney Disease", "title": "Understanding Acute Kidney Injury: Types and Causes", "body": "Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) is classified into three categories based on the location of the problem. Prerenal AKI occurs when blood flow to the kidneys is reduced before reaching the renal tissue. Common causes include hypotension, heart failure, sepsis, dehydration, and hemorrhage. The kidney tissue itself is initially healthy but cannot filter properly without adequate perfusion. Intrarenal (intrinsic) AKI involves direct damage to the kidney tissue itself. The most common form is acute tubular necrosis (ATN), caused by prolonged ischemia or nephrotoxic substances like contrast dye, aminoglycoside antibiotics, or NSAIDs. Other intrarenal causes include glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis, and vascular diseases. Postrenal AKI results from obstruction of urine flow after it leaves the kidneys. Causes include kidney stones, bladder tumors, prostate enlargement, ureteral strictures, or blood clots. Early recognition of the type is critical because treatment differs: prerenal requires volume resuscitation and improving perfusion, intrarenal needs removal of offending agents and supportive care, and postrenal requires relieving the obstruction. Nurses should monitor urine output, serum creatinine trends, and urine sediment findings to help identify the type and guide interventions."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "title": "Chronic Kidney Disease: Staging and Management", "body": "Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is classified into five stages based on glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Stage 1 (GFR \u226590) has normal or high GFR with kidney damage markers like proteinuria. Stage 2 (GFR 60-89) shows mild reduction. Stage 3 (GFR 30-59) is moderate reduction, often when symptoms begin appearing. Stage 4 (GFR 15-29) is severe reduction requiring preparation for renal replacement therapy. Stage 5 (GFR <15) is kidney failure requiring dialysis or transplantation. Key management principles include blood pressure control (target <130/80 mmHg), preferably with ACE inhibitors or ARBs which also reduce proteinuria. Glycemic control is essential for diabetic patients. Dietary modifications include sodium restriction (<2g/day), protein moderation (0.6-0.8g/kg/day in stages 3-5), potassium restriction if hyperkalemic, and phosphate restriction with binders if hyperphosphatemic. Anemia management involves erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron supplementation. Bone-mineral disorder is managed with vitamin D analogs, phosphate binders, and calcimimetics. Regular monitoring includes GFR, electrolytes, hemoglobin, calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio. Patient education focuses on medication adherence, dietary compliance, recognizing complications, and understanding treatment options including dialysis modalities and transplantation."}, {"topic": "Kidney Disease", "title": "Glomerular Diseases: Nephrotic vs Nephritic Syndromes", "body": "Glomerular diseases present as either nephrotic or nephritic syndromes, distinguished by their clinical features and underlying pathology. Nephrotic syndrome results from increased glomerular permeability to proteins. Classic findings include heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia (<3.0g/dL), generalized edema (often periorbital and dependent), hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria. Common causes include minimal change disease (common in children), focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, membranous nephropathy, and diabetic nephropathy. Complications include increased infection risk (loss of immunoglobulins), thromboembolism (loss of antithrombin III), and accelerated atherosclerosis. Treatment involves corticosteroids or immunosuppressants for primary causes, ACE inhibitors/ARBs to reduce proteinuria, diuretics for edema, statins for hyperlipidemia, and anticoagulation if albumin is very low. Nephritic syndrome involves glomerular inflammation with capillary wall damage. Hallmark findings include hematuria with dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts, hypertension, oliguria, mild-to-moderate proteinuria (<3.5g/day), and azotemia. Causes include IgA nephropathy (most common worldwide), post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, lupus nephritis, and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. Management focuses on treating the underlying cause, blood pressure control, sodium and fluid restriction for edema, and immunosuppression for autoimmune causes. Urinalysis showing RBC casts is pathognomonic for glomerulonephritis and distinguishes nephritic from nephrotic presentation. Both syndromes require nephrology consultation and often kidney biopsy for definitive diagnosis."}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "title": "Principles of Hemodialysis: Diffusion, Convection, and Ultrafiltration", "body": "Hemodialysis relies on three key principles to remove waste and excess fluid from the blood. DIFFUSION is the primary mechanism, where solutes move from areas of high concentration (blood) to low concentration (dialysate) across a semipermeable membrane. Small molecules like urea, creatinine, and potassium are efficiently cleared this way. CONVECTION occurs when solutes are dragged along with water as it moves across the membrane due to pressure gradients, particularly important for clearing middle-sized molecules. ULTRAFILTRATION is the controlled removal of excess fluid by applying hydrostatic pressure, forcing plasma water through the membrane pores. The dialyzer membrane's characteristics determine what can pass through: pore size controls the molecular weight cutoff, typically allowing molecules under 50,000 Daltons while retaining albumin and blood cells. High-flux membranes have larger pores for better middle molecule clearance. Blood and dialysate flow in opposite directions (counter-current) to maximize the concentration gradient throughout the dialyzer length."}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "title": "Vascular Access for Hemodialysis: Types, Care, and Complications", "body": "Vascular access is the lifeline for hemodialysis patients. There are three main types: ARTERIOVENOUS FISTULA (AVF), ARTERIOVENOUS GRAFT (AVG), and CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETER. AVF is created by surgically connecting an artery to a vein, usually in the arm. It requires 3-6 months to mature but offers the best long-term patency and lowest infection risk, making it the gold standard. A functioning AVF has a palpable thrill and audible bruit. AVG uses a synthetic tube to connect artery and vein, maturing in 2-4 weeks but with higher thrombosis and infection rates. Catheters (tunneled or non-tunneled) provide immediate access but carry the highest infection and thrombosis risk, reserved for temporary use. COMPLICATIONS include: thrombosis (loss of thrill/bruit), stenosis (reduced flow, prolonged bleeding after needle removal), infection (redness, warmth, fever), aneurysm formation (repeated cannulation sites), and steal syndrome (hand ischemia from diverted blood flow). Proper care includes daily inspection, avoiding blood pressure measurements or IVs in the access arm, and immediate reporting of any changes in thrill or bruit."}, {"topic": "Hemodialysis", "title": "Intradialytic Complications: Recognition and Management", "body": "Several complications can occur during hemodialysis treatments. INTRADIALYTIC HYPOTENSION is the most common, affecting 20-30% of treatments. Causes include rapid ultrafiltration, autonomic dysfunction, and cardiac issues. Management includes stopping ultrafiltration, placing patient in Trendelenburg position, administering saline bolus, and using cool dialysate for prevention. MUSCLE CRAMPS occur in 5-20% of treatments, typically from rapid volume depletion. Treatment includes stretching, hypertonic saline, or reducing ultrafiltration rate. DIALYSIS DISEQUILIBRIUM SYNDROME results from rapid solute removal causing cerebral edema, presenting with headache, nausea, confusion, and potentially seizures. Prevention involves slower, more frequent treatments for new patients. AIR EMBOLISM is rare but life-threatening, occurring when air enters the venous line. Symptoms include sudden chest pain, dyspnea, cough, and cardiovascular collapse. Immediate management: clamp venous line, place patient in left lateral decubitus with head down (Durant's maneuver), administer 100% oxygen, and call for emergency support. Other complications include hemolysis (from overheated dialysate or chloramine exposure), allergic reactions to dialyzer membrane, and bleeding from excessive anticoagulation."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "title": "PD Catheter Placement and Break-In", "body": "Peritoneal dialysis requires secure vascular access via a PD catheter. The Tenckhoff catheter is the most widely used design, featuring a silicone tube with one or two cuffs made of Dacron or polyester. The cuffs promote tissue ingrowth, anchoring the catheter and creating a barrier against bacterial migration. Catheters can be placed surgically (open or laparoscopic), percutaneously, or with fluoroscopic guidance. Laparoscopic placement allows direct visualization, omentopexy if needed, and concurrent hernia repair. Catheter types include standard Tenckhoff (coiled or straight tip), presternal catheters (exit site on chest wall for patients with abdominal ostomies or severe obesity), and moncrief-popovich catheters (external segment buried until use). Proper catheter positioning places the tip in the pelvis (pouch of Douglas) to ensure complete drainage. Break-in period of 2-4 weeks with low-volume exchanges allows healing and reduces pericatheter leak risk. Exit-site should be oriented downward or laterally to minimize trauma and contamination risk."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "title": "Peritoneal Membrane Transport and the PET", "body": "The Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET) characterizes individual membrane transport properties to optimize PD prescriptions. During PET, a standard 2.5% dextrose exchange is performed with dialysate and blood samples collected at specified intervals. The dialysate-to-plasma (D/P) creatinine ratio at 4 hours determines transporter status: high (>0.81), high-average (0.65-0.81), low-average (0.49-0.64), or low (<0.49). High transporters rapidly equilibrate solutes but quickly lose the osmotic gradient, resulting in poor ultrafiltration on long dwells. They benefit from shorter dwell times, automated PD (APD), or icodextrin for long dwells. Low transporters have slower solute clearance but maintain UF longer; they need extended dwell times and may require higher glucose concentrations. Understanding transporter status guides modality selection (CAPD vs. APD), dwell times, and dialysate composition. Repeat PET annually or when clinical changes occur (UF failure, inadequate clearance)."}, {"topic": "Peritoneal Dialysis", "title": "Peritonitis Recognition and Management", "body": "Peritonitis is the most serious common complication of PD, requiring prompt recognition and treatment. Diagnostic criteria include at least two of: cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, and/or effluent WBC >100/\u03bcL with >50% neutrophils. Gram stain and culture should be obtained before antibiotics. Empiric therapy must cover both Gram-positive organisms (vancomycin 15-30 mg/kg IP or cephalosporin) and Gram-negative organisms (ceftazidime 1g IP or aminoglycoside). Intraperitoneal administration achieves superior peritoneal tissue levels compared to IV. Treatment continues for 2-3 weeks depending on organism. Catheter removal is indicated for refractory peritonitis (no improvement after 5 days), fungal peritonitis, relapsing peritonitis, or severe exit-site/tunnel infection. Prevention focuses on proper exchange technique, exit-site care with topical mupirocin or gentamicin, and patient education. Recurrent peritonitis may indicate biofilm formation, requiring catheter removal. Early recognition and appropriate antibiotic selection preserve membrane function and prevent complications like encapsulating peritoneal sclerosis."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "title": "Types of Kidney Transplant Rejection", "body": "Kidney transplant rejection is classified by timing and mechanism. Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours due to pre-existing antibodies against donor HLA antigens, causing immediate thrombosis and graft loss. This is now rare due to mandatory crossmatch testing. Acute rejection typically occurs within the first 3-6 months and has two forms: acute cellular rejection (T-cell mediated, showing tubulitis on biopsy) and acute antibody-mediated rejection (donor-specific antibodies causing complement activation). Both are treatable with intensified immunosuppression if caught early. Chronic rejection develops over months to years and is characterized by progressive interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and transplant glomerulopathy. Chronic antibody-mediated rejection shows glomerular basement membrane double contours and C4d deposition. Unlike acute rejection, chronic changes are largely irreversible and represent the leading cause of late graft loss. Prevention through consistent immunosuppression adherence and regular monitoring is critical."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "title": "Immunosuppressive Medication Management", "body": "Kidney transplant recipients require lifelong immunosuppression to prevent rejection. Standard maintenance therapy uses triple drug regimens: a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus preferred over cyclosporine), an antimetabolite (mycophenolate mofetil or mycophenolic acid), and corticosteroids. Tacrolimus requires therapeutic drug monitoring with trough levels typically targeted at 8-12 ng/mL in early post-transplant period and 5-8 ng/mL long-term, though targets vary by center and patient risk. Mycophenolate inhibits lymphocyte proliferation through IMPDH inhibition and does not require routine level monitoring. Corticosteroids are often tapered to low doses or discontinued in select patients but provide important anti-inflammatory effects. Induction therapy with basiliximab (IL-2 receptor antagonist) or antithymocyte globulin (T-cell depleting antibody) is given at transplant to provide intense early immunosuppression while allowing delayed introduction of nephrotoxic calcineurin inhibitors. Key nursing considerations include medication timing consistency, recognition of toxicity signs (tremor, hyperglycemia, nephrotoxicity), drug interaction awareness (CYP3A4 inhibitors/inducers affect tacrolimus levels), and patient education on adherence importance."}, {"topic": "Transplant", "title": "Infectious Complications Post-Transplant", "body": "Infection is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality post-kidney transplant, with risk varying by time since transplant. In the first month, bacterial infections predominate (wound infections, UTI, pneumonia, line infections). Between 1-6 months, opportunistic infections emerge due to peak immunosuppression: CMV (especially D+/R- recipients), BK virus nephropathy, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and fungal infections. After 6 months, community-acquired infections become more common, though late opportunistic infections occur with intensified immunosuppression for rejection. CMV prophylaxis with valganciclovir is standard for 3-6 months in high-risk patients. PJP prophylaxis with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is given for 6-12 months. BK virus screening via plasma viral load or urine decoy cells is performed monthly for 3-6 months, then quarterly through year one. Elevated BK viral load prompts immunosuppression reduction as the primary treatment. All transplant recipients should receive appropriate vaccinations pre-transplant when possible (avoiding live vaccines post-transplant) and practice infection prevention measures including hand hygiene, avoiding sick contacts, and food safety precautions."}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "title": "CRRT Modalities and Solute Removal Mechanisms", "body": "Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT) encompasses several modalities that differ primarily in their solute removal mechanisms. Understanding these mechanisms is essential for selecting the appropriate therapy and optimizing prescriptions.\n\nCONVECTION: This mechanism uses hydrostatic pressure to push fluid across a semipermeable membrane, dragging dissolved solutes along through 'solvent drag.' Convection is particularly effective for middle-molecule clearance. In CVVH (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemofiltration), convection is the primary clearance mechanism, requiring replacement fluid to maintain volume balance.\n\nDIFFUSION: Solutes move down their concentration gradient from blood to dialysate (or vice versa) across the membrane. Diffusion is most efficient for small molecules like urea and creatinine. CVVHD (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodialysis) relies primarily on diffusion, using dialysate flow to create concentration gradients.\n\nCVVHDF (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodiafiltration) combines both mechanisms, using both replacement fluid and dialysate to maximize clearance across all molecular weight ranges. This modality offers flexibility but requires managing both fluid streams.\n\nSLED (Sustained Low-Efficiency Dialysis) represents an intermediate approach\u2014extended intermittent therapy typically run 6-12 hours with lower blood and dialysate flows than conventional hemodialysis. SLED provides better hemodynamic stability than standard HD while requiring less continuous monitoring than CRRT.\n\nSelection depends on patient hemodynamics, available resources, and specific clearance needs. All modalities require careful attention to anticoagulation, vascular access, and dose delivery."}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "title": "Anticoagulation Strategies in CRRT", "body": "Preventing circuit clotting while minimizing bleeding risk is a central challenge in CRRT management. Two primary anticoagulation strategies are used: systemic heparin and regional citrate anticoagulation.\n\nSYSTEMIC HEPARIN: Unfractionated heparin is administered continuously or intermittently to maintain circuit patency. Monitoring typically uses aPTT or anti-Xa levels. Advantages include familiarity, low cost, and easy reversal with protamine. Disadvantages include bleeding risk, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), and variable response in critically ill patients.\n\nREGIONAL CITRATE ANTICOAGULATION: Citrate is infused pre-filter, chelating ionized calcium within the circuit to prevent clotting. Calcium is then replaced post-filter or systemically to maintain normal patient calcium levels. Citrate is metabolized by the liver to bicarbonate.\n\nAdvantages of citrate include: significantly reduced bleeding risk (anticoagulation is circuit-confined), longer circuit life, and no HIT risk. Disadvantages include: complexity of monitoring, risk of citrate accumulation (especially in liver failure or shock), metabolic alkalosis from citrate metabolism, and need for calcium monitoring.\n\nMonitoring citrate anticoagulation requires checking: circuit ionized calcium (target 0.25-0.4 mmol/L), systemic ionized calcium (target 1.0-1.2 mmol/L), total calcium, acid-base status, and the total-to-ionized calcium ratio (elevation >2.5 suggests accumulation).\n\nContraindications to citrate include severe liver failure, severe shock with impaired perfusion, and certain metabolic conditions. In these cases, heparin or saline flushes may be preferred.\n\nKDIGO guidelines suggest citrate as the preferred anticoagulation method when not contraindicated, based on evidence for longer circuit life and reduced bleeding."}, {"topic": "Acute Therapies", "title": "CRRT Dosing, Access, and Clinical Considerations", "body": "Proper CRRT prescription and management requires attention to dose delivery, vascular access, and ongoing monitoring for complications.\n\nDOSE PRESCRIPTION: KDIGO guidelines recommend a delivered dose of 20-25 mL/kg/hr for critically ill patients with AKI. The dose is calculated based on effluent flow rate (total waste fluid). Importantly, prescribed dose often exceeds delivered dose by 15-30% due to treatment downtime from circuit changes, transport, procedures, and alarms. To achieve target delivered dose, consider prescribing 25-30 mL/kg/hr.\n\nVASCULAR ACCESS: The right internal jugular vein is the preferred site for CRRT catheters. It provides direct access to the superior vena cava/right atrium junction, has lower infection rates than femoral access, and avoids subclavian vein stenosis that could compromise future arteriovenous fistula creation. Femoral access is acceptable when other sites are unavailable but carries higher infection risk. Catheters should be dual-lumen, non-cuffed, designed for high flow rates (typically 11-13 French for adults).\n\nELECTROLYTE MANAGEMENT: CRRT significantly affects electrolyte balance. Potassium is effectively cleared, but rapid shifts during initiation can precipitate arrhythmias. Phosphate clearance is often inadequate, leading to hypophosphatemia during prolonged therapy\u2014monitor and supplement as needed. Sodium correction must be gradual (no more than 10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours) to avoid neurologic complications. Bicarbonate-buffered solutions are standard, but acid-base status requires monitoring and dialysate/replacement fluid adjustments.\n\nSEPSIS-INDUCED AKI: In septic patients, CRRT initiation is based on life-threatening complications rather than arbitrary BUN/creatinine thresholds. Indications include: severe metabolic acidosis refractory to medical management, hyperkalemia with ECG changes, volume overload causing respiratory compromise, and uremic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding). Early initiation before severe complications develop may be considered in rapidly deteriorating patients.\n\nCIRCUIT MONITORING: Watch for rising transmembrane pressure (indicates clotting), decreasing venous pressure (access problems), and visual evidence of clotting in the circuit. Prompt intervention preserves circuit life and maintains dose delivery."}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "title": "Anemia Management in Chronic Kidney Disease", "body": "Anemia is one of the most common complications of CKD, affecting over 90% of patients with stage 5 disease. The primary mechanism is erythropoietin (EPO) deficiency, as damaged kidneys cannot produce adequate amounts of this hormone that stimulates red blood cell production in bone marrow.\n\nKey Assessment Points:\n- Monitor hemoglobin regularly (target: 10-11 g/dL with ESA therapy)\n- Check iron studies: ferritin, transferrin saturation (TSAT)\n- Assess for symptoms: fatigue, weakness, shortness of breath, pallor\n- Rule out other causes: blood loss, B12/folate deficiency, hemolysis\n\nTreatment Approach:\n1. Iron supplementation: Maintain ferritin >100 ng/mL (non-dialysis) or >200 ng/mL (dialysis), TSAT >20%\n2. Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs): epoetin alfa, darbepoetin alfa\n3. Avoid hemoglobin >11.5 g/dL due to increased cardiovascular risk\n4. Consider IV iron for dialysis patients or those with poor oral absorption\n\nNursing Considerations:\n- Educate patients about iron-rich foods and ESA administration\n- Monitor blood pressure (ESAs can worsen hypertension)\n- Watch for signs of iron overload or allergic reactions to IV iron\n- Assess vascular access sites in dialysis patients receiving IV medications"}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "title": "CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder (CKD-MBD): Comprehensive Management", "body": "CKD-MBD is a systemic disorder affecting mineral metabolism, bone health, and cardiovascular function. It encompasses abnormalities in calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and vitamin D metabolism that develop as kidney function declines.\n\nPathophysiology:\nAs GFR decreases, phosphate retention occurs, stimulating FGF23 release. The kidney's ability to activate vitamin D diminishes, reducing calcium absorption. Low calcium and high phosphate trigger secondary hyperparathyroidism. Over time, this leads to bone disease and vascular calcification.\n\nLaboratory Monitoring:\n- Calcium (corrected for albumin): target 8.4-9.5 mg/dL\n- Phosphate: target 2.5-4.5 mg/dL (stage 3-5), 3.5-5.5 mg/dL (dialysis)\n- Intact PTH: target 35-70 pg/mL (stage 3), 70-120 pg/mL (stage 4), 150-300 pg/mL (stage 5/dialysis)\n- Alkaline phosphatase: marker of bone turnover\n- 25-hydroxyvitamin D: maintain >30 ng/mL\n\nTreatment Strategies:\n1. Phosphate control: dietary restriction (800-1000 mg/day), phosphate binders (calcium-based, sevelamer, lanthanum)\n2. Vitamin D supplementation: cholecalciferol for deficiency, calcitriol/analogs for secondary HPT\n3. Calcimimetics: cinacalcet for refractory secondary HPT\n4. Parathyroidectomy: for severe, medication-resistant disease\n\nNursing Priorities:\n- Teach proper timing of phosphate binders (with meals)\n- Monitor for hypercalcemia symptoms (nausea, confusion, polyuria)\n- Assess for bone pain, fractures, height loss\n- Emphasize medication adherence to prevent long-term complications"}, {"topic": "CKD Complications", "title": "Cardiovascular Complications and Risk Reduction in CKD", "body": "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in CKD patients, accounting for approximately 50% of mortality. CKD patients are more likely to die from cardiovascular events than progress to ESRD.\n\nUnique Cardiovascular Risks in CKD:\n- Accelerated atherosclerosis from chronic inflammation and endothelial dysfunction\n- Left ventricular hypertrophy from hypertension and anemia\n- Vascular calcification from CKD-MBD\n- Arrhythmias from electrolyte imbalances (especially hyperkalemia)\n- Uremic cardiomyopathy in advanced disease\n- Increased arterial stiffness\n\nRisk Factor Management:\n1. Blood pressure: Target <130/80 mmHg; ACE inhibitors or ARBs preferred for proteinuria\n2. Lipid management: Statins recommended for most CKD patients (except dialysis)\n3. Glycemic control: HbA1c target ~7% for diabetic patients\n4. Anemia management: Maintain hemoglobin 10-11 g/dL\n5. Mineral metabolism: Control phosphate, calcium, PTH to reduce calcification\n6. Lifestyle: Smoking cessation, weight management, physical activity as tolerated\n\nScreening Recommendations:\n- Regular blood pressure monitoring\n- Lipid panel annually (or more frequently if abnormal)\n- ECG for patients with symptoms or high risk\n- Echocardiogram if heart failure symptoms present\n- Assess for peripheral arterial disease\n\nPatient Education:\n- Recognize cardiac symptoms: chest pain, shortness of breath, palpitations, edema\n- Medication adherence for BP, lipids, diabetes\n- Dietary modifications: low sodium, heart-healthy fats, appropriate potassium/phosphate\n- Importance of regular follow-up and laboratory monitoring\n- When to seek emergency care for cardiac symptoms"}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "title": "Nephrotic Syndrome in Children: Assessment and Management", "body": "Nephrotic syndrome is one of the most common glomerular disorders in pediatric nephrology. The hallmark is heavy proteinuria leading to hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia. In children ages 1-10 years, minimal change disease accounts for 80-90% of cases and typically responds well to corticosteroid therapy. Nursing assessment should focus on daily weights, edema monitoring (periorbital, sacral, extremities), blood pressure checks, and urine protein monitoring via dipstick. Key nursing interventions include sodium restriction (1-2 mEq/kg/day), fluid management based on urine output and edema severity, skin care to prevent breakdown from edema, and infection prevention due to immunosuppression from both the disease and steroid treatment. Families should be taught to monitor urine protein at home using dipsticks and recognize signs of relapse (foamy urine, returning edema, weight gain). Complications to monitor include infection (especially peritonitis and cellulitis), thromboembolism (due to urinary loss of antithrombin III), and acute kidney injury from severe hypovolemia. Steroid therapy typically starts at 60 mg/m2/day or 2 mg/kg/day for 6 weeks, followed by alternate-day dosing. Children who relapse frequently or become steroid-dependent may require steroid-sparing agents such as cyclosporine, tacrolimus, mycophenolate mofetil, or rituximab. Regular monitoring of growth, blood pressure, kidney function, and medication side effects is essential for long-term management."}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "title": "Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome: Recognition and Supportive Care", "body": "Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) is the most common cause of acute kidney injury in children. Typical HUS follows a prodrome of bloody diarrhea caused by Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC), most commonly serotype O157:H7. The toxin damages endothelial cells, particularly in the kidney, leading to the classic triad: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes on blood smear, low hemoglobin), thrombocytopenia (platelet consumption), and acute kidney injury. Nursing care is primarily supportive as there is no specific antidote for Shiga toxin. Critical interventions include careful fluid and electrolyte management, monitoring for and treating hyperkalemia, managing hypertension, and providing dialysis when indicated. Avoid antibiotics in suspected STEC infection as they may increase toxin release and worsen outcomes. Antimotility agents should also be avoided. Monitor complete blood counts daily for anemia and thrombocytopenia progression. Transfuse packed red blood cells for symptomatic anemia but use platelet transfusions cautiously as they may worsen thrombotic microangiopathy. Watch for extrarenal complications including CNS involvement (seizures, stroke), pancreatitis, and cardiac dysfunction. Most children recover kidney function within 2-3 weeks, but approximately 5-10% develop chronic kidney disease or end-stage kidney disease. Long-term follow-up should include blood pressure monitoring, urine protein assessment, and kidney function evaluation. Prevention focuses on food safety: cooking ground beef thoroughly, washing produce, avoiding unpasteurized dairy and juices, and proper hand hygiene."}, {"topic": "Pediatric Nephrology", "title": "Peritoneal Dialysis in Pediatric Patients: Nursing Considerations", "body": "Peritoneal dialysis (PD) is the preferred renal replacement therapy for infants and young children awaiting kidney transplantation. The peritoneal membrane serves as the dialysis surface, with dialysate instilled into the peritoneal cavity via a surgically placed catheter. PD offers several advantages in children: continuous gentle ultrafiltration that is better tolerated hemodynamically, preservation of residual kidney function, more liberal fluid and dietary intake, and the ability to perform exchanges at home allowing normal school attendance and activities. Two main modalities exist: continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) with manual exchanges 4-5 times daily, and automated peritoneal dialysis (APD) using a cycler machine overnight. Nursing responsibilities include comprehensive family education on aseptic technique, exchange procedures, catheter care, and recognition of complications. Peritonitis is the most serious complication, presenting with cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, and fever. Exit site infections require prompt treatment to prevent peritonitis. Other complications include catheter malfunction, hernias, gastroesophageal reflux from increased intra-abdominal pressure, and protein losses requiring nutritional supplementation. Adequate nutrition is critical as children on PD need 100-140% of recommended daily calories and adequate protein (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day) to support growth. Monitor growth parameters closely and coordinate with dietitians. Prescription adjustments may be needed based on ultrafiltration goals, solute clearance (Kt/V target >1.8 weekly), and residual kidney function. Regular assessment of catheter exit site, documentation of exchange volumes and times, and monitoring of monthly labs (electrolytes, hemoglobin, albumin, PTH) are essential nursing functions. Successful PD requires strong family engagement and 24/7 access to the dialysis team for troubleshooting."}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "title": "Physiologic Changes in Pregnancy Affecting Kidney Function", "body": "During normal pregnancy, the kidneys undergo significant anatomic and physiologic changes. Renal plasma flow increases by 50-80% and GFR rises by 40-50%, beginning in the first trimester and peaking around 16-26 weeks. This hyperfiltration state results in lower serum creatinine (0.4-0.8 mg/dL) and BUN (8-10 mg/dL) compared to non-pregnant values. The kidneys increase in length by approximately 1 cm due to vascular engorgement and interstitial edema. The ureters dilate, particularly on the right side, due to progesterone-mediated smooth muscle relaxation and mechanical compression from the gravid uterus. This predisposes pregnant women to urinary stasis and increased risk of pyelonephritis. Sodium retention increases by 500-900 mEq during pregnancy, supporting the 40-50% expansion of plasma volume. Understanding these normal adaptations is critical for recognizing pathologic states - a creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL in pregnancy represents significant impairment, whereas it might be normal in a non-pregnant woman. Protein excretion may increase up to 300 mg/24 hours without indicating pathology. Blood pressure normally decreases in the first and second trimesters due to systemic vasodilation, then returns to baseline near term. Failure of BP to decrease or early elevation may indicate underlying kidney disease or risk for preeclampsia."}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "title": "Preeclampsia vs Chronic Hypertension in Kidney Disease Patients", "body": "Distinguishing between chronic hypertension and preeclampsia in pregnant patients with CKD is essential for management and prognosis. Chronic hypertension is defined as BP \u2265140/90 mmHg present before pregnancy or diagnosed before 20 weeks gestation. Preeclampsia is new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria (\u2265300 mg/24 hours) or severe features including thrombocytopenia, renal insufficiency (creatinine >1.1 mg/dL or doubling), elevated liver enzymes, pulmonary edema, or cerebral/visual symptoms. Women with CKD have 25-50% risk of superimposed preeclampsia - preeclampsia developing on top of chronic hypertension or CKD. Warning signs include sudden worsening of hypertension, new or increasing proteinuria, rising creatinine, or development of severe features. Laboratory findings that favor preeclampsia over chronic hypertension alone include elevated uric acid (often disproportionately elevated), thrombocytopenia, elevated LDH, and schistocytes on peripheral smear. Management differs significantly: chronic hypertension is managed with oral antihypertensives to maintain BP <140/90, while preeclampsia with severe features requires magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis, aggressive BP control, and often delivery regardless of gestational age. Low-dose aspirin 81 mg daily starting at 12 weeks reduces preeclampsia risk in high-risk women including those with CKD. Postpartum, women with preeclampsia need monitoring for at least 3 months and counseling about increased long-term cardiovascular and kidney disease risk."}, {"topic": "Pregnancy and Kidney Disease", "title": "Dialysis Management During Pregnancy in ESKD Patients", "body": "Pregnancy in patients with end-stage kidney disease on dialysis is high-risk but achievable with intensive management. The dialysis prescription must be significantly intensified compared to non-pregnant patients. Standard thrice-weekly hemodialysis is inadequate; instead, daily or near-daily dialysis totaling 20+ hours per week is recommended. The goal is to maintain predialysis BUN below 50 mg/dL (ideally <40 mg/dL), which mimics the low BUN of normal pregnancy and is associated with improved fetal outcomes. High BUN levels cause osmotic diuresis in the fetus, leading to polyhydramnios, which increases preterm labor risk. Fluid removal must be gentle to avoid intradialytic hypotension that could compromise uteroplacental perfusion. Ultrafiltration rates should be minimized, and dry weight should be adjusted frequently to accommodate pregnancy weight gain. Hemodialysis access should be well-functioning; fistulas are preferred over catheters to reduce infection risk. Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents are often needed at higher doses due to increased plasma volume and fetal iron demands. Iron supplementation (often IV) is required. Nutritional support is critical - protein intake should be 1.5-1.8 g/kg/day to support fetal growth and replace dialysis losses. Despite intensive dialysis, complications remain common: preterm delivery occurs in 50-80% of cases, intrauterine growth restriction in 30-50%, and polyhydramnios in up to 70%. Multidisciplinary care involving nephrology, maternal-fetal medicine, and neonatology is essential. Peritoneal dialysis can be continued but requires more frequent exchanges and careful monitoring for peritonitis, which carries high risk in pregnancy."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "title": "Understanding Renal Drug Clearance", "body": "Drugs are eliminated from the body through two primary pathways: renal (kidney) and hepatic (liver) clearance. In patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD), understanding which pathway dominates for each medication is critical for safe prescribing.\n\nRenally cleared drugs include: vancomycin, aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin), digoxin, gabapentin, pregabalin, levetiracetam, metformin, most beta-lactam antibiotics, and low molecular weight heparin. These medications require dose reduction or interval extension as GFR declines.\n\nHepatically cleared drugs include: diazepam, lorazepam, fentanyl, most statins (especially atorvastatin), warfarin, and many antidepressants. These typically do not require renal dose adjustment, though caution is still warranted in severe CKD due to altered protein binding and metabolism.\n\nThe Cockcroft-Gault equation or CKD-EPI formula estimates GFR to guide dosing decisions. General thresholds: GFR >60 mL/min (no adjustment), GFR 30-59 mL/min (moderate reduction), GFR 15-29 mL/min (significant reduction), GFR <15 mL/min or dialysis (major adjustment or avoidance).\n\nKey principle: When GFR decreases, the half-life of renally cleared drugs increases proportionally. A drug with a 2-hour half-life in normal renal function may have an 8-12 hour half-life in end-stage renal disease, leading to dangerous accumulation if dosing is not adjusted."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "title": "High-Risk Medications in CKD", "body": "Certain medications pose particular dangers in CKD patients and require special attention:\n\nNSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen, celecoxib): Contraindicated in CKD. They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, causing afferent arteriole vasoconstriction and reduced renal blood flow. This can precipitate acute kidney injury on chronic kidney disease. Even short courses can be harmful. Use acetaminophen for pain instead.\n\nMetformin: First-line diabetes medication but contraindicated when eGFR <30 mL/min. Accumulation causes lactic acidosis with 50% mortality. Reduce dose when eGFR 30-45 mL/min and discontinue below this threshold. Hold during acute illness or before contrast procedures.\n\nNitrofurantoin: Used for UTIs but ineffective and dangerous in CKD. Requires adequate urinary concentrations for bactericidal activity, which cannot be achieved with reduced GFR. Also increases risk of peripheral neuropathy and pulmonary toxicity in CKD. Avoid when CrCl <30 mL/min. It may be used cautiously for uncomplicated cystitis when CrCl is 30-60 mL/min.\n\nBaclofen: Muscle relaxant that is renally eliminated. Can cause severe CNS toxicity including seizures, coma, and respiratory depression in CKD. Avoid entirely in dialysis patients; use extreme dose reduction (5mg daily) if absolutely necessary in moderate CKD.\n\nOpioids: Morphine, codeine, and meperidine produce active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. Fentanyl and methadone are preferred as they are hepatically metabolized without active renally-cleared metabolites.\n\nContrast dye: Iodinated contrast can cause contrast-induced nephropathy. Prevent with IV normal saline hydration (1 mL/kg/hr for 6-12 hours before and after), hold metformin, and use lowest possible contrast volume. Consider alternative imaging in severe CKD."}, {"topic": "Drug Dosing in Renal Failure", "title": "Dialysis and Drug Dosing Considerations", "body": "Hemodialysis significantly affects drug dosing for many medications. Understanding dialyzability determines whether to dose before, after, or independent of dialysis sessions.\n\nFactors affecting dialyzability:\n1. Molecular weight: Small molecules (<500 Daltons) are more easily removed\n2. Protein binding: Drugs >90% protein-bound are not dialyzable (warfarin, phenytoin)\n3. Water solubility: Water-soluble drugs are more dialyzable than lipid-soluble\n4. Volume of distribution: Drugs with large Vd reside in tissues and are less dialyzable\n\nHighly dialyzable drugs (dose AFTER hemodialysis): Vancomycin, aminoglycosides, gabapentin, pregabalin, most beta-lactam antibiotics, lithium, digoxin (partially).\n\nMinimally dialyzable drugs (dose independent of dialysis): Warfarin, heparin, most beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, benzodiazepines, fentanyl.\n\nVancomycin dosing in hemodialysis: Use weight-based loading (20-25 mg/kg IV) followed by maintenance (10-15 mg/kg after each session) guided by AUC/MIC targets (400-600) or trough levels. Fixed 500-1000 mg dosing is outdated and risks under- or overdosing. Check levels before each session.\n\nAminoglycoside dosing: Use extended-interval dosing (e.g., gentamicin 2-3 mg/kg after each dialysis session). Monitor peak and trough levels to prevent ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity. Use extended-interval dosing (5-7 mg/kg once daily) with random level monitoring at 6-14 hours (Hartford nomogram). When a trough is checked, target <1 mcg/mL to minimize nephrotoxicity.\n\nPeritoneal dialysis considerations: Generally removes drugs less efficiently than hemodialysis due to slower clearance. Many medications can be dosed normally, but some (like vancomycin) may be given intraperitoneally for peritonitis treatment.\n\nGeneral rule: When uncertain about dialyzability, consult drug references or time doses after dialysis sessions for renally-cleared medications with narrow therapeutic indices."}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "title": "Understanding Metabolic Acidosis: Types and Clinical Approach", "body": "Metabolic acidosis is characterized by low arterial pH (<7.35) and reduced serum bicarbonate (<22 mEq/L). The first step in evaluation is calculating the anion gap: AG = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-). A normal anion gap is 8-12 mEq/L, though this varies by laboratory. High anion gap metabolic acidosis occurs when unmeasured anions accumulate. Common causes include diabetic ketoacidosis (ketone bodies), lactic acidosis (lactate from tissue hypoxia), uremia (retained organic acids), and toxic ingestions (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates). Use the mnemonic MUDPILES to remember these causes. Normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis results from bicarbonate loss or impaired renal acid excretion. Causes include diarrhea (GI bicarbonate loss), renal tubular acidosis (defective renal acidification), and early CKD. The mnemonic HARDASS helps recall these etiologies. In nephrology nursing, recognizing the type of metabolic acidosis guides treatment. DKA requires insulin and fluid resuscitation to stop ketogenesis. Lactic acidosis needs treatment of the underlying cause (sepsis, hypoperfusion). RTA types require different approaches: Type 1 needs alkali therapy, Type 2 needs high-dose bicarbonate, and Type 4 may need potassium binders and fludrocortisone. Always assess compensation using Winter's formula for metabolic acidosis: expected PaCO2 = 1.5 \u00d7 HCO3- + 8 (\u00b12). If measured PaCO2 exceeds expected, a concurrent respiratory acidosis exists. If lower, concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present."}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "title": "Respiratory Acid-Base Disorders and Renal Compensation", "body": "Respiratory acid-base disorders result from changes in alveolar ventilation affecting PaCO2. Respiratory acidosis (PaCO2 >45 mmHg, pH <7.35) occurs from hypoventilation due to COPD exacerbation, opioid overdose, neuromuscular disease, or chest wall abnormalities. The kidneys compensate by increasing bicarbonate reabsorption, but this takes 3-5 days for full effect. In acute respiratory acidosis, bicarbonate rises 1 mEq/L per 10 mmHg PaCO2 increase. In chronic respiratory acidosis, bicarbonate rises 3-4 mEq/L per 10 mmHg increase. Respiratory alkalosis (PaCO2 <35 mmHg, pH >7.45) results from hyperventilation due to anxiety, pain, hypoxemia, pulmonary embolism, or mechanical ventilation. Renal compensation decreases bicarbonate reabsorption. In acute respiratory alkalosis, bicarbonate falls 2 mEq/L per 10 mmHg PaCO2 decrease. In chronic respiratory alkalosis, bicarbonate falls 4-5 mEq/L per 10 mmHg decrease. For nephrology nurses, understanding compensation is critical when evaluating CKD or dialysis patients with concurrent pulmonary disease. A patient with COPD may have chronic respiratory acidosis with appropriate renal compensation (elevated bicarbonate). If bicarbonate is lower than expected, a concurrent metabolic acidosis (e.g., from uremia or lactic acidosis) should be suspected. Conversely, if bicarbonate is higher than expected, metabolic alkalosis (e.g., from diuretics or vomiting) may coexist. Never interpret blood gases in isolation\u2014always correlate with clinical context, electrolytes, and renal function. Treatment focuses on the underlying cause: bronchodilators and steroids for COPD, reversing sedation for opioid overdose, or adjusting ventilator settings for iatrogenic causes."}, {"topic": "Acid-Base Disorders", "title": "Renal Tubular Acidosis and Bicarbonate Management in CKD", "body": "Renal tubular acidosis (RTA) comprises disorders of renal acidification with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. Type 1 (distal) RTA involves defective hydrogen ion secretion in the distal nephron. Key features: urine pH >5.5 despite systemic acidosis, hypokalemia, nephrolithiasis (from hypercalciuria and low citrate), and nephrocalcinosis. Causes include autoimmune diseases (Sj\u00f6gren's, lupus), amphotericin B toxicity, and hereditary forms. Treatment requires alkali therapy (sodium bicarbonate or potassium citrate) at 1-3 mEq/kg/day. Type 2 (proximal) RTA involves impaired bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. Key features: urine pH variable (can acidify when bicarbonate is depleted), hypokalemia, and association with Fanconi syndrome (glycosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria). Treatment requires bicarbonate 3-10 mEq/kg/day in divided doses (start 3-5, titrate up to 10 as needed) because administered bicarbonate is wasted in urine until the reabsorption threshold is exceeded. Type 4 RTA involves aldosterone deficiency or resistance. Key features: hyperkalemia, urine pH <5.5 (can acidify), and impaired ammonium excretion. Causes include diabetic nephropathy, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs, and adrenal insufficiency. Treatment includes addressing hyperkalemia (diet, potassium binders), fludrocortisone if deficient, and modest alkali if needed. In CKD, metabolic acidosis develops as GFR declines below 60 mL/min/1.73m\u00b2. The kidneys lose capacity to excrete acid and regenerate bicarbonate. KDIGO guidelines recommend oral bicarbonate supplementation when serum bicarbonate is persistently <22 mEq/L, targeting 22-26 mEq/L. Benefits include slowing CKD progression, reducing muscle wasting, and improving bone health. Sodium bicarbonate tablets (650 mg = 7.7 mEq) are commonly used. Monitor for sodium overload, hypertension, and edema. In dialysis patients, bicarbonate concentration in dialysate can be adjusted to correct acidosis between sessions."}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "title": "Hyperkalemia: Recognition and Emergency Management", "body": "Hyperkalemia is a life-threatening electrolyte emergency requiring immediate recognition and intervention. Normal potassium ranges from 3.5-5.0 mEq/L, with levels above 6.5 mEq/L or any level with ECG changes constituting an emergency. ECG changes progress predictably: peaked T waves appear first, followed by PR interval prolongation, QRS widening, sine wave pattern, and ultimately ventricular fibrillation or asystole. Emergency treatment follows a three-pronged approach. First, stabilize cardiac membranes with IV calcium gluconate (1 gram over 2-3 minutes), which works within minutes but does not lower potassium levels. Second, shift potassium intracellularly using insulin with dextrose (10 units regular insulin IV with 25g dextrose), sodium bicarbonate in acidotic patients, or nebulized albuterol. These measures lower potassium by 0.5-1.5 mEq/L within 15-30 minutes. Third, remove potassium from the body using loop diuretics if renal function permits, potassium binders like sodium polystyrene sulfonate or newer agents, or emergent hemodialysis for severe cases. Nurses must continuously monitor cardiac rhythm, verify IV access, and prepare for rapid sequence intervention. Prevention includes medication review (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, potassium-sparing diuretics), dietary counseling, and ensuring adherence to dialysis schedules."}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "title": "Hyponatremia and Hypernatremia: Safe Correction Principles", "body": "Sodium disorders require careful assessment and measured correction to prevent neurologic complications. Hyponatremia (sodium <135 mEq/L) is classified by volume status: hypovolemic (fluid loss), euvolemic (SIADH), or hypervolemic (heart failure, cirrhosis). SIADH presents with hyponatremia, low serum osmolality, and inappropriately concentrated urine despite euvolemia. The critical principle in hyponatremia management is avoiding overly rapid correction. Chronic hyponatremia corrected too quickly can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis), resulting in irreversible neurologic damage including paralysis, dysarthria, and locked-in syndrome. Sodium should not increase more than 8-10 mEq/L in 24 hours or 18 mEq/L in 48 hours. Severe symptomatic hyponatremia with seizures requires cautious hypertonic saline (3%) administration. Hypernatremia (sodium >145 mEq/L) typically results from water loss exceeding sodium loss, as seen in diabetes insipidus or inadequate water intake. Treatment involves gradual free water replacement, calculating the water deficit and correcting over 48-72 hours. Rapid correction of hypernatremia can cause cerebral edema. Nurses must monitor neurologic status, strict intake/output, daily weights, and serial sodium levels. Both conditions require identifying and treating the underlying cause while maintaining careful fluid balance."}, {"topic": "Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies", "title": "Calcium, Magnesium, and Phosphate Emergencies", "body": "Calcium, magnesium, and phosphate disorders frequently occur together and require integrated management. Hypocalcemia (total calcium <8.5 mg/dL or ionized <4.5 mg/dL) presents with neuromuscular irritability including perioral paresthesias, muscle cramps, tetany, Chvostek sign (facial muscle contraction when tapping facial nerve), and Trousseau sign (carpal spasm with BP cuff inflation). ECG shows prolonged QT interval increasing arrhythmia risk. Emergency treatment involves IV calcium gluconate or chloride for severe symptoms. Hypercalcemia (calcium >10.5 mg/dL, crisis >14 mg/dL) causes shortened QT, confusion, polyuria, constipation, and renal stones. Treatment includes aggressive IV isotonic saline for volume expansion, calcitonin for rapid effect, IV bisphosphonates for sustained reduction, and dialysis for severe cases. Hypermagnesemia (>2.6 mg/dL) typically occurs in renal failure patients receiving magnesium-containing products. Early signs include loss of deep tendon reflexes, progressing to hypotension, bradycardia, respiratory depression, and cardiac arrest. Treatment involves IV calcium gluconate to antagonize effects and dialysis. Hyperphosphatemia commonly occurs in CKD, causing hypocalcemia through calcium-phosphate binding and triggering secondary hyperparathyroidism. Management includes phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer) taken with meals and dietary phosphate restriction. Nurses must monitor ionized calcium levels, assess neuromuscular symptoms, and coordinate timing of medications with meals for optimal phosphate binding."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "title": "Calcineurin Inhibitors: Tacrolimus and Cyclosporine", "body": "Calcineurin inhibitors (CNIs) form the backbone of most transplant immunosuppression regimens. Both tacrolimus and cyclosporine work by inhibiting calcineurin, a phosphatase enzyme critical for T-cell activation. When T-cells encounter antigens, calcineurin dephosphorylates NFAT (nuclear factor of activated T-cells), allowing it to enter the nucleus and transcribe IL-2 and other cytokines. CNIs block this pathway, preventing T-cell proliferation and activation.\n\nTACROLIMUS: Binds to FKBP-12 (FK506-binding protein), forming a complex that inhibits calcineurin. It is 10-100 times more potent than cyclosporine. Key monitoring parameters include trough levels (typically 8-12 ng/mL early post-transplant, 5-10 ng/mL long-term), serum creatinine, blood glucose, and electrolytes (especially potassium and magnesium). Major adverse effects include nephrotoxicity (dose-dependent, reversible with dose reduction), neurotoxicity (tremor, headache, seizures in severe cases), hyperglycemia and new-onset diabetes after transplant (NODAT occurs in 10-20% of patients), hyperkalemia, and hypomagnesemia. Tacrolimus should be taken consistently with or without food, and grapefruit must be avoided.\n\nCYCLOSPORINE: Binds to cyclophilin, forming a complex that inhibits calcineurin. Available in immediate-release and modified formulations (Neoral, Gengraf). Monitoring includes trough levels (variable by institution), creatinine, blood pressure, and lipids. Distinctive side effects include gingival hyperplasia (up to 30% of patients), hirsutism (excessive hair growth), hypertension, and hyperlipidemia. Like tacrolimus, it causes nephrotoxicity and requires avoidance of grapefruit.\n\nDRUG INTERACTIONS: Both CNIs are metabolized by CYP3A4 and are P-glycoprotein substrates. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, diltiazem, verapamil, grapefruit) significantly increase levels and toxicity risk. CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, St. John's wort) decrease levels and increase rejection risk. Always consult pharmacy before adding new medications.\n\nNURSING CONSIDERATIONS: Administer at consistent times (usually every 12 hours), draw trough levels 12 hours after the last dose and immediately before the next dose, monitor for signs of nephrotoxicity (rising creatinine, decreased urine output), assess for neurotoxicity symptoms, check blood glucose regularly, and educate patients about drug interactions and the importance of adherence."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "title": "Antimetabolites and mTOR Inhibitors", "body": "ANTIMETABOLITES work by interfering with purine synthesis, affecting rapidly dividing cells including activated lymphocytes.\n\nMYCOPHENOLATE MOFETIL (MMF): Converted to mycophenolic acid, which inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase (IMPDH), blocking de novo purine synthesis. Lymphocytes depend on this pathway, making them selectively targeted. Standard dosing is 500-1500 mg twice daily. Common side effects include GI symptoms (diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain - often improved by taking with food or dividing doses), bone marrow suppression (leukopenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia), and increased infection risk. Monitoring includes complete blood count (CBC) weekly initially, then monthly; assess for signs of infection and bleeding. MMF is teratogenic and requires effective contraception. It may be available as enteric-coated mycophenolate sodium (Myfortic), which may reduce GI side effects.\n\nAZATHIOPRINE: An older antimetabolite, prodrug converted to 6-mercaptopurine, which interferes with DNA and RNA synthesis. Less commonly used now due to MMF's superior efficacy and tolerability, but still used in some protocols or when MMF is not tolerated. Dosing is typically 1-2 mg/kg/day. Major adverse effect is bone marrow suppression, which can be severe. Patients with low thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity are at higher risk for toxicity. Monitoring includes CBC and liver function tests. Drug interactions include allopurinol (increases azathioprine toxicity - avoid combination or reduce azathioprine dose by 75%).\n\nmTOR INHIBITORS: Sirolimus (rapamycin) and everolimus block the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR), a kinase involved in cell cycle progression.\n\nSIROLIMUS: Used as maintenance immunosuppression, particularly in patients with CNI nephrotoxicity or certain malignancies (Kaposi sarcoma, renal cell carcinoma). Does not cause nephrotoxicity, making it useful for CNI-sparing or CNI-free regimens. However, it has significant side effects including hyperlipidemia (elevated cholesterol and triglycerides in 30-50% of patients), thrombocytopenia and anemia, impaired wound healing (avoid in early post-operative period), proteinuria, and increased risk of lymphocele. Monitoring includes lipid panel, CBC, sirolimus trough levels (target 4-12 ng/mL depending on protocol), and urinalysis for protein. Like CNIs, it is metabolized by CYP3A4 and has similar drug interactions.\n\nNURSING CONSIDERATIONS: For MMF, administer on empty stomach if tolerated (or with food to reduce GI upset), monitor CBC regularly, educate about infection prevention and contraception requirements. For sirolimus, monitor lipids and CBC, assess wound healing, avoid in immediate post-operative period, and counsel about drug interactions. Both require adherence education and regular laboratory monitoring."}, {"topic": "Transplant Pharmacology", "title": "Induction Agents and Corticosteroids", "body": "INDUCTION THERAPY provides intense immunosuppression at the time of transplant to prevent early acute rejection and may allow delayed introduction or lower doses of maintenance immunosuppressants.\n\nBASILIXIMAB (Simulect): A chimeric (human-mouse) monoclonal antibody targeting the alpha chain of the IL-2 receptor (CD25) on activated T-cells. By blocking IL-2 binding, it prevents T-cell proliferation. Given as IV infusion: 20 mg on day 0 (before transplant) and day 4. Does not cause cytokine release syndrome, so premedication is not typically required. Well-tolerated with minimal side effects. Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity. Provides coverage for the first 4-6 weeks post-transplant.\n\nANTITHYMOCYTE GLOBULIN (ATG): Polyclonal antibodies derived from rabbit or horse immunized with human T-cells. Causes profound T-cell depletion through complement-mediated lysis and apoptosis. Used for induction in high-risk patients (high PRA, retransplant, delayed graft function) and for treatment of acute rejection. Dosing varies (typically 1-1.5 mg/kg/day for 3-5 days). Requires premedication with acetaminophen, diphenhydramine, and often corticosteroids to prevent infusion reactions. Side effects include cytokine release syndrome (fever, chills, hypotension during infusion), leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and increased infection risk (particularly CMV, fungal). Requires monitoring of CBC with differential, CD3 counts to assess T-cell depletion, and infection surveillance.\n\nCORTICOSTEROIDS: Prednisone and methylprednisolone have been cornerstone immunosuppressants since the early days of transplantation. They work through multiple mechanisms: inhibiting cytokine production, reducing lymphocyte proliferation, and decreasing inflammation.\n\nUSES: High-dose IV methylprednisolone for acute rejection treatment (pulse therapy: 500-1000 mg daily for 3 days), oral prednisone for maintenance (typically started at 20-30 mg/day and tapered to 5-10 mg/day long-term, or discontinued in steroid-free protocols).\n\nADVERSE EFFECTS (dose and duration dependent): Metabolic - hyperglycemia, weight gain, fluid retention, dyslipidemia; Musculoskeletal - osteoporosis, osteonecrosis (especially femoral head), myopathy; Dermatologic - thin skin, easy bruising, poor wound healing, acne; Ophthalmic - cataracts, glaucoma; Endocrine - adrenal suppression (requires stress-dose steroids for surgery/illness if on chronic therapy), Cushingoid appearance; GI - peptic ulcer disease, gastritis; Neuropsychiatric - mood changes, insomnia, psychosis (rare); Immunologic - increased infection risk.\n\nNURSING CONSIDERATIONS: Monitor blood glucose (especially in diabetics or those on tacrolimus), assess bone density (DEXA scans), ensure calcium and vitamin D supplementation, consider bisphosphonate prophylaxis for long-term therapy, educate about never stopping abruptly (adrenal crisis risk), provide stress-dose education for illness/surgery, monitor for signs of infection, and assess mood and sleep patterns. Tapering should be gradual to allow adrenal recovery."}];
if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) module.exports = {ORIG_QUESTIONS, ORIG_FLASHCARDS, ORIG_LESSONS};
